window.SEARCH_INDEX=[{"t":"Math Olympiad: School/Regional → National → IMO","u":"math-olympiad/index.html","s":"Math Olympiad","h":[{"n":"⚠️ Read first: eligibility and timing","id":"read-first-eligibility-and-timing"},{"n":"The map","id":"the-map"},{"n":"Interactive tools → interactive/","id":"interactive-tools-interactive"},{"n":"Weekly routine (~10–12 h)","id":"weekly-routine-1012-h"}],"x":"Math Olympiad: School/Regional → National → IMO Roadmap ⏱ 2 min read View Markdown source Starting point: you've passed school/regional rounds and are strong at math. Target: national team selection, then IMO-level problem solving. ⚠️ Read first: eligibility and timing The IMO generally requires contestants to be under 20 on the day of the contest and not enrolled at a university . As a high-school senior, this cycle is probably your last chance . Verify the rules with your country's olympiad committee. National selection often starts months before the IMO (July). Find your country's dates now and plan backwards from them. The IMO paper: 2 days × 3 problems × 4.5 hours , 7 points each. Problems cover A lgebra, C ombinatorics, G eometry, and N umber theory, ordered roughly easy → hard (P1/P4 easiest, P3/P6 hardest). The map # Module Time You can… 01 Problem solving & proof writing 2–3 wk write rigorous proofs and use the core strategies 02 Algebra 4–6 wk inequalities, polynomials, functional equations 03 Number theory 4–6 wk modular arithmetic, orders, LTE, Diophantine equations 04 Combinatorics 5–7 wk counting, invariants, extremal arguments, graphs, games 05 Geometry 5–7 wk angle chasing → power of a point → projective/inversion → bash methods 06 Contest practice & solution writing ongoing sit full mocks and write solutions that earn full marks Modules 02–05 run in parallel : rotate topics weekly instead of finishing one area before the next. Run module 06 constantly from week 3 on. Interactive tools → interactive/ Triangle centers explorer: drag a triangle and see the circumcenter, incenter, centroid, orthocenter, Euler line, and nine-point circle live. Modular arithmetic lab: multiplication tables mod n, orders, primitive roots, Fermat/Euler check. Flashcards: spaced repetition for theorems, lemmas, and techniques. Weekly routine (~10–12 h) Day Do 3 days Topic study: read one handout section, then solve 4–6 problems from it 1 day Hard problem: one problem above your level, 1.5–2 h, no hints for the first hour 1 day Mock: 3 problems in 4.5 h (from week 3), then grade yourself strictly 1 day Review: write up solutions cleanly, read official solutions, update progress.md The golden rule: stay stuck for at least 30–60 min before looking at a hint. When you read a solution, write down the key idea in one line . That line is what you'll remember and reuse. Support files: cheatsheet.md · resources.md · progress.md"},{"t":"01 · Problem Solving & Proof Writing","u":"math-olympiad/01-problem-solving-proofs.html","s":"Math Olympiad","h":[{"n":"Skip test","id":"skip-test"},{"n":"Core ideas","id":"core-ideas"},{"n":"Learn from","id":"learn-from"},{"n":"Build","id":"build"},{"n":"Traps","id":"traps"}],"x":"01 · Problem Solving & Proof Writing Module 01 ⏱ 2 min read View Markdown source Goal: have a reliable process for attacking unfamiliar problems, and write proofs a grader can't fault. · Time: 2–3 weeks Skip test Prove by strong induction that every integer ≥ 2 is a product of primes. Prove sqrt 2 is irrational by contradiction, and sqrt2+sqrt3 too. Use pigeonhole: among any 5 points in a unit square, two are within tfracsqrt22. Find an invariant: can a chessboard with two opposite corners removed be tiled by dominoes? Use the extremal principle: show a finite set of points, not all collinear, has a line through exactly 2 of them (Sylvester–Gallai). Core ideas Process (Pólya): understand → try small cases → find a pattern/conjecture → prove → check. Small cases first. Compute n=1,2,3,4,5. Most olympiad insights come from data. Work backwards from what's needed. Draw a big, accurate figure (geometry and combinatorics). Proof toolbox: Direct , contrapositive , contradiction . Induction: weak, strong, and \"strengthen the hypothesis\" (proving something stronger is often easier ). Pigeonhole: n+1 objects in n boxes; more generally, some box has gelceil N/krceil. Extremal principle: look at the largest/smallest/first object, then derive a contradiction or a structure. Invariants & monovariants: a quantity that never changes, or changes in one direction only. This answers \"can we reach state X?\" and \"does the process terminate?\" Coloring / parity: a 2-coloring or a mod-2/mod-3 argument rules things out. Infinite descent: a minimal counterexample produces a smaller one, a contradiction. Writing: state what you prove. One claim per paragraph. Define every variable. Don't skip \"obvious\" steps the grader can't verify. Partial credit comes from clearly proven lemmas. Learn from ⭐ The Art and Craft of Problem Solving , Paul Zeitz 💲: ch. 1–3 (strategies, tactics). The best first book. Problem-Solving Strategies , Arthur Engel 💲: ch. 1–4 (invariants, coloring, extremal, pigeonhole). A huge problem bank. Evan Chen: Notes on proof writing : see his handouts page (\"Proof writing\" and the \"English\" handout). How to Write Proofs, Larry Cusick (short guide) . Build Write 10 complete proofs of Engel ch. 1–4 problems. Have someone (or your future self, a week later) grade them strictly from 0–7. Traps \"Proof by example\": checking cases is not a proof. Skipping writing practice. At IMO level, ~1/3 of lost points are from incomplete write-ups, not wrong ideas."},{"t":"02 · Algebra","u":"math-olympiad/02-algebra.html","s":"Math Olympiad","h":[{"n":"Skip test","id":"skip-test"},{"n":"Core ideas","id":"core-ideas"},{"n":"Inequalities","id":"inequalities"},{"n":"Polynomials","id":"polynomials"},{"n":"Functional equations","id":"functional-equations"},{"n":"Sequences & sums","id":"sequences-sums"},{"n":"Learn from","id":"learn-from"},{"n":"Build","id":"build"},{"n":"Traps","id":"traps"}],"x":"02 · Algebra Module 02 ⏱ 2 min read View Markdown source Goal: inequalities, polynomials, functional equations, and sequences at national-to-IMO level. · Time: 4–6 weeks (in rotation) Skip test Prove AM-GM for n variables, and use it to minimize x + frac4x^2 for x>0. Prove fracab+c+fracbc+a+fracca+bgefrac32 (Nesbitt) in two different ways. Find all f:mathbb Rtomathbb R with f(x+y)=f(x)+f(y) and f monotonic. Use Vieta: if x^3-6x^2+11x-6 has roots r,s,t, find r^2+s^2+t^2 without solving. Explain when equality holds in Cauchy–Schwarz. Core ideas Inequalities AM-GM: fracx_1+dots+x_nngesqrt[n]x_1cdots x_n, with equality iff all are equal. Weighted version too. Power mean chain: QM ≥ AM ≥ GM ≥ HM. Cauchy–Schwarz: left(sum a_i^2right)left(sum b_i^2right)geleft(sum a_ib_iright)^2. Titu/Engel form: sumfraca_i^2b_igefrac(sum a_i)^2sum b_i. Hölder , rearrangement , Chebyshev , Schur (t>0: sum a^t(a-b)(a-c)ge0), Muirhead (for symmetric polynomial inequalities). Jensen: for convex f, f\\!left(fracsum x_inright)lefracsum f(x_i)n. Tangent line trick for sum f(x_i) with sum x_i fixed. Techniques: homogenize, normalize (a+b+c=1 or abc=1), substitution (a=fracxy, Ravi a=y+z for triangle sides), SOS (sum of squares), and uvw / mixing variables. Always find the equality case first. It tells you which inequality can work. Polynomials Factor theorem , Vieta's formulas , Rational root theorem . Integer-coefficient trick: a-b mid P(a)-P(b). Roots of unity filter: frac1nsum_k=0^n-1P(omega^k) extracts the coefficients with index ≡ 0 (mod n). Lagrange interpolation , finite differences (a degree-d polynomial has a constant d-th difference). Irreducibility: Eisenstein's criterion; the mod-p reduction trick. Functional equations Standard plays: substitute x=y=0, y=0, y=x, y=-x, x=1. Find f(0) and f(1). Prove injective / surjective , find fixed points , use symmetry (swap x,y). Cauchy's equation f(x+y)=f(x)+f(y): over mathbb Q gives f(x)=cx. Over mathbb R it needs extra conditions (monotonic, bounded on an interval, or continuous). Guess the answer (usually f(x)=x, cx, x^2, constant), verify it , then prove it's the only one. Over mathbb N / mathbb Z: induction, bounding, and number theory. Sequences & sums Linear recurrences: characteristic equation. Telescoping. Bounding by monotonicity/convergence. Learn from ⭐ Evan Chen: \"A Brief Introduction to Olympiad Inequalities\" , plus his functional equations material on the handouts page . ⭐ Yufei Zhao's olympiad handouts : inequalities, polynomials, and more. Secrets in Inequalities , Pham Kim Hung 💲 · Functional Equations , Venkatachala 💲 · Problems from the Book , Andreescu & Dospinescu 💲 (advanced). AoPS: Intermediate Algebra 💲 if you need a gentler ramp. Build Solve the A1–A4 problems from the last 5 years of IMO Shortlists . Make a one-line \"key idea\" note for each. Traps Applying AM-GM where the equality case can't happen. In functional equations, forgetting to check the solution in the original equation, and forgetting the pathological solutions of Cauchy."},{"t":"03 · Number Theory","u":"math-olympiad/03-number-theory.html","s":"Math Olympiad","h":[{"n":"Skip test","id":"skip-test"},{"n":"Core ideas","id":"core-ideas"},{"n":"Learn from","id":"learn-from"},{"n":"Build","id":"build"},{"n":"Traps","id":"traps"}],"x":"03 · Number Theory Module 03 ⏱ 2 min read View Markdown source Goal: divisibility, modular arithmetic, orders, and Diophantine equations at IMO level. · Time: 4–6 weeks (in rotation) 👉 Use the Modular arithmetic lab to see orders and primitive roots. Skip test Prove there are infinitely many primes ≡ 3 (mod 4). Use Fermat's little theorem to find 2^100bmod 13. Show that if ord_p(a)=d then dmid p-1. Use LTE to find v_2(3^2^n-1). Solve x^2-2y^2=1 in positive integers (Pell) and describe all solutions. Find all n with n mid 2^n - 1. Core ideas Divisibility & gcd: Euclid's algorithm, Bézout (ax+by=gcd(a,b)), gcd(a^m-1,a^n-1)=a^gcd(m,n)-1. Fundamental theorem of arithmetic. p-adic valuation v_p(n): think of numbers as exponent vectors. Modular arithmetic: inverses exist iff gcd(a,n)=1. CRT: you can solve congruences modulo coprime moduli independently. Fermat: a^p-1equiv1pmod p. Euler: a^varphi(n)equiv1pmod n. Wilson: (p-1)!equiv-1pmod p. Order: ord_n(a) = the smallest d with a^dequiv1. Key lemma: a^kequiv1 iff ordmid k. This is the most-used idea in olympiad NT. Primitive roots exist mod 1, 2, 4, p^k, 2p^k. So (mathbb Z/p)^times is cyclic. Quadratic residues: Legendre symbol, Euler's criterion left(frac apright)equiv a^fracp-12, -1 is a QR mod p iff pequiv1pmod4. Quadratic reciprocity. Lifting The Exponent (LTE): for odd prime pmid a-b, pnmid a,b: v_p(a^n-b^n)=v_p(a-b)+v_p(n). (Special rules for p=2.) Legendre's formula: v_p(n!)=sum_kge1lfloor n/p^krfloor. Zsigmondy's theorem (use with care; graders may want a proof): a^n-b^n has a primitive prime divisor, apart from listed exceptions. Diophantine equations: factor, work mod m to rule out cases, bound sizes (inequalities!), use infinite descent, Pell equations, Vieta jumping. Floor functions: write x = lfloor xrfloor + \\x\\. Hermite's identity. Learn from ⭐ Modern Olympiad Number Theory, Aditya Khurmi : a free PDF shared by the author on AoPS (search the title). Thorough and olympiad-focused. Evan Chen's NT handouts (orders) · Yufei Zhao: NT handouts . Number Theory: Structures, Examples, and Problems , Andreescu & Andrica 💲 · 104 Number Theory Problems , Andreescu et al. 💲. AoPS: Introduction to Number Theory 💲 if you need the basics. Build Use the lab to find the order of every a mod 13, then prove the pattern you see (the orders divide 12; there are exactly varphi(d) elements of order d). Solve N1–N4 from recent IMO Shortlists. Traps Dividing in congruences without checking that the inverse exists. Forgetting the p=2 special case of LTE and quadratic residues."},{"t":"04 · Combinatorics","u":"math-olympiad/04-combinatorics.html","s":"Math Olympiad","h":[{"n":"Skip test","id":"skip-test"},{"n":"Core ideas","id":"core-ideas"},{"n":"Learn from","id":"learn-from"},{"n":"Build","id":"build"},{"n":"Traps","id":"traps"}],"x":"04 · Combinatorics Module 04 ⏱ 2 min read View Markdown source Goal: the hardest and least formulaic IMO area. Build a toolkit, then lots of experience. · Time: 5–7 weeks (in rotation) Skip test Count lattice paths from (0,0) to (m,n) and give a bijective proof of binomnk=binomnn-k. Use stars and bars: the number of solutions of x_1+dots+x_k=n with x_ige0. Inclusion–exclusion: count derangements D_n. Double counting: prove sum_v deg v = 2|E|, and sum_k kbinom nk = n2^n-1. Game theory: find the winning positions in Nim. Prove R(3,3)=6. Core ideas Counting: product/sum rule, permutations, binom nk, stars and bars, inclusion–exclusion , bijections (count something else that is easier). Double counting: count the same set two ways, e.g. pairs (person, club) by person and by club. Recursion: set up a_n in terms of smaller cases (tilings, paths). Catalan numbers C_n=frac1n+1binom2nn. Generating functions: sum a_nx^n turns counting into algebra. Pigeonhole, extremal, invariants, monovariants, coloring (from module 01) are the combinatorics weapons. Graph theory: degrees, trees (|E|=|V|-1), bipartite ⟺ no odd cycle, Euler paths, Hall's marriage theorem, Turán's theorem, Ramsey. Algorithms & processes: \"does the process end?\" → find a monovariant (a bounded, strictly changing quantity). Games: work backwards from end positions. Strategy stealing . Symmetry strategies. Nim and the Sprague–Grundy idea. Combinatorial geometry: convex hulls, the extremal point, coloring the plane. Probabilistic method: if a random object has expected bad count < 1, then a good object exists. Constructions: many problems ask for \"the maximum\" → you must give a construction and a bound . Two separate proofs. Learn from ⭐ Problem Solving Methods in Combinatorics, Pablo Soberón 💲: short, by technique, IMO-focused. Yufei Zhao's combinatorics handouts (e.g. \"Combinatorics: a few techniques\", double counting). 102 Combinatorial Problems , Andreescu & Feng 💲 · Engel ch. on combinatorics · Evan Chen's handouts . AoPS: Intro / Intermediate Counting & Probability 💲 for the basics. Build Solve C1–C4 from the last 5–8 IMO Shortlists. Combinatorics gets better mostly with volume , so aim for 60+ problems. Traps Overcounting from treating identical objects as distinct (or the reverse). For max/min problems: giving only the construction or only the bound."},{"t":"05 · Geometry","u":"math-olympiad/05-geometry.html","s":"Math Olympiad","h":[{"n":"Skip test","id":"skip-test"},{"n":"Core ideas (in learning order)","id":"core-ideas-in-learning-order"},{"n":"Learn from","id":"learn-from"},{"n":"Build","id":"build"},{"n":"Traps","id":"traps"}],"x":"05 · Geometry Module 05 ⏱ 2 min read View Markdown source Goal: from angle chasing to projective tools, plus reliable computational backups. · Time: 5–7 weeks (in rotation) 👉 Use the Triangle centers explorer while studying this module. Skip test Prove the four points of a cyclic quadrilateral satisfy \"opposite angles sum to 180°\", and the converse. State and use the power of a point and the radical axis theorem. Prove the orthocenter's reflection over a side lies on the circumcircle. Prove the incenter–excenter lemma : I, B, C, I_A all lie on a circle centered at the arc midpoint M_A. Explain the Euler line and the nine-point circle (center, radius R/2). Set up a problem in complex numbers with the unit circle as the circumcircle. Core ideas (in learning order) Angle chasing: inscribed angle theorem, cyclic quadrilaterals, tangent–chord angle. Directed angles mod 180° avoid configuration issues. Triangle centers & lemmas: centroid G, circumcenter O, incenter I, orthocenter H. Euler line O,G,H with OG:GH=1:2. Nine-point circle. Incenter–excenter lemma. Reflections of H. Length tools: law of sines/cosines, Ceva and Menelaus (with the trig version), Stewart, Ptolemy. Circles: power of a point , radical axis / radical center , coaxal circles, tangent circles. Transformations: homothety (e.g. the incircle–excircle homothety), spiral similarity (Miquel point), reflections, rotations. Projective (IMO level): cross-ratio, harmonic bundles, poles/polars, Pascal and Brianchon, Desargues' involution. Inversion: turns circles through the center into lines. Great for tangency problems. Computational \"bash\" backups: Cartesian coordinates, complex numbers (unit circle), barycentric coordinates . Slow but reliable. Learn one well as a safety net. Learn from ⭐ Euclidean Geometry in Mathematical Olympiads (EGMO), Evan Chen 💲. The olympiad geometry book. Part I (ch. 1–4) is essential, Part II for IMO-level. Some chapters are free on his website . Yufei Zhao: Lemmas in Euclidean geometry, Cyclic quadrilaterals handouts . Lemmas in Olympiad Geometry , Andreescu, Korsky & Pohoata 💲 · Geometry Revisited , Coxeter & Greitzer 💲 (classic). GeoGebra : draw every problem precisely. It's free. Build For every geometry problem: (1) draw an accurate figure, (2) guess the key concurrency/collinearity from the figure, (3) prove it synthetically, (4) as a backup, prove it with complex/barycentric bash. Solve G1–G4 from recent IMO Shortlists. Traps Proofs that only work for one configuration. Use directed angles. Over-relying on bash. It's a time sink on hard problems, so keep it as plan B."},{"t":"06 · Contest Practice & Solution Writing","u":"math-olympiad/06-contest-practice.html","s":"Math Olympiad","h":[{"n":"Core ideas","id":"core-ideas"},{"n":"Where to find problems","id":"where-to-find-problems"},{"n":"Done when","id":"done-when"}],"x":"06 · Contest Practice & Solution Writing Module 06 ⏱ 1 min read View Markdown source Goal: turn knowledge into points under exam conditions. · Time: ongoing from week 3 Core ideas Mock exams: 3 problems, 4.5 h, no notes, real timer. Use past national olympiads → IMO Shortlist (ISL) → past IMOs. Grade strictly on the 0–7 IMO scale. A \"nearly complete\" proof usually earns 0–2, not 6. Time allocation: first 30 min on reading all three problems and trying small cases. Spend the most time on the problem you're most likely to fully solve. Partial credit: write down proven lemmas, reductions, and the answer with a construction. Graders reward substantial progress . Post-mortem every mock: for each problem, what was the key idea, and what would have led you to it faster? Log it in progress.md . Difficulty ladder (roughly): school/regional → national olympiad → hard national (e.g. USAMO/Balkan/APMO style) → ISL easy (A1/C1/G1/N1) → IMO P1/P4 → ISL medium → IMO P2/P5 → P3/P6. Where to find problems ⭐ AoPS Contest Collections : almost every olympiad ever, with community solutions. ⭐ IMO official site: problems & results and the IMO Shortlists (published after the following IMO). Evan Chen's practice materials / OTIS excerpts and his MOHS hardness ratings (to pick problems at the right level). Past papers of your own national olympiad . Ask the committee or past participants. Done when You consistently fully solve P1/P4-level problems in mocks and have partial progress on P2/P5."},{"t":"Math Olympiad Cheatsheet","u":"math-olympiad/cheatsheet.html","s":"Math Olympiad","h":[{"n":"Strategies (→ 01)","id":"strategies-01"},{"n":"Algebra (→ 02)","id":"algebra-02"},{"n":"Number theory (→ 03)","id":"number-theory-03"},{"n":"Combinatorics (→ 04)","id":"combinatorics-04"},{"n":"Geometry (→ 05)","id":"geometry-05"}],"x":"Math Olympiad Cheatsheet Cheatsheet ⏱ 2 min read View Markdown source One page to recap. If a line feels unfamiliar, open the module (→ NN). Strategies (→ 01) Small cases · draw it · work backwards · strengthen the induction · extremal element · invariant/monovariant · coloring/parity · pigeonhole · contradiction/descent · find the equality case / answer first Algebra (→ 02) Tool Statement AM-GM fracsum x_ingesqrt[n]prod x_i, equality iff all are equal Cauchy–Schwarz (sum a_i^2)(sum b_i^2)ge(sum a_ib_i)^2 Titu sumfraca_i^2b_igefrac(sum a_i)^2sum b_i Schur (t>0) sum a^t(a-b)(a-c)ge0 Jensen convex f: f(mean)lemean of f Vieta (cubic) r+s+t=-frac ba, rs+st+tr=frac ca, rst=-frac da Integer poly a-bmid P(a)-P(b) FE plays x=y=0; y=0; y=x; y=-x; swap; injective/surjective; fixed points; verify the answer Number theory (→ 03) Tool Statement Bézout exists x,y: ax+by=gcd(a,b) Fermat / Euler a^p-1equiv1\\ (p) · a^varphi(n)equiv1\\ (n) for gcd(a,n)=1 Wilson (p-1)!equiv-1pmod p Order lemma a^kequiv1ifford(a)mid k; ord_p(a)mid p-1 gcd of powers gcd(a^m-1,a^n-1)=a^gcd(m,n)-1 LTE (odd p) pmid a-b, pnmid ab: v_p(a^n-b^n)=v_p(a-b)+v_p(n) Legendre v_p(n!)=sumlfloor n/p^krfloor QR -1 is a QR mod odd p ⟺ pequiv1\\ (4); Euler's criterion left(frac apright)equiv a^(p-1)/2 Squares mod mod 4: 0,1 · mod 8: 0,1,4 · mod 3: 0,1 · mod 9 (cubes): 0,1,8 Combinatorics (→ 04) Stars & bars: binomn+k-1k-1 · Derangements D_n=n!sum_i=0^nfrac(-1)^ii! · Catalan frac1n+1binom2nn Handshake: sumdeg=2|E| · Tree: |E|=|V|-1 · Bipartite ⟺ no odd cycle · Hall: |N(S)|ge|S|\\ forall S Max/min problems = bound + construction . Expected value < 1 ⇒ a good object exists. Geometry (→ 05) Inscribed angle = ½ arc · cyclic ⟺ opposite angles sum to 180° ⟺ equal angles on the same chord Power of a point: PAcdot PB=PCcdot PD=|PO^2-r^2| · radical axes of 3 circles concur Euler line: O,G,H collinear, OG:GH=1:2 · nine-point circle center = midpoint of OH, radius R/2 Reflections of H over the sides and side midpoints lie on the circumcircle · AH=2Rcos A Incenter–excenter: M_A (arc midpoint of BC) is the center of the circle through B,C,I,I_A Ceva: fracBDDCfracCEEAfracAFFB=1 · Menelaus: same product =-1 (signed) · Ptolemy: ACcdot BD=ABcdot CD+ADcdot BC for cyclic ABCD Law of sines: fracasin A=2R · Area =rs=fracabc4R=sqrts(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)"},{"t":"Math Olympiad Resources","u":"math-olympiad/resources.html","s":"Math Olympiad","h":[{"n":"Books by stage","id":"books-by-stage"},{"n":"Free handouts (the best free material)","id":"free-handouts-the-best-free-material"},{"n":"Problems & community","id":"problems-community"},{"n":"Tools","id":"tools"}],"x":"Math Olympiad Resources Resources ⏱ 1 min read View Markdown source ⭐ = the main resource · 💲 = paid · everything else is free. Books by stage Stage Book Use for Ramp ⭐ The Art and Craft of Problem Solving , Zeitz 💲 strategies, first proofs Ramp AoPS Volume 1 & 2 , Intermediate Algebra , Intermediate Counting , Intro to NT 💲 filling gaps National ⭐ Problem-Solving Strategies , Engel 💲 huge problem bank by technique National ⭐ EGMO , Evan Chen 💲 geometry National Modern Olympiad Number Theory , Khurmi (free PDF, on AoPS) number theory National Problem Solving Methods in Combinatorics , Soberón 💲 combinatorics IMO Problems from the Book , Andreescu & Dospinescu 💲 hard algebra/NT IMO Lemmas in Olympiad Geometry , Andreescu et al. 💲 geometry lemmas IMO Secrets in Inequalities , Pham Kim Hung 💲 inequalities Free handouts (the best free material) ⭐ Evan Chen's handouts : inequalities, orders, functional equations, geometry, proof writing. ⭐ Yufei Zhao's olympiad handouts : from MOP (the US training camp). Evan Chen's Napkin : higher math for olympiad students (optional, beyond the IMO). Problems & community ⭐ AoPS Contest Collections : past olympiads with solutions. ⭐ IMO official site : problems, rules, and results. AoPS forums : ask questions, read solution discussions. MOHS hardness scale (Evan Chen) : difficulty ratings so you can pick problems at the right level. Tools GeoGebra : accurate geometry figures. This repo's interactive tools : triangle centers, modular lab, flashcards."},{"t":"Math Olympiad: Progress & Log","u":"math-olympiad/progress.html","s":"Math Olympiad","h":[{"n":"Key dates (fill these in first!)","id":"key-dates-fill-these-in-first"},{"n":"Status board","id":"status-board"},{"n":"Mock exam log","id":"mock-exam-log"},{"n":"Key-idea notebook","id":"key-idea-notebook"},{"n":"Session log (newest first)","id":"session-log-newest-first"}],"x":"Math Olympiad: Progress & Log Progress log ⏱ 1 min read View Markdown source Key dates (fill these in first!) Event Date Notes National olympiad round 1 National final / team selection IMO 2027 July 2027 check eligibility Status board ⬜ not started · 🟨 in progress · ✅ done # Module Status Problems solved Weakest sub-topic 01 Problem solving & proofs ⬜ 02 Algebra ⬜ 03 Number theory ⬜ 04 Combinatorics ⬜ 05 Geometry ⬜ 06 Contest practice ⬜ mocks: Mock exam log Date Source P1 P2 P3 Total /21 Key lesson Key-idea notebook One line per problem. This is what you review before contests. | Problem | Area | Key idea (1 line) | |---|---|---| | | | | Session log (newest first)"},{"t":"Physics Olympiad: School/Regional → National → IPhO","u":"physics-olympiad/index.html","s":"Physics Olympiad","h":[{"n":"⚠️ Read first: eligibility and format","id":"read-first-eligibility-and-format"},{"n":"The map","id":"the-map"},{"n":"Interactive tools → interactive/","id":"interactive-tools-interactive"},{"n":"Weekly routine (~10–12 h)","id":"weekly-routine-1012-h"}],"x":"Physics Olympiad: School/Regional → National → IPhO Roadmap ⏱ 2 min read View Markdown source Starting point: you've passed school/regional rounds and are strong at math and physics. Target: national team selection, then IPhO-level theory and experiment. ⚠️ Read first: eligibility and format IPhO contestants generally must be under 20 and not yet university students . As a senior, this cycle is likely your last . Check your national rules and selection dates now. IPhO format: a theory exam (3 long multi-part problems, 5 h, 30 points) and an experimental exam (1–2 problems, 5 h, 20 points). 40% of the score is experimental , and many self-learners neglect it. Problems are long and guided . Each problem builds a model step by step, so careful approximation and modeling beat obscure formulas. The map # Module Time You can… 01 Math methods & estimation 1–2 wk approximations, dimensional analysis, ODEs, vector calculus basics 02 Mechanics 5–7 wk rigid bodies, oscillations, orbits, non-inertial frames, fluids 03 Thermodynamics & statistical physics 3–4 wk heat engines, kinetic theory, Boltzmann, radiation 04 Electromagnetism 5–7 wk fields, circuits, induction, image charges, EM waves 05 Waves & optics 2–3 wk interference, diffraction, geometric optics, Doppler 06 Modern physics 3–4 wk special relativity, quantum basics, atomic and nuclear physics 07 Experimental physics ongoing uncertainties, linearization, graphing, designing measurements Order: 01 → 02 first (mechanics underlies everything). Then rotate 03–06. Run 07 every week from the start. Interactive tools → interactive/ Orbit simulator: set the launch speed and see circles, ellipses, and escape. Kepler's laws and energy live. Driven damped oscillator: resonance curve, phase lag, Q-factor, transients. Wave interference: two-source pattern + double-slit/grating intensity. Linear fit & uncertainty: least squares with slope/intercept errors, for the experimental exam. Flashcards: formulas, constants, and standard results. Weekly routine (~10–12 h) Block Do 3 × 2 h Theory: Kalda/Zhou handout section → 3–5 problems 1 × 2 h Experiment: a home experiment or a past IPhO/APhO experimental problem's data analysis 1 × 3 h Mock (from week 4): one full past theory problem in 1h40, then grade strictly with the official marking scheme 30 min Update progress.md and review flashcards Support files: cheatsheet.md · resources.md · progress.md"},{"t":"01 · Math Methods & Estimation","u":"physics-olympiad/01-math-methods.html","s":"Physics Olympiad","h":[{"n":"Skip test","id":"skip-test"},{"n":"Core ideas","id":"core-ideas"},{"n":"Learn from","id":"learn-from"},{"n":"Build","id":"build"}],"x":"01 · Math Methods & Estimation Module 01 ⏱ 1 min read View Markdown source Goal: the mathematical moves olympiad physics uses constantly. · Time: 1–2 weeks (you're strong at math, so this is mostly about the physics habits ) Skip test Use dimensional analysis to find the period of a pendulum and the speed of deep-water waves (vproptosqrtglambda). Expand (1+x)^n, sqrt1+x, sin x, cos x, e^x, ln(1+x) to second order. Solve ddot x + 2gammadot x + omega_0^2 x = 0 in all three damping regimes. Estimate the number of air molecules in your room, to within a factor of 3. Compute the flux of a uniform field through a hemisphere using a symmetry argument, not an integral. Core ideas Dimensional analysis: write [Q] = M^aL^bT^cldots and match exponents. It gives the answer up to a constant, and it's a quick check on every result. Small-quantity expansions: (1+x)^napprox1+nx+fracn(n-1)2x^2. Keep the first non-vanishing order. Most IPhO tasks say \"to first order in epsilon\". Fermi estimation: break it into factors and get each within ×3. ODEs you must solve instantly: exponential growth/decay, SHM, damped/driven oscillator, dot v = -kv^2-type drag, separation of variables. Vector calculus (light): gradient, divergence, curl as physical ideas. Gauss and Stokes theorems → Gauss's law and Ampère's law. Symmetry first: before integrating, ask what symmetry makes the integral trivial. Limiting cases: check every answer at mto0, thetato0, Rtoinfty, etc. Graphical/numerical thinking: sketch functions, find fixed points, linearize around equilibria (stability = sign of the second derivative of the potential). Learn from ⭐ Kevin Zhou's handouts : start with the introductory/preliminary handout and M1 (which covers the ODE toolkit). These are the best free olympiad physics materials. Street-Fighting Mathematics, Sanjoy Mahajan : an open-access book (free PDF from MIT Press) on estimation and approximation. Mathematical Methods for Physics and Engineering , Riley, Hobson & Bence 💲: reference only. Build Redo 10 textbook results using only dimensional analysis + one physical argument, and check them against the exact formula."},{"t":"02 · Mechanics","u":"physics-olympiad/02-mechanics.html","s":"Physics Olympiad","h":[{"n":"Skip test","id":"skip-test"},{"n":"Core ideas","id":"core-ideas"},{"n":"Learn from","id":"learn-from"},{"n":"Build","id":"build"},{"n":"Traps","id":"traps"}],"x":"02 · Mechanics Module 02 ⏱ 2 min read View Markdown source Goal: the backbone of every IPhO (often 1–2 of the 3 theory problems). · Time: 5–7 weeks 👉 Interactive: Orbit simulator · Driven damped oscillator Skip test A ladder leans on a frictionless wall with floor friction μ. Find the minimum angle before it slips. A cylinder rolls without slipping down an incline. Find its acceleration two ways: forces/torques, and energy. Find the period of small oscillations about a stable equilibrium of U(x) (omega^2 = U''(x_0)/m). Derive Kepler's third law for circular orbits, and the escape velocity. Explain the effective potential. In a rotating frame, write down the centrifugal and Coriolis forces. A rocket expels mass at relative speed u. Derive Delta v = uln(m_0/m_f). Core ideas Kinematics: projectiles, relative motion, constraints (strings, rods, rolling: v = omega R). Newton's laws: free-body diagrams. Choose axes wisely. Non-inertial frames add -mmathbf a_frame, centrifugal momega^2r, and Coriolis -2mboldsymbolomegatimesmathbf v. Conservation laws: energy (work–energy, potential energy), momentum (collisions, CM frame), angular momentum (central forces, a skater pulling in her arms). Ask which quantity is conserved before writing equations. Rigid bodies: moment of inertia (parallel/perpendicular axis theorems), torque boldsymboltau = mathbf rtimesmathbf F, tau = Ialpha about the CM or a fixed axis, rolling, the instantaneous axis of rotation, statics (Σ F = 0 and Σ τ = 0 about any point). Oscillations: SHM, small-oscillation approximations, energy method, coupled oscillators and normal modes , damped/driven oscillators, resonance, the Q-factor. Gravitation: Kepler's laws, the effective potential U_eff = -fracGMmr+fracL^22mr^2, orbital energy E = -fracGMm2a, the shell theorem. Variable mass: rockets, chains, conveyor belts. Fluids: hydrostatics, Archimedes, Bernoulli, continuity, surface tension (Laplace pressure Delta p = 2sigma/R), viscosity basics. 💡 Lagrangian mechanics is optional at IPhO but often a shortcut for complicated constraints. Learn it after the Newtonian toolkit is solid. Learn from ⭐ Jaan Kalda: Mechanics study guide : a compact list of \"ideas\" with olympiad problems. Built specifically for IPhO. Also see Kevin Zhou's mechanics ( M ) handouts. ⭐ Introduction to Classical Mechanics, David Morin 💲: the best problem-based textbook. Ch. 1–9. 200 Puzzling Physics Problems , Gnädig, Honyek & Riley 💲 · Problems in General Physics , Irodov (classic and hard). Build Solve every Kalda mechanics idea's associated problems. Then do 5 past IPhO mechanics problems under time pressure. Traps Taking torques about a point that's accelerating (only the CM or a fixed point is safe for tau = Ialpha). Forgetting friction can point either way. Assume a direction and let the sign tell you."},{"t":"03 · Thermodynamics & Statistical Physics","u":"physics-olympiad/03-thermo-stat.html","s":"Physics Olympiad","h":[{"n":"Skip test","id":"skip-test"},{"n":"Core ideas","id":"core-ideas"},{"n":"Learn from","id":"learn-from"},{"n":"Build","id":"build"}],"x":"03 · Thermodynamics & Statistical Physics Module 03 ⏱ 1 min read View Markdown source Goal: gases, heat engines, entropy, and the Boltzmann factor. · Time: 3–4 weeks Skip test Derive the work done in isothermal and adiabatic expansions of an ideal gas. Prove the Carnot efficiency 1 - T_c/T_h and why no engine can beat it. Derive pV = frac13 Nmlangle v^2rangle from kinetic theory. State equipartition. Use the Boltzmann factor to find the isothermal atmosphere's density profile. Use Stefan–Boltzmann to estimate Earth's equilibrium temperature. Core ideas Ideal gas: pV = nRT. Internal energy U = frac f2 nRT (f = degrees of freedom). C_p - C_V = R, gamma = C_p/C_V. First law: dU = delta Q - p\\,dV. Processes: isobaric, isochoric, isothermal, adiabatic (pV^gamma = const), polytropic. Second law & entropy: dS = delta Q_rev/T. The entropy of an isolated system never decreases. Carnot cycle, refrigerators, COP. Kinetic theory: pressure from collisions, Maxwell speed distribution, mean free path lambda = frac1sqrt2 nsigma, effusion. Boltzmann factor: P propto e^-E/kT. Equipartition: frac12 kT per quadratic degree of freedom. Heat transfer: conduction (Fourier's law, thermal resistance as in circuits), convection (qualitative), radiation (Stefan–Boltzmann P = sigma A T^4, Wien's law lambda_maxT = b). Phase transitions: latent heat, Clausius–Clapeyron (basic), vapor pressure and humidity. Surface phenomena and real gases: van der Waals (qualitative). Learn from ⭐ Kalda: Thermodynamics study guide + Kevin Zhou's thermodynamics ( T ) handouts. An Introduction to Thermal Physics, Daniel Schroeder 💲: excellent and readable (ch. 1–4, 6). Fundamentals of Physics, Halliday/Resnick/Walker 💲: the basics, if you need them. Build Draw p–V diagrams for 5 cycles and compute their efficiencies. Estimate Earth's temperature with and without albedo."},{"t":"04 · Electromagnetism","u":"physics-olympiad/04-electromagnetism.html","s":"Physics Olympiad","h":[{"n":"Skip test","id":"skip-test"},{"n":"Core ideas","id":"core-ideas"},{"n":"Learn from","id":"learn-from"},{"n":"Build","id":"build"}],"x":"04 · Electromagnetism Module 04 ⏱ 1 min read View Markdown source Goal: fields, circuits, and induction at the level of IPhO's long problems. · Time: 5–7 weeks Skip test Use Gauss's law for the field of a uniformly charged sphere (inside and outside) and an infinite plane. Use an image charge: the force between a point charge and a grounded conducting plane. Solve an RC charging circuit and an LC circuit. Give the energy stored in C and L. Find mathbf B of a long straight wire and a solenoid with Ampère's law, and at the center of a loop with Biot–Savart. A rod slides on rails in a uniform B. Find the EMF, current, and terminal velocity. Find the motion of a charged particle in uniform E ⟂ B (drift velocity E/B). Core ideas Electrostatics: Coulomb, superposition, mathbf E = -nabla V. Gauss's law ointmathbf Ecdot dmathbf A = Q/varepsilon_0 (use symmetry!). Conductors: E = 0 inside, the surface is an equipotential. Method of images . Capacitance, energy frac12CV^2, energy density frac12varepsilon_0E^2. Dielectrics: varepsilon_r, polarization (qualitative/basic). Circuits: Kirchhoff's laws, series/parallel, Thevenin equivalent , infinite ladders (self-similarity), RC/RL/LC/RLC transients, AC with complex impedance (Z_L = iomega L, Z_C = 1/(iomega C)), non-linear elements (diodes, graphically). Magnetostatics: Lorentz force q(mathbf E + mathbf vtimesmathbf B), Biot–Savart, Ampère's law , magnetic dipoles, the force on a current-carrying wire. Induction: Faraday mathcal E = -dPhi/dt, Lenz's law, motional EMF vBL, self/mutual inductance, energy frac12LI^2, eddy currents. Maxwell's equations & EM waves: displacement current, c = 1/sqrtmu_0varepsilon_0, the Poynting vector, radiation pressure. 🔭 Analogies: thermal circuits ↔ electric circuits; gravity ↔ electrostatics (Gauss). Learn from ⭐ Kalda: Electricity & Magnetism study guide + Kevin Zhou's E&M ( E ) handouts. ⭐ Electricity and Magnetism, Purcell & Morin (3rd ed.) 💲: the olympiad-standard E&M textbook. Introduction to Electrodynamics, Griffiths 💲: ch. 1–7, for more mathematical depth. Build Solve 5 infinite/symmetric circuit puzzles. Derive the field of a uniformly magnetized sphere (stretch). Do 3 past IPhO E&M problems."},{"t":"05 · Waves & Optics","u":"physics-olympiad/05-waves-optics.html","s":"Physics Olympiad","h":[{"n":"Skip test","id":"skip-test"},{"n":"Core ideas","id":"core-ideas"},{"n":"Learn from","id":"learn-from"},{"n":"Build","id":"build"}],"x":"05 · Waves & Optics Module 05 ⏱ 1 min read View Markdown source Goal: interference, diffraction, and geometric optics. These show up in both the theory and the experiment. · Time: 2–3 weeks 👉 Interactive: Wave interference & diffraction Skip test Derive the double-slit fringe spacing Delta y = lambda L/d. Find the condition for thin-film constructive reflection (including the phase flip). Locate the image for a thin lens and a spherical mirror. Trace the rays for a two-lens system. Use the Doppler formula for a moving source and a moving observer (sound). Find the resolving power of a telescope (Rayleigh criterion 1.22lambda/D). Core ideas Waves: y = Asin(kx - omega t), v = omega/k = flambda. The wave equation. String speed v = sqrtT/mu, sound speed. Standing waves and harmonics. Beats. Superposition: phase difference Deltaphi = frac2pilambdaDelta(path) +  reflection phase. Constructive at Deltaphi = 2pi m. Interference: double slit, thin films (π phase shift on reflection off a higher-n medium), Newton's rings, Michelson interferometer. Diffraction: single slit minima at asintheta = mlambda, diffraction grating maxima at dsintheta = mlambda. Intensity = (single-slit envelope) × (multi-slit interference). Rayleigh criterion. Geometric optics: Fermat's principle, Snell's law, total internal reflection, the thin lens/mirror equation frac1f = frac1d_o+frac1d_i, magnification, optical instruments (telescope, microscope), prisms and dispersion. Polarization: Malus's law I = I_0cos^2theta, Brewster's angle tantheta_B = n_2/n_1. Doppler effect: sound f' = ffracv pm v_ov mp v_s. Relativistic Doppler in module 06. Learn from ⭐ Kalda: Optics/waves material + Kevin Zhou's waves/optics handouts. Optics, Eugene Hecht 💲 for reference · Falstad wave simulations to play with ripples. Build Measure the track spacing of a CD/DVD with a laser pointer (diffraction grating). A classic home experiment for module 07, too."},{"t":"06 · Modern Physics","u":"physics-olympiad/06-modern-physics.html","s":"Physics Olympiad","h":[{"n":"Skip test","id":"skip-test"},{"n":"Core ideas","id":"core-ideas"},{"n":"Learn from","id":"learn-from"},{"n":"Build","id":"build"}],"x":"06 · Modern Physics Module 06 ⏱ 1 min read View Markdown source Goal: special relativity, quantum basics, and atomic/nuclear physics at IPhO level. · Time: 3–4 weeks Skip test Derive time dilation and length contraction from the Lorentz transformation. Use 4-momentum invariance to find the threshold energy of a particle-creation reaction. Compute the photoelectric stopping potential, and the Compton shift. Use the Bohr model to derive the hydrogen energy levels E_n = -13.6\\,eV/n^2. Use Heisenberg to estimate the ground-state energy of a particle in a box. Compute the activity after t given the half-life. Compute a nuclear reaction's Q-value. Core ideas Special relativity: postulates; Lorentz transformation, gamma = 1/sqrt1-v^2/c^2; time dilation, length contraction, relativity of simultaneity; velocity addition u' = fracu-v1-uv/c^2; E = gamma mc^2, mathbf p = gamma mmathbf v; invariant E^2 - (pc)^2 = (mc^2)^2. Use invariants for collisions and decays. Relativistic Doppler. Quantum basics: photon E = hf, p = h/lambda; photoelectric effect; Compton Deltalambda = frachm_ec(1-costheta); de Broglie lambda = h/p; uncertainty Delta xDelta p gtrsim hbar/2 (for estimates); particle in a box E_n = fracn^2h^28mL^2; tunneling (qualitative). Atoms: Bohr model, spectral lines (Rydberg), the energy-level diagram, X-rays. Nuclear: binding energy and mass defect, alpha/beta/gamma decay, half-life N = N_02^-t/T_1/2, fission/fusion Q-values. Optional (IPhO-adjacent): blackbody (Planck), lasers, semiconductors basics, cosmology/astrophysics estimates. Learn from ⭐ Kevin Zhou's relativity (R) and modern-physics handouts + Kalda . Spacetime Physics, Taylor & Wheeler 💲 (relativity done beautifully) · Morin's Classical Mechanics ch. 11–14 (relativity). Feynman Lectures, Vol. III for quantum intuition (free online). Build 10 relativity problems solved with invariants only (no frame juggling). Compare them against the brute-force method."},{"t":"07 · Experimental Physics","u":"physics-olympiad/07-experimental.html","s":"Physics Olympiad","h":[{"n":"Skip test","id":"skip-test"},{"n":"Core ideas","id":"core-ideas"},{"n":"Learn from","id":"learn-from"},{"n":"Home experiments (cheap)","id":"home-experiments-cheap"}],"x":"07 · Experimental Physics Module 07 ⏱ 2 min read View Markdown source Goal: earn the 20 experimental points. It's the most trainable part of the IPhO. · Time: ~2 h/week, ongoing 👉 Interactive: Linear fit & uncertainty tool Skip test Propagate uncertainty for g = 4pi^2L/T^2 given Delta L and Delta T. Linearize T = 2pisqrtL/g, I = I_0e^-mu x, and y = ax^n so a straight-line graph gives the unknowns. Find a slope and its uncertainty from a least-squares fit (or the max/min slope method). Explain systematic vs random error, and how to reduce each. Explain why you should measure 20 oscillations instead of 1. Core ideas Uncertainty: report x = bar x pm Delta x with 1–2 significant figures in Delta x, and the value rounded to match. Sum/difference: absolute errors add in quadrature: Delta(apm b) = sqrtDelta a^2+Delta b^2. Product/power: relative errors add in quadrature: fracDelta ff = sqrtleft(nfracDelta aaright)^2 + left(mfracDelta bbright)^2 for f = a^nb^m. Repeated measurements: standard error of the mean sigma/sqrt N. Linearization: transform the data so the theory predicts a straight line (T^2 vs L; ln I vs x; ln y vs ln x). The slope/intercept then give the physical constants. Graphing at IPhO (graded!): a title, labeled axes with units, sensible scales (the data fills ≥ ½ the page), error bars, a best-fit line, and the slope read off a large triangle. Experimental design: measure many periods, vary over a wide range, repeat, spot outliers, control variables, and check for systematics (zero offset, parallax, timing reaction). Read the problem's instructions on rounding and on which method to use. Points are given per step. Learn from ⭐ Past IPhO / APhO / EuPhO experimental problems with marking schemes (see resources ). Practice the data-analysis parts even without the apparatus. ⭐ Kalda: Experimental study guide . An Introduction to Error Analysis, John Taylor 💲: the standard text (ch. 1–8). Home experiments (cheap) Pendulum → g to < 1% (linearize T^2 vs L). CD/DVD as a diffraction grating with a laser pointer → track spacing. Cooling coffee cup → Newton's law of cooling (fit ln(T - T_room) vs t). A bouncing ball → coefficient of restitution from the sound of the bounces (phone audio recorder). A smartphone sensor app (e.g. phyphox ) → acceleration, rotation, magnetic field, sound frequency."},{"t":"Physics Olympiad Cheatsheet","u":"physics-olympiad/cheatsheet.html","s":"Physics Olympiad","h":[{"n":"Constants (to 3 sig figs)","id":"constants-to-3-sig-figs"},{"n":"Approximations (→ 01)","id":"approximations-01"},{"n":"Mechanics (→ 02)","id":"mechanics-02"},{"n":"Thermo (→ 03)","id":"thermo-03"},{"n":"E&M (→ 04)","id":"em-04"},{"n":"Waves & optics (→ 05)","id":"waves-optics-05"},{"n":"Modern (→ 06)","id":"modern-06"},{"n":"Experiment (→ 07)","id":"experiment-07"}],"x":"Physics Olympiad Cheatsheet Cheatsheet ⏱ 2 min read View Markdown source One-page recap. If a line feels unfamiliar, open the module (→ NN). Constants (to 3 sig figs) c = 3.00times10^8 m/s · G = 6.67times10^-11 · h = 6.63times10^-34 J·s · hbar = 1.05times10^-34 · k_B = 1.38times10^-23 J/K · N_A = 6.02times10^23 · R = 8.31 J/(mol·K) · e = 1.60times10^-19 C · m_e = 9.11times10^-31 kg · m_p = 1.67times10^-27 kg · varepsilon_0 = 8.85times10^-12 · mu_0 = 4pitimes10^-7 · sigma = 5.67times10^-8 W/(m²K⁴) · 1 eV = 1.60times10^-19 J Approximations (→ 01) (1+x)^napprox1+nx · sin xapprox x - x^3/6 · cos xapprox1-x^2/2 · ln(1+x)approx x - x^2/2 · e^xapprox1+x+x^2/2 Small oscillations about x_0: omega^2 = U''(x_0)/m Mechanics (→ 02) Moments of inertia: rod (center) frac112ML^2 · rod (end) frac13ML^2 · disk frac12MR^2 · ring MR^2 · solid sphere frac25MR^2 · shell frac23MR^2 · parallel axis I = I_cm+Md^2 Rolling down an incline: a = fracgsintheta1+I/(mR^2) Rotating frame: mathbf F_cf = momega^2mathbf r_perp, mathbf F_cor = -2mboldsymbolomegatimesmathbf v Orbits: v_circ = sqrtGM/r · v_esc = sqrt2GM/r · T^2 = frac4pi^2GMa^3 · E = -fracGMm2a · U_eff = -fracGMmr+fracL^22mr^2 Rocket: Delta v = ulnfracm_0m_f · Damped: omega_d = sqrtomega_0^2-gamma^2 (for ddot x+2gammadot x+omega_0^2x=0) · Q = omega_0/(2gamma) Fluids: p = p_0+rho gh · Bernoulli p+frac12rho v^2+rho gh = const · Laplace Delta p = 2sigma/R (drop) Thermo (→ 03) pV = nRT · U = frac f2nRT · C_p-C_V = R · adiabatic pV^gamma = const, TV^gamma-1 = const W_iso = nRTlnfracV_2V_1 · eta_Carnot = 1-fracT_cT_h · dS = delta Q/T · Ppropto e^-E/kT · v_rms = sqrt3kT/m · P = sigma AT^4 · lambda_maxT = 2.90times10^-3 m·K E&M (→ 04) Sphere: E = frackQr^2 outside, frackQrR^3 inside (uniform) · plane fracsigma2varepsilon_0 · capacitor C = fracvarepsilon_0Ad, U = frac12CV^2 Wire B = fracmu_0I2pi r · loop center fracmu_0I2R · solenoid mu_0nI · U_L = frac12LI^2 · u = fracvarepsilon_0E^22+fracB^22mu_0 tau_RC = RC · tau_RL = L/R · omega_LC = 1/sqrtLC · Z_L = iomega L, Z_C = frac1iomega C Cyclotron r = fracmvqB, omega = fracqBm · E⟂B drift v = E/B · mathcal E = -fracdPhidt, motional mathcal E = vBL Waves & optics (→ 05) Double slit Delta y = fraclambda Ld · grating dsintheta = mlambda · single-slit minima asintheta = mlambda · Rayleigh 1.22lambda/D · thin lens frac1f = frac1d_o+frac1d_i · Snell n_1sintheta_1 = n_2sintheta_2 · Brewster tantheta_B = n_2/n_1 · string v = sqrtT/mu · Doppler f' = ffracvpm v_ovmp v_s Modern (→ 06) gamma = (1-beta^2)^-1/2 · E^2 = (pc)^2+(mc^2)^2 · u' = fracu-v1-uv/c^2 · relativistic Doppler f' = fsqrtfrac1+beta1-beta (approaching) E = hf · lambda = h/p · Compton Deltalambda = frachm_ec(1-costheta) · Bohr E_n = -frac13.6 eVn^2 · box E_n = fracn^2h^28mL^2 · hc = 1240 eV·nm · N = N_02^-t/T_1/2 Experiment (→ 07) f = a^nb^m: fracDelta ff = sqrt(nfracDelta aa)^2+(mfracDelta bb)^2 · mean error sigma/sqrt N · linearize: T^2 vs L, ln y vs x (exponential), ln y vs ln x (power law)"},{"t":"Physics Olympiad Resources","u":"physics-olympiad/resources.html","s":"Physics Olympiad","h":[{"n":"The core stack","id":"the-core-stack"},{"n":"Textbooks (💲, pick per module)","id":"textbooks-pick-per-module"},{"n":"Past problems","id":"past-problems"},{"n":"Free extras","id":"free-extras"}],"x":"Physics Olympiad Resources Resources ⏱ 1 min read View Markdown source ⭐ = the main resource · 💲 = paid · everything else is free. The core stack ⭐ Kevin Zhou's olympiad handouts : complete, free, problem-rich (mechanics, E&M, thermo, waves, relativity, modern). The best single resource. ⭐ Jaan Kalda's study guides : compact \"idea lists\" (kinematics, mechanics, E&M, thermodynamics, experimental) with IPhO-level problems. ⭐ Past IPhO problems with official solutions and marking schemes (below). Textbooks (💲, pick per module) Book Module Introduction to Classical Mechanics, Morin 02 (and relativity, 06) Electricity and Magnetism, Purcell & Morin 04 An Introduction to Thermal Physics, Schroeder 03 An Introduction to Error Analysis, Taylor 07 200 Puzzling Physics Problems / 200 More Puzzling…, Gnädig et al. all (problem books) Physics Olympiad: Basic to Advanced Exercises (Japan PhO committee) all Problems in General Physics, Irodov all (hard drill) Fundamentals of Physics, Halliday/Resnick/Walker gap filling Past problems ⭐ IPhO official site and the IPhO unofficial archive : past theory and experimental problems, with solutions. APhO (Asian), EuPhO (European), and USAPhO ( AAPT Physics Team past exams ): excellent intermediate-level sets. Physics Cup (Kalda) : very hard, creative problems, with an online competition each year. Your country's national olympiad archive. Free extras The Feynman Lectures on Physics : deep intuition. Falstad simulations : circuits, waves, E&M. phyphox : turns your phone into a lab (for module 07). This repo's interactive tools ."},{"t":"Physics Olympiad: Progress & Log","u":"physics-olympiad/progress.html","s":"Physics Olympiad","h":[{"n":"Key dates","id":"key-dates"},{"n":"Status board","id":"status-board"},{"n":"Mock log","id":"mock-log"},{"n":"Ideas notebook","id":"ideas-notebook"},{"n":"Session log (newest first)","id":"session-log-newest-first"}],"x":"Physics Olympiad: Progress & Log Progress log ⏱ 1 min read View Markdown source Key dates Event Date Notes National olympiad round 1 National final / team selection IPhO 2027 July 2027 check eligibility Status board ⬜ not started · 🟨 in progress · ✅ done # Module Status Kalda ideas done Past IPhO problems done 01 Math methods ⬜ 02 Mechanics ⬜ 03 Thermo & stat ⬜ 04 E&M ⬜ 05 Waves & optics ⬜ 06 Modern ⬜ 07 Experimental ⬜ home experiments: Mock log Date Problem (year/no.) Type Score / max Time Lesson theory / exp Ideas notebook Problem Key idea / trick Session log (newest first)"},{"t":"Informatics Olympiad: School/Regional → National → IOI","u":"informatics-olympiad/index.html","s":"Informatics Olympiad","h":[{"n":"⚠️ Read first: eligibility and format","id":"read-first-eligibility-and-format"},{"n":"The map","id":"the-map"},{"n":"Interactive tools → interactive/","id":"interactive-tools-interactive"},{"n":"Weekly routine (~10–12 h)","id":"weekly-routine-1012-h"}],"x":"Informatics Olympiad: School/Regional → National → IOI Roadmap ⏱ 2 min read View Markdown source Starting point: you've passed school/regional rounds and know C++ and the STL. Target: national team selection, then IOI-level algorithms. ⚠️ Read first: eligibility and format IOI contestants must generally be secondary-school students (or have finished very recently) and under 20 . As a senior, this is likely your last cycle . Check your national selection dates now. IOI format: 2 contest days × 3 problems × 5 hours , 100 points per problem, split into subtasks . Submissions are judged live with full feedback. C++ is the language to use. Partial scoring changes strategy: collecting subtasks across all problems usually beats sinking 5 h into one full solution. The map # Module Time You can… 01 C++ for competitive programming 1 wk fast I/O, STL fluency, templates, debugging, stress testing 02 Complexity & core techniques 3–4 wk choose an algorithm from the constraints; binary search, two pointers, prefix sums, greedy 03 Data structures 4–5 wk DSU, Fenwick tree, segment tree (lazy), sparse table, monotonic stack 04 Graphs & trees 5–6 wk shortest paths, MST, SCC, LCA, tree DP 05 Dynamic programming 5–7 wk from knapsack to bitmask, tree DP, and DP optimizations 06 Math, strings & geometry 3–4 wk modular arithmetic, combinatorics, hashing, KMP/Z, convex hull 07 IOI skills & advanced topics ongoing subtask strategy, interactive problems, HLD, centroid decomposition, sqrt decomposition Order: 01 → 02 → (03 & 04 interleaved) → 05 → 06 → 07. Start doing virtual contests in week 2 . Interactive tools → interactive/ Graph search visualizer: draw walls/weights on a grid. Step through BFS, DFS, Dijkstra, and A*. Segment tree visualizer: watch the build, point update, and range query visit exactly O(log n) nodes. DP table animator: LCS, edit distance, 0/1 knapsack filled cell by cell, with traceback. Flashcards: complexities, patterns, and \"constraints → algorithm\" recall. Weekly routine (~10–12 h) Block Do 3 × 2 h Topic: read the USACO Guide section → solve 4–6 problems (CSES + guide problems) 1 × 3 h Virtual contest: Codeforces Div 2 / AtCoder ABC/ARC, or a past olympiad (oj.uz), under real time 1 × 1.5 h Upsolve: solve the contest problems you missed, after reading the editorial 15 min Log in progress.md : rating, solved count, one idea per problem Rough benchmarks (vary by country): strong national level ≈ Codeforces 1800–2100; IOI medal range ≈ 2100–2600+. The rating is a proxy, so spend your time on olympiad-style problems with subtasks. Support files: cheatsheet.md · resources.md · progress.md"},{"t":"01 · C++ for Competitive Programming","u":"informatics-olympiad/01-cpp-for-cp.html","s":"Informatics Olympiad","h":[{"n":"Skip test","id":"skip-test"},{"n":"Core ideas","id":"core-ideas"},{"n":"Learn from","id":"learn-from"},{"n":"Build","id":"build"}],"x":"01 · C++ for Competitive Programming Module 01 ⏱ 1 min read View Markdown source Goal: turn your STL knowledge into contest speed. · Time: ~1 week Skip test Write a template with fast I/O, long long aliases, and a multi-test-case loop. Sort a vector<pair<int,int>> by second element descending with a lambda. Use lower_bound on a sorted vector and on a set , and explain the complexity difference vs std::lower_bound(set.begin(), …) . Explain when int overflows (≈ 2.1 × 10⁹) and when to use long long . Write a stress test : brute force vs fast solution on random inputs, stopping at the first mismatch. Core ideas Template: cpp #include <bits/stdc++.h> using namespace std; using ll = long long; int main()  ios::sync_with_stdio(false); cin.tie(nullptr); int t = 1; // cin >> t; while (t--)  /* solve */   STL you'll use constantly: vector , array , pair / tuple , sort with a custom comparator, lower_bound / upper_bound , set / multiset / map (O(log n)), unordered_map (beware anti-hash tests), priority_queue (max-heap by default; use greater<> for a min-heap), deque , bitset , __builtin_popcount , iota , accumulate , next_permutation . Overflow: products of two ints ≤ 10⁹ need ll . Use 1LL * a * b . Debugging: compile with -Wall -Wextra -fsanitize=address,undefined -D_GLIBCXX_DEBUG locally. Print-debug to cerr . Stress testing: a random generator + a brute force + a diff loop. This finds most wrong-answer bugs, and at IOI you have time to do it. IOI specifics: problems often require implementing a function (no main ). Practice that format on oj.uz / past IOI tasks. Learn from ⭐ USACO Guide: General & Bronze : setup, C++ basics for CP, debugging, and stress testing. Competitive Programmer's Handbook (Laaksonen), ch. 1–4. cppreference for exact STL semantics. Build Your personal template + a stress.sh script (generator, brute, solution, compare). Keep both in a repo."},{"t":"02 · Complexity & Core Techniques","u":"informatics-olympiad/02-core-techniques.html","s":"Informatics Olympiad","h":[{"n":"Skip test","id":"skip-test"},{"n":"Core ideas","id":"core-ideas"},{"n":"Learn from","id":"learn-from"},{"n":"Build","id":"build"}],"x":"02 · Complexity & Core Techniques Module 02 ⏱ 1 min read View Markdown source Goal: read the constraints, infer the target complexity, and use the standard techniques. · Time: 3–4 weeks Skip test Given n ≤ 2·10⁵, name an acceptable complexity and an unacceptable one. Binary search on the answer: minimum maximum load when splitting an array into k parts. Two pointers: the longest subarray with sum ≤ S (positive numbers). Prefix sums + difference arrays: apply q range-add updates, then print the array, in O(n + q). Prove a greedy algorithm correct with an exchange argument (e.g. activity selection). Generate all subsets of n ≤ 20 elements with bitmasks. Core ideas The constraints → complexity table (≈ 10⁸ simple ops/second): n target ≤ 10–12 O(n!) ≤ 20–25 O(2ⁿ · n) ≤ 500 O(n³) ≤ 5000 O(n²) ≤ 10⁶ O(n log n) ≤ 10⁸ or larger O(n), O(log n), O(1) math - Sorting + custom comparators. Coordinate compression. - Binary search: on sorted arrays, and on the answer (a monotone predicate \"is x feasible?\"). Write it the same way every time ( lo always bad, hi always good). - Two pointers / sliding window: when moving the right end only ever moves the left end forward. - Prefix sums (1D/2D), difference arrays , prefix XOR/min. - Greedy: sort by the right key and prove it with an exchange argument . If you can't prove it, stress-test it. - Complete search / backtracking with pruning. Meet in the middle for n ≈ 40. - Bit manipulation: subsets, x & -x , XOR tricks. - Subtask thinking: brute force for small n is free points. Always submit it first. Learn from ⭐ USACO Guide: Bronze → Silver : complete search, sorting, binary search, two pointers, prefix sums, greedy. ⭐ CSES Problem Set : \"Introductory Problems\" and \"Sorting and Searching\" (do all ~45). Competitive Programmer's Handbook , ch. 5–8. Build Finish CSES Introductory + Sorting and Searching . Track the time per problem."},{"t":"03 · Data Structures","u":"informatics-olympiad/03-data-structures.html","s":"Informatics Olympiad","h":[{"n":"Skip test","id":"skip-test"},{"n":"Core ideas","id":"core-ideas"},{"n":"Learn from","id":"learn-from"},{"n":"Build","id":"build"}],"x":"03 · Data Structures Module 03 ⏱ 1 min read View Markdown source Goal: answer queries fast. These structures appear in a large share of IOI tasks. · Time: 4–5 weeks 👉 Interactive: Segment tree visualizer Skip test Implement DSU with path compression + union by size. Explain its α(n) complexity. Implement a Fenwick tree (BIT) for point update / prefix sum. Explain i & -i . Implement a segment tree with lazy propagation for range add + range sum. Answer static range-minimum queries in O(1) with a sparse table. Use a monotonic stack to find the next greater element for every index in O(n). Use a sliding-window maximum with a deque. Core ideas Stacks / queues / deques: monotonic stack (next greater/smaller, largest rectangle in a histogram), monotonic deque (sliding window min/max). Heaps ( priority_queue ): k-th largest, merging sorted lists, event simulation. Ordered sets/maps: set , multiset , and map for dynamic order statistics by value. The policy-based ordered_set (GNU pbds) gives the k-th element. DSU (union-find): connectivity, Kruskal, offline queries. Near-O(1) per operation. Fenwick tree: prefix sums with point updates, O(log n), very short code. Variants: range update/point query, 2D. Segment tree: any associative operation (sum, min, max, gcd, matrix product, custom merge). Lazy propagation for range updates. Walking down the tree (\"first index with prefix ≥ k\"). Sparse table: O(1) idempotent range queries (min/max/gcd) on static arrays. Square root decomposition and Mo's algorithm (offline range queries) are useful fallbacks. Offline tricks: sort queries, process events with a sweep line, reverse time (deletions → insertions with DSU). Learn from ⭐ USACO Guide: Silver → Gold : DSU, prefix sums, Fenwick/segment trees. ⭐ Codeforces EDU: Segment Tree (parts 1–2) : lectures + graded practice. cp-algorithms: Data structures : segment tree, Fenwick, DSU, sparse table. CSES: \"Range Queries\" section. Build Write each structure from memory 3 times on different days. Solve all of CSES Range Queries ."},{"t":"04 · Graphs & Trees","u":"informatics-olympiad/04-graphs-trees.html","s":"Informatics Olympiad","h":[{"n":"Skip test","id":"skip-test"},{"n":"Core ideas","id":"core-ideas"},{"n":"Learn from","id":"learn-from"},{"n":"Build","id":"build"}],"x":"04 · Graphs & Trees Module 04 ⏱ 1 min read View Markdown source Goal: model problems as graphs and pick the right traversal or shortest-path algorithm. · Time: 5–6 weeks 👉 Interactive: Graph search visualizer Skip test BFS on a grid with walls: the shortest path length and the path itself. Explain why Dijkstra fails with negative edges, and what to use instead. Topological sort with Kahn's algorithm. Detect a cycle. Find strongly connected components (Tarjan or Kosaraju). LCA with binary lifting in O(log n) per query. Find the diameter of a tree with two BFS/DFS passes. Core ideas Representation: adjacency lists vector<vector<int>> (or pair<int,int> for weights). Grids are implicit graphs. DFS: connected components, cycle detection, entry/exit times, bridges and articulation points (low-link). BFS: unweighted shortest paths, multi-source BFS, 0-1 BFS with a deque. Shortest paths: Dijkstra O((n+m) log n) for non-negative weights · Bellman–Ford O(nm) with negative edges/cycles · Floyd–Warshall O(n³) all pairs. DAGs: topological sort, DP over a DAG (longest path, counting paths). MST: Kruskal (sort + DSU) or Prim. SCC: Tarjan/Kosaraju → the condensation DAG. 2-SAT comes from SCC. Trees: parent/depth arrays, subtree sizes, Euler tour (subtree = contiguous range → segment tree), binary lifting LCA , distances via LCA, tree DP (rerooting), diameter, centroid. Modeling tricks: add a super-source, put states (node, extra info) in the vertex, reverse the edges, binary search + BFS. Learn from ⭐ USACO Guide: Silver/Gold graphs & trees . ⭐ CSES: \"Graph Algorithms\" and \"Tree Algorithms\" sections. cp-algorithms: Graphs · Competitive Programmer's Handbook , ch. 11–20. Build Solve the CSES graph section (≈ 36 problems) and tree section (≈ 16). Implement Dijkstra, LCA, and SCC from memory."},{"t":"05 · Dynamic Programming","u":"informatics-olympiad/05-dynamic-programming.html","s":"Informatics Olympiad","h":[{"n":"Skip test","id":"skip-test"},{"n":"Core ideas","id":"core-ideas"},{"n":"Learn from","id":"learn-from"},{"n":"Build","id":"build"}],"x":"05 · Dynamic Programming Module 05 ⏱ 1 min read View Markdown source Goal: IOI's favorite topic. Define states well, then optimize. · Time: 5–7 weeks 👉 Interactive: DP table animator Skip test 0/1 knapsack in O(nW) time and O(W) memory (loop direction!). LIS in O(n log n). Edit distance / LCS with path reconstruction. Interval DP: the minimum cost to merge stones / matrix-chain multiplication. Bitmask DP: TSP on n ≤ 20 in O(2ⁿn²). Tree DP: a maximum independent set on a tree. Core ideas Recipe: (1) state : what do I need to know about the past? (2) transition (3) base cases (4) order of computation (5) answer location. Write the state in words first. Classic families: Linear: Fibonacci-like, max subarray, coin change (count vs. minimize), LIS. Knapsack: 0/1 (iterate weight down ), unbounded (iterate up ), bounded (binary splitting). Grid / two-sequence: paths, LCS, edit distance. Interval: dp[l][r], with the split point inside. Bitmask: dp[mask][last], subsets, SOS DP. Tree DP: dp[v][state] from children. Rerooting for \"for every root\". Digit DP: count numbers ≤ N with a property, with state (pos, tight, …). DP on DAGs = shortest/longest paths. Optimizations (IOI level): prefix-sum transitions, monotonic deque, convex hull trick / Li Chao tree , divide & conquer DP, Knuth, \"Aliens\" trick (Lagrangian relaxation) , which is famous from IOI 2016. Memory: rolling arrays. Reconstruction: store choices or backtrack through the table. Subtasks often map to DP states: O(n³) → O(n²) → O(n log n) as the subtasks get harder. Learn from ⭐ AtCoder Educational DP Contest : 26 classic problems A–Z. Do all of them. ⭐ USACO Guide: Gold DP sections + CSES \"Dynamic Programming\" . cp-algorithms: DP (CHT, D&C DP, Knuth) · Errichto's DP videos on YouTube. Build AtCoder DP contest A–Z + CSES DP section. For each, write the state in one sentence in your progress log."},{"t":"06 · Math, Strings & Geometry","u":"informatics-olympiad/06-math-strings-geometry.html","s":"Informatics Olympiad","h":[{"n":"Skip test","id":"skip-test"},{"n":"Core ideas","id":"core-ideas"},{"n":"Learn from","id":"learn-from"},{"n":"Build","id":"build"}],"x":"06 · Math, Strings & Geometry Module 06 ⏱ 1 min read View Markdown source Goal: the math and string toolkit (your math-olympiad training helps here). · Time: 3–4 weeks Skip test Fast modular exponentiation and the modular inverse via Fermat (p prime). Precompute factorials and inverse factorials to answer nCr mod p in O(1). Sieve of Eratosthenes up to 10⁷. A smallest-prime-factor sieve for fast factorization. Polynomial rolling hash: compare substrings in O(1). Explain collision risk and double hashing. KMP prefix function or Z-function: find all occurrences of a pattern. Convex hull (monotone chain) using the cross product. Core ideas Number theory: gcd/lcm, extended Euclid, modular arithmetic mod 10⁹+7, modpow , inverses, sieve, factorization, Euler φ, CRT. Combinatorics: nCr mod p, stars and bars, inclusion–exclusion, Catalan numbers, counting with DP. Matrix exponentiation: linear recurrences in O(k³ log n). Probability/expectation: linearity of expectation, expected-value DP. Strings: hashing, KMP / prefix function , Z-function , trie (also the binary trie for max XOR), Manacher (palindromes), suffix array (advanced), Aho–Corasick (advanced). Geometry (light at IOI): use integer cross products for orientation, segment intersection, polygon area (shoelace), convex hull. Avoid floating point when possible. Game theory: Sprague–Grundy (like Nim from the math olympiad). Learn from ⭐ cp-algorithms : algebra, combinatorics, strings, geometry sections. ⭐ CSES: \"Mathematics\" and \"String Algorithms\" sections. USACO Guide: Gold/Platinum (math, strings) . Build Add modint , combinatorics, sieve, hashing, KMP, and convex hull snippets to your personal library ( kactl -style). Test each one."},{"t":"07 · IOI Skills & Advanced Topics","u":"informatics-olympiad/07-ioi-advanced.html","s":"Informatics Olympiad","h":[{"n":"Contest strategy (the highest-leverage skill)","id":"contest-strategy-the-highest-leverage-skill"},{"n":"Advanced techniques (learn after 01–06)","id":"advanced-techniques-learn-after-0106"},{"n":"Learn from","id":"learn-from"},{"n":"Build","id":"build"}],"x":"07 · IOI Skills & Advanced Topics Module 07 ⏱ 1 min read View Markdown source Goal: convert knowledge into IOI points, and learn the advanced techniques that separate medal colors. · Time: ongoing Contest strategy (the highest-leverage skill) First 20–30 min: read all three problems. Note every subtask and its points. Bank points: implement the easy subtasks of every problem before going deep. 3 × 40 points beats 1 × 100. Subtasks are hints: \"n ≤ 1000\", \"the tree is a line\", \"all values distinct\" each show a step toward the full solution. Think before coding. Prove (or at least stress-test) your idea. With live feedback at IOI, use it to verify. Keep the brute force for stress testing. Don't delete old working submissions. Problem types to practice: batch (function implementation), interactive (minimize the queries), communication (two programs), output-only , constructive . Advanced techniques (learn after 01–06) Trees: heavy-light decomposition (HLD), centroid decomposition , small-to-large merging (DSU on tree), virtual trees. Data structures: persistent segment tree, segment tree beats (rare), Li Chao tree, merge-sort tree, parallel binary search , offline divide & conquer (CDQ). Sqrt techniques: sqrt decomposition on queries, Mo's algorithm (with updates), heavy/light threshold splitting. Graphs: max flow / min cut / bipartite matching (rarer at IOI but useful), bridges/2-edge-connected components, Euler tours. DP: CHT, D&C optimization, the Aliens trick, DP over subsets / profiles. Learn from ⭐ Past IOI problems on oj.uz (judged with subtasks) plus past national/regional olympiads (APIO, BOI, CEOI, JOI). The best practice material. ⭐ USACO Guide: Platinum & Advanced . IOI official site : the syllabus, past tasks, and solutions. Codeforces (Div 1/2) and AtCoder (ARC/AGC) for contest practice. Build Simulate full IOI days: 3 past-IOI problems, 5 h, scored by subtasks on oj.uz. Log the scores."},{"t":"Informatics Olympiad Cheatsheet","u":"informatics-olympiad/cheatsheet.html","s":"Informatics Olympiad","h":[{"n":"Constraints → complexity (≈10⁸ ops/s) (→ 02)","id":"constraints-complexity-10⁸-opss-02"},{"n":"Snippets (→ 01–06)","id":"snippets-0106"},{"n":"Complexities (→ 03–04)","id":"complexities-0304"},{"n":"DP patterns (→ 05)","id":"dp-patterns-05"},{"n":"Pitfalls","id":"pitfalls"}],"x":"Informatics Olympiad Cheatsheet Cheatsheet ⏱ 2 min read View Markdown source One-page recap. If a line feels unfamiliar, open the module (→ NN). Constraints → complexity (≈10⁸ ops/s) (→ 02) n ≤ 12: n! · n ≤ 25: 2ⁿ·n · n ≤ 500: n³ · n ≤ 5000: n² · n ≤ 10⁶: n log n · bigger: n, log n, O(1) Snippets (→ 01–06) // binary search on answer: smallest x with ok(x) true (ok monotone false→true) ll lo = L - 1, hi = R; // ok(lo) false, ok(hi) true while (hi - lo > 1)  ll m = lo + (hi - lo) / 2; (ok(m) ? hi : lo) = m;  // DSU int p[N], sz[N]; int find(int x) return p[x]==x ? x : p[x]=find(p[x]);  bool unite(int a,int b) a=find(a); b=find(b); if(a==b) return false; if(sz[a]<sz[b]) swap(a,b); p[b]=a; sz[a]+=sz[b]; return true;  // Fenwick (1-indexed) void add(int i, ll v) for(; i<=n; i+=i&-i) bit[i]+=v;  ll sum(int i) ll s=0; for(; i>0; i-=i&-i) s+=bit[i]; return s;  // modpow ll pw(ll b, ll e, ll m) ll r=1; b%=m; while(e) if(e&1) r=r*b%m; b=b*b%m; e>>=1;  return r;  // inverse mod prime p: pw(a, p-2, p) // Dijkstra priority_queue<pair<ll,int>, vector<pair<ll,int>>, greater<>> pq; dist[s]=0; pq.push(0,s); while(!pq.empty()) auto [d,u]=pq.top(); pq.pop(); if(d>dist[u]) continue; for(auto [v,w]: g[u]) if(dist[u]+w<dist[v]) dist[v]=dist[u]+w; pq.push(dist[v],v);   Complexities (→ 03–04) Structure / algorithm Time DSU (path compr. + size) α(n) ≈ O(1) Fenwick / segment tree op O(log n) Sparse table build / query O(n log n) / O(1) BFS / DFS O(n + m) Dijkstra (binary heap) O((n + m) log n) Bellman–Ford / Floyd–Warshall O(nm) / O(n³) Kruskal O(m log m) Tarjan SCC / topo sort O(n + m) LCA binary lifting O(n log n) build, O(log n) query KMP / Z-function O(n + m) Sieve O(n log log n) DP patterns (→ 05) 0/1 knapsack: for item: for w = W..wt: dp[w] = max(dp[w], dp[w-wt]+val) (down!) · unbounded: up LIS: auto it = lower_bound(d.begin(), d.end(), x); if(it==d.end()) d.push_back(x); else *it = x; Bitmask TSP: dp[mask][v] · interval: dp[l][r] = min over k · tree: dp[v][0/1] from children Pitfalls int overflow (use 1LL* ) · uninitialized arrays across tests · recursion depth (~10⁵–10⁶) · endl flushes (use 'n' ) · comparing doubles · off-by-one in binary search · unordered_map hacks"},{"t":"Informatics Olympiad Resources","u":"informatics-olympiad/resources.html","s":"Informatics Olympiad","h":[{"n":"The core stack","id":"the-core-stack"},{"n":"References","id":"references"},{"n":"Practice & contests","id":"practice-contests"},{"n":"Video","id":"video"},{"n":"This repo","id":"this-repo"}],"x":"Informatics Olympiad Resources Resources ⏱ 1 min read View Markdown source ⭐ = the main resource · 💲 = paid · everything else is free. The core stack ⭐ USACO Guide : a structured curriculum from Bronze to Platinum, with problems per topic. Use it as the spine of modules 01–07. ⭐ CSES Problem Set (300+ classic problems) + Competitive Programmer's Handbook (free PDF, Laaksonen). ⭐ AtCoder Educational DP Contest : for module 05. ⭐ Past IOI and olympiad tasks on oj.uz : subtask-judged practice. References cp-algorithms : an encyclopedia of algorithms with proofs and code. KACTL : a well-tested code library (great for learning clean implementations). Guide to Competitive Programming , Laaksonen 💲 (the expanded handbook) · Algorithms , Erickson ( free ) for proofs. cppreference . Practice & contests Codeforces : weekly contests, plus the EDU section (segment tree, DSU, binary search courses). AtCoder : ABC (beginner–intermediate), ARC/AGC (hard, math-flavored). IOI official site : the syllabus and past tasks. Past APIO, BOI, CEOI, JOI, EGOI, and your national olympiad problems (most are on oj.uz). Video Errichto (YouTube): problem solving, DP, binary search. Colin Galen , William Lin (YouTube): contest walkthroughs and how they practice. This repo Interactive tools : graph search, segment tree, DP table, flashcards."},{"t":"Informatics Olympiad: Progress & Log","u":"informatics-olympiad/progress.html","s":"Informatics Olympiad","h":[{"n":"Key dates","id":"key-dates"},{"n":"Status board","id":"status-board"},{"n":"Rating log","id":"rating-log"},{"n":"IOI-day simulations","id":"ioi-day-simulations"},{"n":"Idea log (one line per problem)","id":"idea-log-one-line-per-problem"},{"n":"Session log (newest first)","id":"session-log-newest-first"}],"x":"Informatics Olympiad: Progress & Log Progress log ⏱ 1 min read View Markdown source Key dates Event Date Notes National olympiad round 1 National final / team selection IOI 2027 summer 2027 check eligibility Status board ⬜ not started · 🟨 in progress · ✅ done # Module Status CSES solved Other problems 01 C++ for CP ⬜ — template + stress script: ⬜ 02 Core techniques ⬜ / 03 Data structures ⬜ / 04 Graphs & trees ⬜ / 05 DP ⬜ / AtCoder DP: /26 06 Math, strings, geometry ⬜ / 07 IOI skills & advanced ⬜ — IOI days simulated: Rating log Date Platform Contest Solved Rating after IOI-day simulations Date Problems (year) P1 P2 P3 Total /300 Lesson Idea log (one line per problem) Problem Topic Key idea Session log (newest first)"},{"t":"IELTS Academic: Target Band 7.0–7.5","u":"ielts/index.html","s":"IELTS Academic","h":[{"n":"The test at a glance","id":"the-test-at-a-glance"},{"n":"The map","id":"the-map"},{"n":"Interactive tools → interactive/","id":"interactive-tools-interactive"},{"n":"10-week plan","id":"10-week-plan"}],"x":"IELTS Academic: Target Band 7.0–7.5 Roadmap ⏱ 2 min read View Markdown source Goal: an overall band 7.0–7.5 with no skill below 6.5 (a common university requirement; check yours). Time budget: ~8–10 weeks at 5–7 h/week, alongside the olympiads. The test at a glance Skill Time Format Band 7 needs (approx.) Listening ~30 min (+ transfer time on paper) 4 parts, 40 questions ~30/40 (7.5 ≈ 32/40) Reading (Academic) 60 min 3 long texts, 40 questions ~30/40 (7.5 ≈ 33/40) Writing 60 min Task 1 (≥150 words, data/process/map) + Task 2 (≥250 words, essay). Task 2 counts double. Band 7 on 4 criteria Speaking 11–14 min Part 1 interview · Part 2 a 2-min talk · Part 3 discussion Band 7 on 4 criteria Overall band = the average of the four, rounded to the nearest half band (x.25 → x.5, x.75 → next whole band). Raw-score conversions vary slightly between tests, so treat the numbers above as targets. Use the band calculator . The map # Module Time Focus 01 Test strategy & scoring 2 h what examiners reward, the band 7 descriptors 02 Listening 1–2 wk prediction, signpost words, spelling, distractors 03 Reading 2 wk skim/scan, T/F/NG, matching headings, time control 04 Writing Task 1 1–2 wk overview, key features, comparison language 05 Writing Task 2 2–3 wk position, paragraph structure, idea development 06 Speaking 2–3 wk fluency, extending answers, Part 2 structure 07 Vocabulary & grammar for band 7 ongoing collocations, academic words, complex sentences Writing and Speaking are where most strong students lose marks. Give them ~60% of your time. Interactive tools → interactive/ Band calculator: raw Listening/Reading scores → bands, and overall band rounding. Writing practice: timed Task 1/Task 2 with a live word count, a prompt bank, and a band-7 self-check. Speaking practice: random Part 1/2/3 questions, a 1-min prep + 2-min talk timer, and recording yourself (if your browser allows the microphone). Flashcards: academic collocations, linking language, Task 1 trend vocabulary. 10-week plan Weeks Focus 1 Diagnostic: one full official practice test, under real timing → find your weakest skill. Read module 01. 2–3 Listening + Reading technique (modules 02–03): 1 section a day, review every error. 3–6 Writing (modules 04–05): 2 Task 2 essays + 1 Task 1 per week. Self-check with the descriptors, and get feedback if you can. 4–8 Speaking (module 06): 15 min/day of out-loud practice, recording yourself 2–3×/week. all Vocabulary (module 07): 10 collocations/day in the flashcards. Read 1 Economist/Guardian/Scientific American article a day. 9–10 3–4 full mock tests under exam conditions. Book the test with a buffer for one retake before your deadlines. Support files: cheatsheet.md · resources.md · progress.md"},{"t":"01 · Test Strategy & Scoring","u":"ielts/01-test-strategy.html","s":"IELTS Academic","h":[{"n":"What examiners grade","id":"what-examiners-grade"},{"n":"General strategy","id":"general-strategy"},{"n":"Skip test","id":"skip-test"}],"x":"01 · Test Strategy & Scoring Module 01 ⏱ 2 min read View Markdown source Goal: know exactly what is graded so you practice the right things. · Time: ~2 h What examiners grade Writing (each criterion is 25%): | Criterion | Band 7 in plain words | |---|---| | Task Achievement (T1) / Task Response (T2) | covers every part of the task. T1: a clear overview of the main trends. T2: a clear position throughout , with main ideas extended and supported | | Coherence & Cohesion | logical organization, clear progression, a range of linking devices (not mechanical), one clear central idea per paragraph | | Lexical Resource | enough range for flexibility and precision, less common vocabulary , awareness of style and collocation , only occasional errors | | Grammatical Range & Accuracy | a variety of complex structures , frequent error-free sentences , good control | Speaking (each 25%): Fluency & Coherence · Lexical Resource · Grammatical Range & Accuracy · Pronunciation. Band 7 = speaks at length without noticeable effort, uses some less common and idiomatic vocabulary, has frequent error-free sentences, and is easy to understand. Read the official public band descriptors (links in resources ). Everything in modules 04–06 follows from them. General strategy Accuracy beats ambition. A simple sentence with no errors is better than a complex sentence that breaks. Band 7 needs frequent error-free sentences. Answer the question asked. Off-topic content caps Task Response no matter how good the English is. Never memorize whole answers. Examiners recognize them and penalize them. Memorize structures and collocations instead. Computer vs paper: the content is the same. Computer-delivered has faster results and typing (it helps if you type quickly). Do your mocks in the same format you'll take. One Skill Retake: some test centers let you retake a single skill. Check availability. It's useful if one skill drags you below 7. Skip test You can name the 4 Writing criteria and 4 Speaking criteria, and say what band 7 needs in each. You know how the overall band is rounded."},{"t":"02 · Listening","u":"ielts/02-listening.html","s":"IELTS Academic","h":[{"n":"The 4 parts","id":"the-4-parts"},{"n":"Core techniques","id":"core-techniques"},{"n":"Practice routine","id":"practice-routine"},{"n":"Learn from","id":"learn-from"}],"x":"02 · Listening Module 02 ⏱ 1 min read View Markdown source Goal: ≥ 30/40 consistently (band 7), 32+ for 7.5. · Time: 1–2 weeks, then maintenance The 4 parts A conversation, everyday social context (form/note completion). A monologue, everyday context (maps, MCQ, matching). A conversation, academic context (2–4 speakers discussing an assignment). A monologue, an academic lecture (note completion, the hardest). The audio plays once . Difficulty rises from Part 1 to Part 4. Core techniques Predict before listening: use the reading time to underline keywords, and predict the type of answer (number? noun? plural?) and grammar. Follow the signposts: \"however\", \"actually\", \"but then\", \"the main reason\", \"finally\". Answers often follow a correction. Distractors: speakers often say a wrong answer first and then correct it (\"Tuesday… no, sorry, Thursday\"). Wait for the final answer. Paraphrase: the question rarely uses the same words as the audio. Train your ear for synonyms. Spelling and word limits count: \"NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS\" means a 3-word answer is wrong. Practice spelling numbers, dates, and common words (accommodation, environment, necessary…). Don't get stuck: if you miss one, move on immediately or you lose the next three. Maps: orient yourself first (entrance, \"north\", \"opposite\", \"next to\", \"on your left\"). Practice routine Do one section under test conditions. Mark it. For every error, listen again with the transcript and label the cause: spelling / distractor / missed paraphrase / lost place / vocabulary . Log the causes in progress.md . Fix the most frequent one first. Daily ear training: 15 min of podcasts/lectures at natural speed (BBC 6 Minute English → TED → university lectures). Learn from ⭐ Cambridge IELTS Academic books (official past-style tests) 💲, the newest volumes first. IELTS official free practice tests . British Council: IELTS preparation (free practice and the IELTS Ready course)."},{"t":"03 · Reading (Academic)","u":"ielts/03-reading.html","s":"IELTS Academic","h":[{"n":"Format","id":"format"},{"n":"Question types & tactics","id":"question-types-tactics"},{"n":"Core techniques","id":"core-techniques"},{"n":"Practice routine","id":"practice-routine"},{"n":"Learn from","id":"learn-from"}],"x":"03 · Reading (Academic) Module 03 ⏱ 1 min read View Markdown source Goal: ≥ 30/40 (band 7), 33+ for 7.5. · Time: 2 weeks, then maintenance Format 3 long passages (journals, books, magazines; ~2,150–2,750 words total), 40 questions, 60 minutes, with no extra transfer time . Aim for ~17 / 20 / 23 min per passage, since passage 3 is the hardest. Question types & tactics Type Tactic True / False / Not Given (facts) · Yes / No / Not Given (writer's views) False = the text contradicts it. Not Given = the text says nothing about it. Don't use outside knowledge. Watch qualifiers: all/some/always/usually . Matching headings Read the headings first. Skim each paragraph's first and last sentences for its main idea , not a detail. Do it before the detail questions. Matching information (which paragraph contains…) Usually not in order and hardest. Do it last , after you know the text. Sentence / summary / note completion Respect the word limit. Answers usually come in text order. Check the grammar of the gap. Multiple choice Find the relevant part, then eliminate. The correct option is usually a paraphrase , while the wrong ones reuse text words. Diagram labeling / short answer Scan for keywords and numbers. Core techniques Skim (2–3 min): get the topic and paragraph structure. Scan : hunt for names, numbers, and keywords. Paraphrase awareness again: \"decline\" ↔ \"fall\", \"a growing number of\" ↔ \"increasingly\". Time discipline: never spend > 2 min on one question. Mark it and return. Read widely daily (academic register): The Economist , Scientific American , New Scientist , The Conversation . Your physics/math reading counts if it's in English! Practice routine Do one passage a day under time pressure. For each error, find the exact sentence in the text that gives the answer, and write why you chose wrong. Learn from ⭐ Cambridge IELTS Academic books 💲 + official free practice . IELTS Liz: Reading tips (free lessons per question type)."},{"t":"04 · Writing Task 1 (Academic)","u":"ielts/04-writing-task1.html","s":"IELTS Academic","h":[{"n":"The task","id":"the-task"},{"n":"The 4-paragraph structure","id":"the-4-paragraph-structure"},{"n":"Language toolkit","id":"language-toolkit"},{"n":"Traps","id":"traps"},{"n":"Learn from","id":"learn-from"}],"x":"04 · Writing Task 1 (Academic) Module 04 ⏱ 1 min read View Markdown source Goal: a reliable band 7 report in 20 minutes. · Time: 1–2 weeks 👉 Practice with the timer: Writing practice tool The task Describe visual information (line/bar/pie chart, table, process, map, or a mix) in ≥ 150 words in ~20 min . Report what's there. No opinions, no causes you invent. The 4-paragraph structure Introduction (1 sentence): paraphrase the prompt. \"The line graph illustrates how … changed between 2000 and 2020.\" Overview (2 sentences): the most important paragraph. The 2–3 biggest trends or features, with no numbers . \"Overall, X rose steadily, whereas Y declined. X was the highest throughout.\" Without a clear overview, you're capped at around band 5–6 on Task Achievement. Body 1: details + data for the first group of features. Body 2: details + data for the second group, with comparisons . Language toolkit Trends: rise / increase / grow / climb · fall / decrease / decline / drop · fluctuate · remain stable / level off · peak at · hit a low of. Degree: dramatically, sharply, significantly · steadily, gradually · slightly, marginally. Two patterns: \"X rose sharply\" (verb + adverb) ↔ \"There was a sharp rise in X\" (adjective + noun). Alternate them. Comparison: twice as high as , considerably more than , the largest share , by contrast , whereas , while . Approximation: just under a third , roughly half , approximately 40% . Processes: the passive voice + sequencers. \"First, the beans are harvested. After that, they are dried…\" Maps: was demolished / replaced by / converted into / extended to the north . Traps Listing every number with no grouping or comparison. Missing the overview, or putting numbers in it. Copying phrases from the prompt (paraphrase!). Writing < 150 words (penalized). Learn from ⭐ IELTS Liz: Writing Task 1 (free model answers). Cambridge IELTS books' model answers with examiner comments 💲."},{"t":"05 · Writing Task 2","u":"ielts/05-writing-task2.html","s":"IELTS Academic","h":[{"n":"Essay types (identify the type first!)","id":"essay-types-identify-the-type-first"},{"n":"Structure (4–5 paragraphs, ~270–300 words)","id":"structure-45-paragraphs-270300-words"},{"n":"Planning (5 min, the step that decides your band)","id":"planning-5-min-the-step-that-decides-your-band"},{"n":"Band 7 moves","id":"band-7-moves"},{"n":"Common topics (build idea banks)","id":"common-topics-build-idea-banks"},{"n":"Traps","id":"traps"},{"n":"Learn from","id":"learn-from"}],"x":"05 · Writing Task 2 Module 05 ⏱ 2 min read View Markdown source Goal: band 7+ essays in 40 minutes. This is worth twice Task 1. · Time: 2–3 weeks, then 2 essays/week 👉 Practice with the timer: Writing practice tool Essay types (identify the type first!) Type Prompt signal What you must do Opinion \"To what extent do you agree or disagree?\" A clear position in the intro, kept consistent to the end Discussion \"Discuss both views and give your own opinion.\" Both sides + your view Advantages / disadvantages \"Do the advantages outweigh the disadvantages?\" Both, plus which one outweighs Problem / solution (or cause / solution) \"What are the problems… What can be done?\" Answer both questions Two-part / direct questions Two separate questions Answer each one explicitly Structure (4–5 paragraphs, ~270–300 words) Intro (2–3 sentences): paraphrase the topic + thesis (your position / what the essay will do). Body 1 and Body 2: a topic sentence → explanation → example (specific, realistic) → link back to the question. One main idea per paragraph, developed deeply , beats three shallow ideas. Conclusion (2 sentences): restate the position. No new ideas. Planning (5 min, the step that decides your band) Underline the topic + the task. Decide your position. Write 2 main ideas, each with its explanation and example. Check: does every paragraph answer this question? Band 7 moves Position throughout: the same view in the intro, the body topic sentences, and the conclusion. Extend ideas: \"This is because…\" , \"As a result…\" , \"For instance, in countries such as…\" . Cohesion without overuse: vary the linkers, and use reference words ( this trend , such policies ). Less common vocabulary used precisely: exacerbate , mitigate , a deterrent , disproportionately . Collocations matter more than rare words. Complex grammar, accurately: conditionals, relative clauses, concessive clauses ( Although… ), passives, nominalization. Common topics (build idea banks) Education · technology · environment · health · work · globalization · government spending · crime · media · cities · science & research (your strong area!). Traps Memorized template intros (\"In this modern era…\"). Examiners discount them. Sitting on the fence in an opinion essay. Under 250 words. Spending 50 min on Task 2 and rushing Task 1. Learn from ⭐ IELTS Liz: Writing Task 2 + IELTS Advantage (Chris Pell) essay guides. ⭐ Feedback: have a teacher or a strong English user mark your essays against the descriptors. Without feedback, writing improves slowly. Cambridge IELTS model essays with examiner comments 💲."},{"t":"06 · Speaking","u":"ielts/06-speaking.html","s":"IELTS Academic","h":[{"n":"The 3 parts","id":"the-3-parts"},{"n":"Part 2 structure (works for any cue card)","id":"part-2-structure-works-for-any-cue-card"},{"n":"Band 7 moves","id":"band-7-moves"},{"n":"Practice routine","id":"practice-routine"},{"n":"Learn from","id":"learn-from"}],"x":"06 · Speaking Module 06 ⏱ 2 min read View Markdown source Goal: band 7: speak at length, naturally, with flexible vocabulary. · Time: 15 min/day, 2–3 weeks focused 👉 Practice: Speaking practice tool (random questions, timers, recording) The 3 parts Part Time What happens How to answer 1 4–5 min Familiar topics (home, studies, hobbies…) 2–3 sentences: answer → reason → example/detail . Don't give one-word answers, and don't give speeches. 2 1 min prep + 1–2 min talk A cue card (\"Describe a time when…\") Speak the full 2 minutes . Use the prep minute to jot 4–5 keywords, not sentences. 3 4–5 min Abstract discussion linked to Part 2 Opinion → reason → example → (other side / future) . Talk about society in general, not only you. Part 2 structure (works for any cue card) Set the scene: what/who/when/where (20 s). The story/details: what happened, following the bullet points (60 s). Feelings & reflection: why it mattered, how you feel now (30 s). Filler you can always add: comparisons (\"Compared with…\"), a hypothetical (\"If I could do it again, I'd…\"). Band 7 moves Fluency over perfection. Self-correct quickly. Use natural fillers ( \"Well…\" , \"Let me think…\" , \"That's an interesting question…\" ) rather than silence. Extend: because… / for example… / on the other hand… / I suppose it depends on… Vocabulary: topic-specific words + some idioms/phrasal verbs used naturally ( \"I was over the moon\" , \"it boils down to\" ). Paraphrase when you don't know a word. Grammar range: mix the tenses (past stories, present perfect experiences, conditionals for hypotheticals, future for plans). Pronunciation: clear word stress, sentence stress, and intonation. Chunk your speech. Your accent is fine; clarity is what counts. Practice routine Daily: 3 random Part 1 questions + 1 Part 2 card (timed) + 2 Part 3 questions, out loud . 2–3×/week: record a Part 2, listen back, and note 3 issues (hesitations, repeated words, grammar slips). Re-record once. Weekly: a mock interview with a partner/teacher, ideally someone who'll interrupt and ask follow-ups. Learn from ⭐ IELTS Liz: Speaking (question lists + model answers). E2 IELTS and IELTS Advantage (YouTube speaking strategies + mock tests). Official speaking test videos on the IELTS and British Council sites."},{"t":"07 · Vocabulary & Grammar for Band 7","u":"ielts/07-vocab-grammar.html","s":"IELTS Academic","h":[{"n":"Vocabulary: learn collocations, not lists","id":"vocabulary-learn-collocations-not-lists"},{"n":"Grammar: range + accuracy","id":"grammar-range-accuracy"},{"n":"Learn from","id":"learn-from"}],"x":"07 · Vocabulary & Grammar for Band 7 Module 07 ⏱ 2 min read View Markdown source Goal: the language range band 7 requires, used accurately. · Time: 10–15 min/day, ongoing 👉 Drill: IELTS flashcards Vocabulary: learn collocations, not lists Band 7's Lexical Resource rewards collocation and precision , not rare words. \"Make a decision\" (✓) vs \"do a decision\" (✗). Academic Word List (AWL): ~570 word families that appear across academic texts (analyze, significant, factor, benefit, policy…). Learn them in phrases . Topic banks: for each common Task 2 / Part 3 topic, 15–20 collocations, e.g. Environment: curb emissions · renewable energy sources · carbon footprint · tackle climate change · sustainable development Education: rote learning · critical thinking skills · tertiary education · a well-rounded curriculum Technology: a double-edged sword · automation of jobs · digital literacy · data privacy concerns Paraphrasing skill: synonyms + changing the word form ( grow → growth , economic → economy ). It's essential in all four skills. Method: keep a vocabulary log (in progress.md or the flashcards). Each entry is a phrase + example sentence . Review it with spaced repetition. Grammar: range + accuracy Structure Example Complex sentences (subordination) Although remote work offers flexibility, it can blur the line between work and home life. Relative clauses Students who study abroad often become more independent. Conditionals (1st, 2nd, 3rd, mixed) If governments invested more in public transport, congestion would fall. Passive voice The data were collected over a decade. (Task 1 processes) Nominalization The rapid expansion of cities has led to… (instead of \"cities expanded fast\") Cleft sentences What concerns many experts is the pace of change. Modal verbs for hedging This may/might/could lead to… · It is likely that … Accuracy checklist (the most common errors at band 6–6.5): articles ( a/the/∅ ), subject–verb agreement, countable/uncountable ( information , research , advice have no plural), comma splices, tense consistency, prepositions ( interested in , responsible for ). Learn from ⭐ English Collocations in Use (Advanced) , McCarthy & O'Dell 💲. ⭐ Advanced Grammar in Use , Hewings 💲 (or English Grammar in Use , Murphy, for gaps). Academic Word List (Coxhead) : the official list. Oxford Learner's Dictionaries : example sentences and collocations. Ozdic : a free collocation dictionary."},{"t":"IELTS Cheatsheet","u":"ielts/cheatsheet.html","s":"IELTS Academic","h":[{"n":"Score targets (approx.)","id":"score-targets-approx"},{"n":"Timing","id":"timing"},{"n":"Listening & Reading","id":"listening-reading"},{"n":"Writing Task 1 frame","id":"writing-task-1-frame"},{"n":"Writing Task 2 frame","id":"writing-task-2-frame"},{"n":"Linking (vary them!)","id":"linking-vary-them"},{"n":"Speaking","id":"speaking"},{"n":"Accuracy checklist","id":"accuracy-checklist"}],"x":"IELTS Cheatsheet Cheatsheet ⏱ 2 min read View Markdown source Skim this before every practice session and on test day. Score targets (approx.) Band Listening /40 Academic Reading /40 6.5 26–29 27–29 7.0 30–31 30–32 7.5 32–34 33–34 8.0 35–36 35–36 Overall = the average of 4, rounded to the nearest 0.5 (x.25 ↑ to x.5, x.75 ↑ to the next whole band). Timing Listening ~30 min · Reading 60 min (no transfer time: ~17/20/23 min) · Writing 20 min T1 + 40 min T2 · Speaking 11–14 min Listening & Reading Predict the answer type · watch for corrections (\"…no, actually…\") · respect the word limits · spelling counts F = contradicts · NG = not mentioned · do matching headings first, matching information last Writing Task 1 frame The [chart] illustrates/compares [what] in [where] between [when]. Overall, [main trend 1], while [main trend 2]. [Biggest/smallest feature]. ← no numbers Body 1: group A + data + comparison · 4. Body 2: group B + data + comparison Trend verbs: rise, climb, surge, decline, dip, plummet, fluctuate, plateau, peak at · adverbs: sharply, steadily, marginally Writing Task 2 frame Intro: paraphrase + thesis ( \"I strongly agree that… because…\" ) Body: topic sentence → explain → example → link back (×2) Conclusion: \"In conclusion, …\" restate the position + the main reasons Plan 5 min · write 30 min · check 5 min (articles, plurals, agreement, tense) Linking (vary them!) Addition: Furthermore, Moreover, In addition · Contrast: However, Nevertheless, Whereas, Although · Cause/effect: Consequently, As a result, This leads to · Example: For instance, A case in point is · Concession: Admittedly, … Even so, … Speaking Part 1: answer + reason + example (2–3 sentences) Part 2: scene → story → feelings → reflection. Talk the full 2 minutes. Part 3: opinion → reason → example → other side/future Buying time: \"That's an interesting question…\" , \"I've never really thought about it, but…\" Hedging: \"I'd say…\" , \"It depends on…\" , \"To some extent…\" Accuracy checklist articles · subject–verb agreement · uncountables (information, advice, research) · comma splices · prepositions · consistent tense · ≥150 / ≥250 words"},{"t":"IELTS Resources","u":"ielts/resources.html","s":"IELTS Academic","h":[{"n":"Official (use these first; they're the most accurate)","id":"official-use-these-first-theyre-the-most-accurate"},{"n":"Teachers & free lessons","id":"teachers-free-lessons"},{"n":"Language building","id":"language-building"},{"n":"Daily input (Reading & Listening)","id":"daily-input-reading-listening"},{"n":"Practice partners & feedback","id":"practice-partners-feedback"}],"x":"IELTS Resources Resources ⏱ 1 min read View Markdown source ⭐ = the main resource · 💲 = paid · everything else is free. Official (use these first; they're the most accurate) ⭐ IELTS official site (ielts.org) : test format, free sample tests, and the public band descriptors for Writing and Speaking. ⭐ Cambridge IELTS Academic books (numbered volumes, each with 4 full tests, answer keys, transcripts, and examiner-marked model answers) 💲. The newest volumes are closest to the current test. British Council: Take IELTS : free practice tests and the IELTS Ready preparation course. IDP IELTS : free practice materials and webinars (IDP also runs tests in many countries). Teachers & free lessons ⭐ IELTS Liz : free lessons for every question type, model answers, and topic lists. IELTS Advantage (Chris Pell) : writing structures and band-7+ strategy. E2 IELTS (YouTube) : test walkthroughs and speaking mocks. Language building English Collocations in Use: Advanced 💲 · Advanced Grammar in Use 💲 · Academic Word List . Oxford Learner's Dictionaries · Ozdic collocations . Daily input (Reading & Listening) Read: The Economist , The Guardian , Scientific American , New Scientist , The Conversation . Listen: BBC Learning English ( 6 Minute English ), BBC In Our Time , TED talks, university lecture podcasts. Practice partners & feedback A teacher or a study partner for Writing feedback and Speaking mocks. This matters more than any book. This repo's interactive tools : band calculator, writing timer, speaking practice, flashcards."},{"t":"IELTS: Progress & Log","u":"ielts/progress.html","s":"IELTS Academic","h":[{"n":"Key dates","id":"key-dates"},{"n":"Target","id":"target"},{"n":"Mock test log","id":"mock-test-log"},{"n":"Error log (Listening/Reading)","id":"error-log-listeningreading"},{"n":"Writing log","id":"writing-log"},{"n":"Speaking log","id":"speaking-log"},{"n":"Vocabulary log (phrase + example)","id":"vocabulary-log-phrase-example"}],"x":"IELTS: Progress & Log Progress log ⏱ 1 min read View Markdown source Key dates Item Date University application deadlines (IELTS needed by) Test booked Backup / retake date Target Overall 7.0–7.5 , no skill below 6.5 (check your universities' exact requirements). Mock test log Date Source L raw → band R raw → band W (est.) S (est.) Overall Weakest diagnostic Error log (Listening/Reading) Date Skill Question type Cause (spelling / distractor / paraphrase / time / vocab) Writing log Date Task Prompt (short) Words Time TR CC LR GRA Main fix for next time T2 Speaking log Date Part Topic Recorded? 3 issues heard 2 Vocabulary log (phrase + example) Phrase Example sentence Topic curb emissions Governments must curb emissions from heavy industry. environment"},{"t":"Deep Learning: Beginner → Advanced","u":"deep-learning/index.html","s":"Deep Learning","h":[{"n":"The map","id":"the-map"},{"n":"Fast track (if you want to see a GPT in under a month)","id":"fast-track-if-you-want-to-see-a-gpt-in-under-a-month"},{"n":"Interactive tools → interactive/","id":"interactive-tools-interactive"},{"n":"Support files","id":"support-files"},{"n":"Hardware","id":"hardware"},{"n":"Study protocol (per module)","id":"study-protocol-per-module"}],"x":"Deep Learning: Beginner → Advanced Roadmap ⏱ 4 min read View Markdown source Profile this is tuned for: high-school senior, strong in math and physics, basic Python and C++ STL, highly self-directed. What that means for the plan: - Math is a strength, so the math stays short. Module 01 covers only the DL-specific math you probably haven't seen yet: matrix calculus, probability for ML, and information theory. It skips the high-school parts. - Physics intuition will pay off. Gradient descent is a ball rolling on a potential. Softmax is the Boltzmann distribution. Diffusion models run Langevin dynamics. Energy-based models, Hamiltonian Monte Carlo, and physics-informed nets all lean on it. These links are pointed out where they show up (🔭). - C++ is an advantage later. GPU/CUDA programming (module 12) is where serious performance work happens, and C++ gets you there faster than most people. - Code-first from day one. You learn DL by building it, so every module ends with a project. 💡 Key idea · How each chapter works Every module is a self-contained chapter: you can learn everything from the page without opening another source. Each section follows the same flow: 📖 definitions → 🧮 formulas → ✍️ worked examples → 🎛️ try it (an interactive tool, right where it helps) → ▶️ videos that play inside the page → 📚 optional books and courses for going deeper. Every chapter starts with a skip test and ends with practice questions (answers hidden), a project to build , common traps , and a done when checklist. The map PHASE 1 · FOUNDATIONS PHASE 2 · CORE DL PHASE 3 · MODERN DL PHASE 4 · ADVANCED ┌──────────────────────┐ ┌──────────────────────────┐ ┌────────────────────────┐ ┌────────────────────────┐ │ 01 Math for DL │──▶│ 04 Neural nets (scratch) │──▶│ 08 Transformers │──▶│ 12 Frontiers: │ │ 02 Python + NumPy │ │ 05 PyTorch + training │ │ 09 LLMs │ │ theory, SSL, GNNs, │ │ 03 ML fundamentals │ │ 06 CNNs + vision │ │ 10 Generative models │ │ interpretability, │ └──────────────────────┘ │ 07 Sequences + RNNs │ │ 11 Reinforcement learn. │ │ sci-ML, GPU/systems │ └──────────────────────────┘ └────────────────────────┘ └────────────────────────┘ # Module Phase Est. time* You can… when done 01 Math for DL 1 15–25 h differentiate through matrices, reason with probability and likelihood 02 Python, NumPy & tools 1 8–12 h write vectorized NumPy, use git and Jupyter comfortably 03 ML fundamentals 1 20–30 h train and evaluate classical models, diagnose over/underfitting 04 Neural nets from scratch 2 15–20 h build backprop and a tiny autograd engine yourself 05 PyTorch & training practice 2 20–30 h train any model reliably and debug it when it fails 06 CNNs & computer vision 2 20–25 h build and fine-tune ResNet-style models 07 Sequences, embeddings & RNNs 2 10–15 h explain why attention was invented 08 Transformers 3 20–30 h write a GPT from a blank file 09 Large language models 3 25–40 h pretrain, fine-tune, and evaluate LMs, and understand the frontier 10 Generative models 3 25–35 h derive and implement a VAE and a diffusion model 11 Reinforcement learning 3 25–35 h implement DQN and PPO, and connect RL to RLHF 12 Advanced frontiers 4 open-ended read papers and pick a specialization *Times assume you're strong at math. At ~10 h/week , phases 1–3 take about 6–9 months . Skip tests can cut that a lot. Order rules: 01→02→03→04→05 are sequential. After 05, module 06 (vision) and module 07 (sequences) can be done in either order. 08 needs 07. 09 needs 08. 10 and 11 need only 05, so do them whenever they excite you. Fast track (if you want to see a GPT in under a month) 01 (matrix calculus section only) → 04 → 05 (first half) → 08. Come back for the rest later. Interactive tools → interactive/ Matrix as a map: drag a 2×2 matrix; see det, rank, eigenvectors, and the SVD as rotate → scale → rotate (module 01). Broadcasting lab: right-align shapes, see stretch vs error and matmul rules, plus a predict-the-shape quiz (module 02). Generalization lab: under/overfitting with train/val curves and ridge λ; thresholds, confusion matrix, ROC and PR curves (module 03). Backprop step-through: run a computational graph forward and backward node by node, gradient-check it, take a GD step (module 04). Training doctor: diagnose 11 realistic loss-curve failure modes (module 05). LR schedule designer: warmup + cosine/linear/WSD/step/inverse-√t, with generated PyTorch code (module 05). Normalization explorer: which values share μ, σ in BatchNorm, LayerNorm, RMSNorm, InstanceNorm, GroupNorm (modules 05, 06, 08). Optimizer landscape: SGD vs Momentum vs Adam on 2D loss surfaces (modules 01, 05). Neural net playground: an MLP trained live with from-scratch backprop. Decision boundaries, overfitting, vanishing gradients (modules 03–05). Attention & sampling: drag q/k vectors, see the √d scaling and the causal mask, try temperature/top-k/top-p (modules 08–09). Foundations quiz: 27 auto-graded questions on modules 01–04, scored per module. Flashcards: ~65 core facts with spaced repetition. Support files cheatsheet.md : every key formula on one page. Use it for recaps. resources.md : the full curated list, plus a paper reading list in order. progress.md : checklists and your learning log. Update it every session. Hardware You don't need a GPU to start. Modules 01–07 run on a laptop CPU. For 08 and beyond, use free GPUs: Google Colab and Kaggle Notebooks (~30 GPU-h/week free). Rent a cloud GPU only for the large projects. Study protocol (per module) Take the Skip test (5 min). Can you do it all? Skip to the next module. Read Core ideas (10 min) so you know where you're headed. Work through the ⭐ resource. Code along. Don't just watch. Do the Build . Get stuck, then fix it yourself. That's where the learning happens. Re-take the skip test. Write a log entry in progress.md . Spaced review: skim Core ideas again after 1 week and after 1 month (≈5 min each)."},{"t":"01 · Math for Deep Learning","u":"deep-learning/01-math-for-dl.html","s":"Deep Learning","h":[{"n":"Skip test","id":"skip-test"},{"n":"1 · Vectors: data as arrows","id":"1-vectors-data-as-arrows"},{"n":"2 · Matrices: functions that move space","id":"2-matrices-functions-that-move-space"},{"n":"3 · Rank, determinant and solving equations","id":"3-rank-determinant-and-solving-equations"},{"n":"4 · Eigenvectors and the SVD","id":"4-eigenvectors-and-the-svd"},{"n":"5 · Derivatives, gradients and Jacobians","id":"5-derivatives-gradients-and-jacobians"},{"n":"6 · Optimization: gradient descent","id":"6-optimization-gradient-descent"},{"n":"7 · Probability and statistics","id":"7-probability-and-statistics"},{"n":"8 · Information theory: entropy, cross-entropy, KL","id":"8-information-theory-entropy-cross-entropy-kl"},{"n":"Practice (answers hidden)","id":"practice-answers-hidden"},{"n":"Build","id":"build"},{"n":"Traps","id":"traps"},{"n":"Go deeper (all optional)","id":"go-deeper-all-optional"},{"n":"Done when","id":"done-when"}],"x":"01 · Math for Deep Learning Module 01 ⏱ 21 min read View Markdown source Goal: the math DL actually uses and nothing more. · Time: 15–25 h · Needs: high-school calculus 🎯 What you'll learn By the end of this chapter you can: read a matrix as a map that moves space, and explain rank, determinant, eigenvectors and the SVD in pictures; take gradients of vector and matrix expressions and check them with shapes; explain why gradient descent works and exactly when it blows up; explain why almost every loss in DL is a negative log-likelihood , and what entropy, cross-entropy and KL divergence measure. How to use this chapter: each section teaches one idea in the same order: explanation → 📖 definitions → 🧮 formulas → ✍️ example → 🎛️ try it → 📚 optional video/reading. Everything you need is on this page; the videos and books are there only if you want a second explanation. Skip test Explain matrix multiplication as a composition of linear maps, and what eigenvectors/SVD mean geometrically. For f(x) = x^top A x, give nabla f. For L = \\|Xw-y\\|^2, give nabla_mathbf w L and solve nabla L = 0. Explain the chain rule for a composition f(g(h(x))) with vectors, using Jacobians. Derive the MLE of a Gaussian's mean. Explain why minimizing MSE = maximizing Gaussian likelihood. Define entropy, cross-entropy, and KL divergence, and say why D_KL ge 0. Explain why gradient descent with too large a step size diverges on f(x)=tfraca2x^2, and give the exact threshold. Say why a rotation matrix has singular values 1, 1 but no real eigenvectors, and why softmax(mathbf z - max_j z_j) is used in code. 🎯 Not sure? Take the auto-graded Foundations Quiz (filter by module) before deciding. 1 · Vectors: data as arrows In deep learning, everything is a vector : an image is a long list of pixel values, a word is a list of 768 numbers (its embedding ), and the network's parameters are one giant vector. So we need two views of a vector at once: a list of numbers mathbf x = (x_1, dots, x_d), which is how the computer stores it, and an arrow (or point) in d-dimensional space , which is how you reason about it. You can't picture 768 dimensions, but everything you see in 2D and 3D (lengths, angles, projections) works the same way in high dimensions. Draw it in 2D, then trust the algebra. 📖 Definition · Vector, dimension A vector mathbf x in mathbb R^d is an ordered list of d real numbers. d is its dimension . Vectors are added component by component, (mathbf x+mathbf y)_i = x_i + y_i, and scaled by a number, (cmathbf x)_i = c\\,x_i. 📖 Definition · Dot product The dot product of two vectors of the same dimension multiplies matching entries and adds them up: mathbf acdotmathbf b = mathbf a^topmathbf b = sum_i=1^d a_i b_i Geometrically it equals \\|mathbf a\\|\\,\\|mathbf b\\|costheta, where theta is the angle between the arrows. It is large and positive when they point the same way, zero when they are perpendicular ( orthogonal ), and negative when they point apart. 📖 Definition · Norms (length) A norm measures the size of a vector. L2 (Euclidean) norm: \\|mathbf x\\|_2 = sqrtsum_i x_i^2 = sqrtmathbf x^topmathbf x, the ordinary length of the arrow. L1 norm: \\|mathbf x\\|_1 = sum_i |x_i|. Penalizing it pushes entries to exactly zero (sparsity). Frobenius norm of a matrix: \\|A\\|_F = sqrtsum_ij A_ij^2, the L2 norm of all entries. A unit vector has length 1. Any nonzero mathbf x becomes one after dividing by its length: hatmathbf x = mathbf x/\\|mathbf x\\|. 🧮 Formula · Cosine similarity costheta = fracmathbf acdotmathbf b\\|mathbf a\\|\\,\\|mathbf b\\| in [-1, 1] This is how embeddings are compared (\"are these two sentences about the same thing?\"), and the dot product mathbf qcdotmathbf k is the core of attention in transformers (module 08). ✍️ Worked example · Dot product and angle mathbf a=(3,4), mathbf b=(4,3). Then mathbf acdotmathbf b = 12+12 = 24, \\|mathbf a\\|=\\|mathbf b\\|=5, so costheta = 24/25 = 0.96 and thetaapprox 16°: almost the same direction. For mathbf c=(-4,3): mathbf acdotmathbf c = -12+12 = 0, so mathbf aperpmathbf c. 💡 Key idea · Shapes are everything In code, a batch of N examples with d features each is stored as a matrix X in mathbb R^Ntimes d: one row per example . Most bugs in DL are shape bugs, so get into the habit of writing the shape next to every symbol. 2 · Matrices: functions that move space A matrix is not just a table of numbers. A matrix A in mathbb R^mtimes n is a function : it takes a vector mathbf xinmathbb R^n and returns Amathbf xinmathbb R^m. These functions are special: they keep straight lines straight and keep the origin fixed. Such functions are called linear maps . The single most useful fact: the columns of A tell you where the basis vectors land. In 2D, the first column is where hatmathbf e_1=(1,0) goes and the second column is where hatmathbf e_2=(0,1) goes. Since every vector is a combination x_1hatmathbf e_1 + x_2hatmathbf e_2, its image is the same combination of the columns: Amathbf x = x_1\\,mathbf a_1 + x_2\\,mathbf a_2 qquad(mathbf a_j = column  j) 📖 Definition · Linear map A function f is linear if f(mathbf x+mathbf y) = f(mathbf x)+f(mathbf y) and f(cmathbf x) = c f(mathbf x). Every linear map from mathbb R^n to mathbb R^m can be written as multiplication by an mtimes n matrix, and vice versa. 🧮 Formula · Matrix–vector and matrix–matrix product (Amathbf x)_i = sum_j A_ijx_j qquad (AB)_ij = sum_k A_ikB_kj Shapes: (mtimes n)(ntimes p) = (mtimes p). The inner sizes must match. Meaning: AB is the map \"apply B first, then A\" (composition, read right to left). That's why AB ne BA in general: rotating then stretching is not the same as stretching then rotating. ✍️ Worked example · A shear, then a scaling B=beginpmatrix1&1\\\\0&1endpmatrix (shear: pushes the top sideways), A=beginpmatrix2&0\\\\0&1endpmatrix (stretch x by 2). AB=beginpmatrix2&2\\\\0&1endpmatrix but BA=beginpmatrix2&1\\\\0&1endpmatrix. Check on hatmathbf e_2=(0,1): shear sends it to (1,1) and the stretch then gives (2,1), the second column of AB. ✓ 📖 Definition · Transpose, identity, inverse The transpose A^top swaps rows and columns: (A^top)_ij = A_ji. Rule: (AB)^top = B^top A^top. The identity I has 1s on the diagonal and 0 elsewhere; Imathbf x = mathbf x. A square matrix A is invertible if there is A^-1 with A^-1A = AA^-1 = I: the map can be undone. (AB)^-1 = B^-1A^-1. 💡 Key idea · A neural-network layer is a matrix (plus a bend) A dense layer computes mathbf h = phi(Wmathbf x + mathbf b): a linear map W, a shift mathbf b, then a nonlinear function phi applied to each entry. Stacking layers without phi would collapse to a single matrix (W_2W_1 is just another matrix), which is why the nonlinearity is essential (module 04). 🎛️ Try it · Matrix as a Map Drag the two column vectors and watch the grid deform. Try: a rotation, a shear, a matrix whose columns are parallel (the plane collapses to a line), and swapping the columns (orientation flips, so the determinant turns negative). ▶ Playlist · Essence of Linear Algebra (full series) ▶️ Essence of Linear Algebra (full series) · 3Blue1Brown · ~3 h, chapters 1–4 cover this section (plays here; or open on YouTube) 3 · Rank, determinant and solving equations When does a matrix lose information? Look at what it does to space. 📖 Definition · Span, linear independence The span of some vectors is everything you can build from them with scaled sums. Vectors are linearly independent if none of them is a combination of the others, i.e. c_1mathbf v_1+dots+c_kmathbf v_k = mathbf 0 only when every c_i=0. 📖 Definition · Column space, null space, rank Column space (image) of A: the span of its columns = every possible output Amathbf x. Null space (kernel): every mathbf x with Amathbf x = mathbf 0, the directions that get squashed to nothing. Rank: the dimension of the column space = the number of linearly independent columns. An mtimes n matrix has full rank if its rank is min(m,n). Otherwise it is rank-deficient : some direction is crushed, and that information is gone for good. 🧮 Formula · Rank–nullity theorem For Ainmathbb R^mtimes n: rank(A) + dimnull(A) = n Every input dimension either survives (counts toward rank) or is crushed (counts toward the null space). 📖 Definition · Determinant For a square matrix, det A is the factor by which A scales area (2D) or volume (3D+). A negative sign means the map flips orientation (like a mirror). detbeginpmatrixa&b\\c&dendpmatrix = ad-bc qquad det(AB)=det A\\,det B det A = 0 exactly when A squashes space into a lower dimension, i.e. when A is not invertible . 🧮 Formula · 2×2 inverse beginpmatrixa&b\\c&dendpmatrix^-1 = frac1ad-bcbeginpmatrixd&-b\\\\-c&aendpmatrix In code you almost never form A^-1; you call np.linalg.solve(A, b) to solve Amathbf x=mathbf b, which is faster and more accurate. ✍️ Worked example · A rank-1 matrix A=beginpmatrix1&2\\\\2&4endpmatrix. The second column is twice the first, so the column space is the line through (1,2): rank 1. det A = 4-4 = 0. The null space is the line through (2,-1), because A(2,-1)^top = (0,0)^top. Rank–nullity: 1 + 1 = 2 ✓. 💡 Key idea · Why DL cares about rank Low-rank structure is everywhere: LoRA fine-tunes a big model by learning a weight update Delta W = BA that is a product of two thin matrices (rank r ll d), and compression methods replace a matrix by a low-rank approximation (section 4). 📚 Go deeper (optional) 3Blue1Brown lessons: Linear algebra topic page , especially \" Inverse matrices, column space and null space \". ⭐ Mathematics for Machine Learning , ch. 2 (free PDF). 4 · Eigenvectors and the SVD Most vectors change direction when a matrix acts on them. A few special ones don't. 📖 Definition · Eigenvector, eigenvalue A nonzero vector mathbf v is an eigenvector of a square matrix A if Amathbf v = lambdamathbf v for some number lambda, its eigenvalue . The map only stretches mathbf v (by lambda; flipping it if lambda<0) without turning it. Eigenvalues are the roots of det(A-lambda I) = 0. ✍️ Worked example · Eigenvalues of a 2×2 matrix A=beginpmatrix2&1\\\\1&2endpmatrix. det(A-lambda I) = (2-lambda)^2-1 = 0 Rightarrow lambda = 3 or 1. For lambda=3: (A-3I)mathbf v = 0 gives mathbf v = (1,1). For lambda=1: mathbf v=(1,-1). They are perpendicular, because A is symmetric (next box). A rotation by 90° has no real eigenvectors: every arrow turns. A shear has only one eigen-direction (the one it slides along). 🧮 Formula · Spectral theorem (symmetric matrices) If A = A^top, it has real eigenvalues and orthogonal eigenvectors, and A = QLambda Q^top where the columns of Q are unit eigenvectors and Lambda is diagonal with the eigenvalues. In words: a symmetric matrix just stretches space along perpendicular axes. Covariance matrices and Hessians (section 5) are symmetric. 📖 Definition · Positive (semi-)definite A symmetric A is positive semi-definite (Asucceq 0) if mathbf x^top Amathbf x ge 0 for all mathbf x, equivalently all eigenvalues ge 0. Positive definite (Asucc0) means strictly >0. Covariance matrices are always PSD. Eigenvectors only exist for square matrices, and even then may be missing or not perpendicular. The singular value decomposition fixes all of that: it works for every matrix. 🧮 Formula · Singular value decomposition (SVD) Any Ainmathbb R^mtimes n can be written A = USigma V^top - V (ntimes n, orthogonal): a rotation/reflection of the input space, - Sigma (mtimes n, diagonal): stretches by the singular values sigma_1gesigma_2gedotsge0, - U (mtimes m, orthogonal): a rotation/reflection of the output space. Picture: rotate → scale each axis → rotate. The unit circle becomes an ellipse whose semi-axes have lengths sigma_i. Useful facts: rank = number of nonzero sigma_i · |det A| = prod_isigma_i (square A) · \\|A\\|_2 = sigma_max · sigma_i(A)^2 are the eigenvalues of A^top A. 📖 Definition · Condition number kappa(A) = sigma_max/sigma_min. It measures how \"stretched\" the ellipse is. A large kappa (ill-conditioned) means small input errors can become large output errors, and gradient descent becomes slow (section 6). 🧮 Formula · Best low-rank approximation (Eckart–Young) Keep only the top k singular values: A_k = sum_i=1^ksigma_imathbf u_imathbf v_i^top. No other rank-k matrix is closer to A (in Frobenius or spectral norm). The error is \\|A-A_k\\|_F = sqrtsum_i>ksigma_i^2. PCA is exactly this, applied to centered data X: the top right-singular vectors mathbf v_i are the directions of greatest variance. ⚠️ Common mistake · Eigenvectors ≠ singular vectors They coincide only for symmetric positive semi-definite matrices. A rotation has singular values 1,1 (it doesn't stretch anything) but no real eigenvectors (it turns everything). 🎛️ Try it · Matrix as a Map Turn on the eigenvector and SVD overlays. Make the matrix symmetric and watch the eigenvectors become perpendicular; make it a rotation and watch them disappear while the SVD still works. 📚 Go deeper (optional) 3Blue1Brown: \" Eigenvectors and eigenvalues \" (chapter 14 of the series above). ⭐ Mathematics for Machine Learning , ch. 4 (matrix decompositions) and ch. 10 (PCA). Optional depth: MIT 18.06, Gilbert Strang . 5 · Derivatives, gradients and Jacobians Training a network means nudging millions of parameters to reduce a loss. To know which way to nudge, we need derivatives of a scalar (the loss) with respect to vectors and matrices (the parameters). 📖 Definition · Partial derivative and gradient For f:mathbb R^dtomathbb R, the partial derivative partial f/partial x_i is the ordinary derivative in x_i with every other variable held fixed. The gradient collects them into a vector of the same shape as mathbf x: nabla f(mathbf x) = left(fracpartial fpartial x_1,dots,fracpartial fpartial x_dright) It points in the direction of steepest increase , its length is the slope in that direction, and it is perpendicular to the level sets (contour lines) of f. 📖 Definition · Jacobian For a vector-valued mathbf f:mathbb R^ntomathbb R^m, the Jacobian is the mtimes n matrix of all partial derivatives: J_ij = fracpartial f_ipartial x_j It is the best linear approximation of mathbf f near a point: mathbf f(mathbf x+boldsymboldelta)approxmathbf f(mathbf x)+Jboldsymboldelta. 🧮 Formula · Chain rule with vectors For a composition mathbf fcircmathbf g: J_mathbf fcircmathbf g(mathbf x) = J_mathbf f(mathbf g(mathbf x))\\;J_mathbf g(mathbf x) Jacobians multiply, just like the 1D rule (fcirc g)' = f'(g(x))\\,g'(x). A network is a long composition L(f_n(cdots f_1(mathbf x))), so its gradient is a long product of Jacobians. Backpropagation computes this product right-to-left starting from the scalar loss, as cheap vector–Jacobian products (module 04). 🧮 Formula · Identities worth memorizing Function Gradient w.r.t. mathbf x (or mathbf w) mathbf a^topmathbf x mathbf a mathbf x^top Amathbf x (A+A^top)mathbf x (= 2Amathbf x if A symmetric) \\|mathbf x\\|^2 2mathbf x \\|Xmathbf w-mathbf y\\|^2 2X^top(Xmathbf w-mathbf y) softmax then cross-entropy, w.r.t. logits mathbf z hatmathbf y-mathbf y 💡 Key idea · The shape trick The gradient of a scalar with respect to a matrix W has the same shape as W . If your derived gradient has a different shape, it's wrong. Use shapes to decide where transposes go: e.g. in nabla_mathbf w\\|Xmathbf w-mathbf y\\|^2 the residual is Ntimes1 and the answer must be dtimes1, so it has to be X^top(cdot). ✍️ Worked example · Gradient of least squares, with shapes L(mathbf w)=\\|Xmathbf w-mathbf y\\|^2 with Xinmathbb R^Ntimes d. Let mathbf r = Xmathbf w-mathbf y (Ntimes1). Then L=mathbf r^topmathbf r, dL = 2mathbf r^top dmathbf r = 2mathbf r^top X\\,dmathbf w, so nabla_mathbf wL = 2X^topmathbf r (dtimes1 ✓ same shape as mathbf w). Setting it to 0 gives the normal equations X^top Xmathbf w = X^topmathbf y. Numbers: points (0,1),(1,2),(2,2) with a bias column: X has rows (1,0),(1,1),(1,2) and mathbf y=(1,2,2)^top. Then X^top X has rows (3,3),(3,5) and X^topmathbf y=(5,6)^top, so mathbf w=(7/6,\\ 1/2): the line hat y = 1.167 + 0.5x. 📖 Definition · Hessian and second-order Taylor expansion The Hessian H = nabla^2 f is the matrix of second derivatives, H_ij=partial^2 f/partial x_ipartial x_j. It is symmetric and describes curvature . f(mathbf x+boldsymboldelta) approx f(mathbf x) + nabla f^topboldsymboldelta + tfrac12boldsymboldelta^top Hboldsymboldelta Every optimizer comes from this expansion: gradient descent uses only the first-order term; Newton's method also uses H. 📖 Definition · Convex function f is convex if the straight line between any two points of its graph lies on or above the graph. For twice-differentiable f: convex iff Hsucceq 0 everywhere. Convex functions have no bad local minima. Linear and logistic regression losses are convex; neural-network losses are not , yet gradient descent still works well in practice. ⚠️ Common mistake · Check gradients numerically Always verify a hand-derived gradient with finite differences : fracf(w+h)-f(w-h)2h with happrox10^-5 (in float64). The relative error should be around 10^-6 or smaller. ▶ Visualizing the chain rule and product rule ▶️ Visualizing the chain rule and product rule · 3Blue1Brown · 16 min (plays here; or open on YouTube) 📚 Go deeper (optional) ⭐ The Matrix Calculus You Need for Deep Learning , Parr & Howard: a short, essential article. Mathematics for Machine Learning , ch. 5 (vector calculus). Optional depth: MIT 18.S096 Matrix Calculus for ML . 6 · Optimization: gradient descent The gradient points uphill, so to minimize a loss L(theta) we repeatedly step downhill. 🧮 Formula · Gradient descent (GD) theta_t+1 = theta_t - eta\\,nabla L(theta_t) eta>0 is the learning rate (step size). Too small: painfully slow. Too large: the iterates overshoot and diverge. ✍️ Worked example · Why eta < 2/lambda_max On f(x)=tfrac a2x^2, GD gives x_t+1=x_t-eta a x_t=(1-eta a)x_t. This shrinks iff |1-eta a|<1 iff 0<eta<2/a. For eta ain(1,2) it converges while oscillating (sign flips), and for eta a>2 it blows up. A quadratic in d dimensions decouples along the eigenvectors of H, so the steepest direction (lambda_max) limits eta, and the flattest (lambda_min) then converges at rate 1-etalambda_min approx 1-2/kappa. That's why ill-conditioning (large kappa=lambda_max/lambda_min) is slow. 📖 Definition · Stochastic gradient descent (SGD) and mini-batches Computing nabla L over the whole dataset is expensive. SGD instead estimates it on a small random mini-batch B of examples: theta leftarrow theta - eta\\,frac1|B|sum_iin Bnablaell_i(theta) The estimate is unbiased but noisy, and each step is far cheaper. The noise is widely believed to help generalization (it favors flatter minima), though this is still an active research question. 📖 Definition · Momentum Keep a running velocity mathbf v leftarrow betamathbf v + nabla L, then step thetaleftarrowtheta-etamathbf v (typically beta=0.9). It averages out zig-zags across a narrow valley and speeds up along its floor. Adam (module 05) adds a per-parameter step size on top. 🔭 Intuition · Physics view GD is an overdamped particle sliding down the potential L. Momentum is a heavy ball with friction. SGD noise acts like temperature, jiggling the ball out of sharp, narrow holes. 📖 Definition · Lagrange multipliers (constrained optimization) To optimize f(mathbf x) subject to g(mathbf x)=0, solve nabla f = munabla g together with the constraint. At the optimum, the level set of f just touches the constraint surface. It shows up in derivations such as \"maximum entropy ⇒ softmax\" and SVMs. 🎛️ Try it · Optimizer Landscape Pick a narrow valley and raise the learning rate until GD starts to oscillate, then diverges. Then switch on momentum and Adam and compare the paths. ▶ Playlist · Neural networks (series) ▶️ Neural networks (series) · 3Blue1Brown · chapter 2 is \"Gradient descent, how neural networks learn\" (plays here; or open on YouTube) 7 · Probability and statistics A model's output is best read as a probability distribution , and training is about making the observed data probable. 📖 Definition · Random variable, distribution A random variable X is a quantity whose value is uncertain. A discrete one has a probability mass function p(x)=P(X=x) with sum_x p(x)=1. A continuous one has a density p(x)ge0 with int p(x)\\,dx = 1 (probabilities are areas under it). 📖 Definition · Expectation and variance mathbb E[X] = sum_x x\\,p(x) \\;Big(or int x\\,p(x)\\,dxBig) qquad Var(X) = mathbb Ebig[(X-mathbb E X)^2big] = mathbb E[X^2]-(mathbb E X)^2 Linearity always holds: mathbb E[aX+bY] = a\\,mathbb E X + b\\,mathbb E Y. For independent variables, variances add: Var(X+Y)=VarX+VarY. 📖 Definition · Conditional probability and independence P(Amid B) = P(A,B)/P(B): the probability of A once you know B happened. A and B are independent if P(A,B)=P(A)P(B), i.e. knowing one tells you nothing about the other. 🧮 Formula · Bayes' rule p(thetamid D) = fracp(Dmidtheta)\\,p(theta)p(D) \\;propto\\; underbracep(Dmidtheta)_likelihood\\;underbracep(theta)_prior The posterior (belief after seeing data D) is proportional to likelihood × prior. ✍️ Worked example · A medical test A disease affects 1% of people. A test catches 90% of cases and has a 9% false-positive rate. You test positive. P(sickmid+) = frac0.9cdot0.010.9cdot0.01 + 0.09cdot0.99 = frac0.0090.0981approx 9\\%. The low prior dominates, so most positives are false alarms. 🧮 Formula · Key distributions Distribution Probability Mean / variance Used for Bernoulli(p) p^x(1-p)^1-x, xin\\0,1\\ p / p(1-p) binary classification Categorical(mathbf p) p_k for class k — multi-class classification Gaussian mathcal N(mu,sigma^2) frac1sqrt2pisigma^2e^-(x-mu)^2/2sigma^2 mu / sigma^2 regression noise, VAEs, diffusion 📖 Definition · Maximum likelihood (MLE) and MAP Given data x_1,dots,x_N and a model p(xmidtheta), MLE picks the parameters that make the data most probable: theta^* = argmax_thetasum_ilog p(x_imidtheta) (logs turn the product of probabilities into a sum). MAP adds a prior: maximize sum_ilog p(x_imidtheta)+log p(theta). 💡 Key idea · Almost every DL loss is a negative log-likelihood Gaussian likelihood → mean squared error (example below). Bernoulli / Categorical likelihood → cross-entropy (section 8). A Gaussian prior on the weights → L2 weight decay (practice 5). ✍️ Worked example · MSE is Gaussian MLE Model y_i = f_theta(x_i)+varepsilon_i, varepsilon_isimmathcal N(0,sigma^2). Then -log p(mathbf ymid X,theta) = sum_i frac(y_i-f_theta(x_i))^22sigma^2 + frac N2log(2pisigma^2) The second term doesn't depend on theta, so maximizing likelihood = minimizing sum_i (y_i-f_theta(x_i))^2. Special case f_theta=mu: fracddmu=0 gives hatmu=bar y, the sample mean. 🧮 Formula · Monte Carlo and the reparameterization trick Expectations can be estimated by sampling: mathbb E_p[f(X)] approx frac1Nsum_i=1^N f(x_i) with x_isim p. To sample from mathcal N(mu,sigma^2) in a way gradients can flow through, write x=mu+sigmaepsilon with epsilonsimmathcal N(0,1) (used in VAEs and diffusion, module 10). ▶ Bayes theorem, the geometry of changing beliefs ▶️ Bayes theorem, the geometry of changing beliefs · 3Blue1Brown · 15 min (plays here; or open on YouTube) 📚 Go deeper (optional) Mathematics for Machine Learning , ch. 6 (probability) and ch. 8–9 (models, linear regression as MLE). Optional depth: Harvard Stat 110 (Blitzstein) . 8 · Information theory: entropy, cross-entropy, KL Information theory measures surprise . An event with probability p carries -log p units of surprise: certain events (p=1) carry none, rare events carry a lot. With natural logs the unit is the nat ; with log_2 it is the bit . 📖 Definition · Entropy H(p) = -sum_x p(x)log p(x) the average surprise of a distribution. It is largest for the uniform distribution (log K for K outcomes) and 0 when one outcome is certain. 🔭 Same form as the Gibbs entropy in statistical physics. 📖 Definition · Cross-entropy H(p,q) = -sum_x p(x)log q(x) the average surprise when reality follows p but you predict with q. This is the classification loss: with a one-hot label p (all mass on the true class c) it reduces to -log q(c), the negative log-probability your model gave to the correct answer. 📖 Definition · KL divergence D_KL(p\\,\\|\\,q) = sum_x p(x)logfracp(x)q(x) = H(p,q) - H(p) \\;ge\\; 0 the extra surprise from using q instead of the truth p. It is zero only when p=q. It is not symmetric (D_KL(p\\|q)ne D_KL(q\\|p)), so it is not a distance. Since H(p) is fixed by the data, minimizing cross-entropy = minimizing KL to the data. ✍️ Worked example · D_KLge 0 (Gibbs' inequality) -D_KL(p\\|q)=sum_x p(x)logfracq(x)p(x) le logsum_x p(x)fracq(x)p(x) = logsum_x q(x) le log 1 = 0, by Jensen's inequality (log is concave). Equality iff p=q. 🧮 Formula · Softmax (with temperature) A network outputs raw scores ( logits ) mathbf z. Softmax turns them into probabilities: sigma(mathbf z)_i = frace^z_i/Tsum_j e^z_j/T 🔭 This is the Boltzmann distribution with energy -z_i and temperature T. Tto0 picks the max; large T flattens toward uniform. The \"temperature\" setting in LLM sampling is literally this T. ⚠️ Common mistake · Compute softmax and log-softmax stably e^1000 overflows to inf in float32. Since softmax is unchanged by subtracting a constant, libraries compute e^z_i-max_j z_j. For the loss, use a fused log-softmax / log-sum-exp (logsum_j e^z_j = m + logsum_j e^z_j-m with m=max_j z_j) instead of log(softmax(z)) . Practice (answers hidden) Give nabla_mathbf w of the ridge loss \\|Xmathbf w-mathbf y\\|^2+lambda\\|mathbf w\\|^2 and its minimizer. Answer 2X^top(Xmathbf w-mathbf y)+2lambdamathbf w = 0 Rightarrow mathbf w^*=(X^top X+lambda I)^-1X^topmathbf y. The +lambda I makes the matrix invertible even when X^top X is singular, and improves its condition number. GD on f(x,y)=x^2+10y^2. What is the largest stable eta? Roughly how many steps does the x-coordinate need to shrink by e^-1 at eta=0.09? Answer H=diag(2,20), so eta<2/20=0.1. Along x: factor 1-0.09cdot2=0.82 per step, and 0.82^t=e^-1 at t=1/(-ln 0.82)approx5 steps. Along y: factor 1-1.8=-0.8, which oscillates but converges. Compute D_KL(p\\|q) and D_KL(q\\|p) for p=Bernoulli(0.5), q=Bernoulli(0.9) (natural log). Answer D_KL(p\\|q)=0.5lnfrac0.50.9+0.5lnfrac0.50.1approx 0.511 nats. D_KL(q\\|p)=0.9lnfrac0.90.5+0.1lnfrac0.10.5approx 0.368 nats. Different, so KL is not symmetric (and not a distance). Show that softmax(mathbf z+cmathbf 1)=softmax(mathbf z). Why does every library compute softmax as e^z_i-max_j z_j/sum_j e^z_j-max_j z_j? Answer e^z_i+c/sum_j e^z_j+c = e^c e^z_i/(e^csum_j e^z_j). Subtracting the max makes every exponent le 0, so nothing overflows (e.g. e^1000 = inf in float32), and the result is unchanged. MAP with prior mathbf wsimmathcal N(0,tau^2 I) and Gaussian noise sigma^2: which loss do you minimize? Answer -log p(mathbf wmid D) = frac12sigma^2\\|Xmathbf w-mathbf y\\|^2 + frac12tau^2\\|mathbf w\\|^2 + const: ridge regression with lambda=sigma^2/tau^2. A tighter prior (small tau) means stronger weight decay. A matrix has singular values 10 and 0.01. What is its condition number, and what does it do to the unit circle? Answer kappa=1000. The circle becomes a very thin ellipse (semi-axes 10 and 0.01). Solving Amathbf x=mathbf b can amplify relative errors in mathbf b by up to 1000×, and GD on \\|Amathbf x-mathbf b\\|^2 (Hessian condition number kappa^2=10^6) crawls. A=beginpmatrix3&0\\\\0&0endpmatrix. What are its rank, determinant, column space and null space? Answer Rank 1, det A=0. Column space: the x-axis (every output is (3x_1, 0)). Null space: the y-axis (anything (0, x_2) maps to zero). Rank–nullity: 1+1=2 ✓. Find the eigenvalues of beginpmatrix4&1\\\\2&3endpmatrix. Answer det(A-lambda I) = (4-lambda)(3-lambda)-2 = lambda^2-7lambda+10 = (lambda-5)(lambda-2), so lambda=5 and lambda=2. Check: their sum 7 equals the trace and their product 10 equals the determinant. A classifier gives the true class probability 0.8 on one example and 0.05 on another. What is the cross-entropy loss on each (natural log)? Answer -ln 0.8approx0.22 and -ln0.05approx3.0. Confident wrong answers are punished heavily: the loss grows without bound as the probability of the true class goes to 0. Build Derive nabla_mathbf w for linear regression by hand. Then check it numerically with finite differences fracf(w+h)-f(w-h)2h in NumPy. Write PCA with np.linalg.svd on a toy 2D dataset and plot the principal axes. Simulate GD on f(x,y)=x^2+10y^2 with different eta. Watch the oscillation and divergence, and relate it to the condition number. Traps Memorizing formulas without checking shapes → derive with shapes, then verify numerically. Confusing eigenvectors with singular vectors. They coincide only for symmetric positive semi-definite matrices. Treating KL as a distance. It's asymmetric, and D_KL(p\\|q)=infty whenever q=0 somewhere p>0. Computing log(softmax) in two steps. Use a fused log-softmax / log-sum-exp for stability. Over-studying math up front. Learn the rest just in time when a later module needs it. Go deeper (all optional) Everything above is self-contained. Use these if you want a second explanation or more exercises: - ⭐ Mathematics for Machine Learning , Deisenroth, Faisal & Ong (free PDF). Read ch. 2–7 selectively . Skim what you know. - ⭐ The Matrix Calculus You Need for Deep Learning , Parr & Howard. - 3Blue1Brown: Essence of Linear Algebra : the geometric intuition, ~3 h (embedded in section 2). - Optional depth: MIT 18.06 (Strang) , Harvard Stat 110 (Blitzstein) , MIT 18.S096 Matrix Calculus for ML . Done when Every skip-test item is answerable · gradient check passes to ~1e-6 relative error"},{"t":"02 · Python, NumPy & Tools","u":"deep-learning/02-python-numpy-tools.html","s":"Deep Learning","h":[{"n":"Skip test","id":"skip-test"},{"n":"1 · Python essentials for numeric code","id":"1-python-essentials-for-numeric-code"},{"n":"2 · NumPy arrays: shape, dtype, axes","id":"2-numpy-arrays-shape-dtype-axes"},{"n":"3 · Broadcasting","id":"3-broadcasting"},{"n":"4 · Views vs copies","id":"4-views-vs-copies"},{"n":"5 · Vectorization and einsum","id":"5-vectorization-and-einsum"},{"n":"6 · Numbers on a computer: dtypes","id":"6-numbers-on-a-computer-dtypes"},{"n":"7 · Tooling: environments, notebooks, git","id":"7-tooling-environments-notebooks-git"},{"n":"Practice (answers hidden)","id":"practice-answers-hidden"},{"n":"Build","id":"build"},{"n":"Traps","id":"traps"},{"n":"Go deeper (all optional)","id":"go-deeper-all-optional"},{"n":"Done when","id":"done-when"}],"x":"02 · Python, NumPy & Tools Module 02 ⏱ 12 min read View Markdown source Goal: fluent, vectorized numeric Python and a working dev setup. · Time: 8–12 h · Needs: basic Python 🎯 What you'll learn By the end of this chapter you can: avoid the Python habits that cause silent bugs in numeric code (references, mutability); create, index and reduce NumPy arrays, and always know their shape and dtype ; predict broadcasting results and tell views from copies ; replace Python loops with whole-array operations and einsum ; set up an environment, a notebook, and a git repo. Everything you learn about NumPy carries over almost unchanged to PyTorch tensors (module 05). Skip test Explain NumPy broadcasting and predict the shape of (32,1,5) + (4,5) and of (N,) - (N,1) . Say why the second is a common bug. Compute pairwise distances between two point sets A (n,d) , B (m,d) with no Python loop . Use list/dict comprehensions, classes with __call__ , generators, and type hints. Say which of x[1:3] , x[[1,2]] , x[x > 0] , x.reshape(-1) return views and which return copies. Create a virtual env, install packages, and run a Jupyter notebook. git commit, branch, push. Write a .gitignore . 🎯 Not sure? Take the auto-graded Foundations Quiz (filter by module) before deciding. 1 · Python essentials for numeric code You already know basic Python. These are the parts that matter most when writing ML code, and the ones that bite people coming from C++ or Java. 📖 Definition · Names are references A Python variable is a name attached to an object , not a box holding a value. b = a makes b a second name for the same object; nothing is copied. For immutable objects (numbers, strings, tuples) this never matters. For mutable ones (lists, dicts, arrays) a change through one name is visible through the other. 💻 In code · References and copies a = [1, 2, 3] b = a # same list b.append(4) # a is now [1, 2, 3, 4] too c = a.copy() # a new list; changing c leaves a alone ⚠️ Common mistake · Mutable default arguments Default values are created once , when the function is defined: def add(x, items=[]): # BUG: the same list is shared by every call items.append(x); return items add(1); add(2) # -> [1, 2] Use items=None and create the list inside: items = [] if items is None else items . 📖 Definition · Comprehensions A compact way to build a list, dict or set from a loop: [x**2 for x in range(5) if x % 2 == 0] → [0, 4, 16] · w: len(w) for w in words · x % 3 for x in data . 📖 Definition · Classes and __call__ A class bundles data (attributes) with functions that use it (methods). Defining __call__ lets an object be used like a function. This is exactly how PyTorch layers work: layer(x) calls layer.__call__(x) , which runs its forward method. class Linear: def __init__(self, W, b): self.W, self.b = W, b def __call__(self, x): return x @ self.W.T + self.b 📖 Definition · Generators A function containing yield produces values one at a time, lazily, instead of building the whole list in memory. Data loaders are generators: they hand you one mini-batch at a time. def batches(X, size): for i in range(0, len(X), size): yield X[i:i + size] 📖 Definition · Type hints Optional annotations like def f(x: np.ndarray, k: int = 3) -> float: . Python ignores them at runtime, but editors and tools (mypy, pyright) use them to catch mistakes and they document intent. 2 · NumPy arrays: shape, dtype, axes NumPy's ndarray is a block of numbers of one type , laid out in memory, with a shape that says how to read it as a grid. All the speed of NumPy (and PyTorch) comes from running compiled loops over that block instead of looping in Python. 📖 Definition · ndarray, shape, ndim, dtype shape: a tuple of sizes, e.g. (32, 3, 28, 28) = 32 images, 3 channels, 28×28 pixels. ndim: number of axes ( len(shape) ). A scalar has ndim 0, a vector 1, a matrix 2. dtype: the element type, e.g. float32 , int64 , bool . Every element has the same dtype. size: total number of elements (product of the shape). 💻 In code · Creating arrays np.array([[1, 2], [3, 4]]) # from a list, shape (2, 2) np.zeros((3, 4)); np.ones(5); np.full((2, 2), 7.0) np.arange(0, 10, 2) # [0 2 4 6 8] np.linspace(0, 1, 5) # 5 evenly spaced points from 0 to 1 rng = np.random.default_rng(0) # seeded generator (reproducible) rng.normal(size=(100, 3)) # standard normal samples 📖 Definition · Indexing Basic slicing: x[1:4] , x[:, 0] , x[::2] , x[..., -1] . Start:stop:step, stop excluded. Negative indices count from the end. Fancy (integer-array) indexing: x[[0, 2, 5]] picks those rows. Boolean masking: x[x > 0] picks the elements where the mask is True (result is 1-D). None (or np.newaxis ) inserts an axis of size 1: x[:, None] turns shape (N,) into (N, 1) . 📖 Definition · Axes and reductions Reductions like sum , mean , max , argmax take an axis argument: the axis that disappears . For X of shape (N, C) : X.sum(axis=0) → shape (C,) (sum over rows, one value per column); X.sum(axis=1) → (N,) . keepdims=True keeps the reduced axis with size 1, e.g. (N, 1) , so the result still broadcasts against X . 💡 Key idea · Reshape and transpose x.reshape(4, -1) reinterprets the same numbers with a new shape ( -1 = \"work it out\"); the total size must match. x.T / x.transpose(1, 0, 2) reorders axes. Reshape keeps elements in reading (row-major) order; transpose changes which axis is which. They are not interchangeable: reshaping a (2,3) matrix to (3,2) is not its transpose. 3 · Broadcasting Broadcasting lets you combine arrays of different shapes without writing loops or making copies, e.g. subtract a per-column mean from every row. 🧮 Formula · The broadcasting rule Line the shapes up from the right . If one has fewer axes, pad it on the left with 1s. For each axis, the sizes must be equal , or one of them must be 1 . Axes of size 1 are stretched (virtually, with no copy) to match. The result takes the larger size on every axis. If any axis breaks rule 2, NumPy raises an error. ✍️ Worked example · Predicting shapes Left Right Result (32,1,5) (4,5) → (1,4,5) (32,4,5) (N,C) (C,) → (1,C) (N,C) : one value per column (N,C) (N,1) (N,C) : one value per row (3,4) (3,) → (1,3) error : 4 vs 3 (N,) → (1,N) (N,1) (N,N) ⚠️ ⚠️ Common mistake · The (N,) vs (N,1) bug y_pred of shape (N,1) minus y of shape (N,) gives an (N,N) matrix of every prediction against every target. The loss is then computed on nonsense, often without any error. Always assert y_pred.shape == y.shape before computing a loss. 🎛️ Try it · Broadcasting Lab Enter two shapes and see how they align, which axes stretch, and the resulting shape, then drill yourself with the random quiz until you get 10 in a row. 4 · Views vs copies Because arrays are blocks of memory with a shape on top, many operations just create a new view of the same memory. 📖 Definition · View, copy A view is a new array object that shares memory with the original: changing one changes the other. A copy has its own memory. Check with np.shares_memory(a, b) . Operation Result basic slicing x[1:4] , x[:, 0] , x[::2] view x.reshape(...) , x.T , x[None] view (reshape copies only if it can't avoid it) fancy indexing x[[1, 2]] copy boolean masking x[x > 0] copy arithmetic x + 1 , x.copy() new array ✍️ Worked example · View vs copy x = np.arange(6); v = x[1:4]; v[0] = 99 # x is now [0 99 2 3 4 5]: v was a view y = np.arange(6); c = y[[1, 2, 3]]; c[0] = 99 # y is unchanged: fancy indexing copied 📖 Definition · Strides and contiguity An array stores strides : how many bytes to jump to move one step along each axis. A transpose just swaps strides, so it's free, but the result is no longer laid out row by row ( not contiguous ). Some operations need contiguous memory and will copy; in PyTorch, .view() refuses non-contiguous tensors while .reshape() copies when needed. ⚠️ Common mistake · In-place updates through views v += 1 on a view changes the original array too. That is useful when intended and a nasty bug when not. 5 · Vectorization and einsum Vectorizing means expressing a computation as operations on whole arrays. A Python loop over a million elements is typically 10–100× slower than the equivalent NumPy call, which runs one compiled loop over contiguous memory. Thinking in whole arrays is the core DL skill, and PyTorch works the same way. ✍️ Worked example · Pairwise distances with no loop A (n,d) , B (m,d) → D (n,m) with D_ij = \\|mathbf a_i-mathbf b_j\\|. # Version 1: broadcasting. (n,1,d) - (1,m,d) -> (n,m,d), then sum over d. Memory O(n·m·d). D = np.sqrt(((A[:, None, :] - B[None, :, :]) ** 2).sum(-1)) # Version 2: expand ||a-b||² = ||a||² + ||b||² - 2a·b. Memory O(n·m), and the matmul is fast. sq = (A**2).sum(1)[:, None] + (B**2).sum(1)[None, :] - 2 * A @ B.T D = np.sqrt(np.maximum(sq, 0)) # clip tiny negatives caused by rounding Both agree ( np.allclose ✓). Version 2 is what k-NN in CS231n assignment 1 asks for. ✍️ Worked example · Row-wise softmax, stable and vectorized def softmax(Z): # Z: (N, C) Z = Z - Z.max(axis=1, keepdims=True) # keepdims -> (N,1), broadcasts back over C E = np.exp(Z) return E / E.sum(axis=1, keepdims=True) Without keepdims , Z.max(axis=1) has shape (N,) , and (N,C) - (N,) either errors or (if N == C) silently subtracts the wrong thing. 📖 Definition · Matrix multiplication with @ A @ B is matrix multiplication (module 01). With more than 2 axes it is batched : (b,n,k) @ (b,k,m) → (b,n,m) , one matmul per batch element. * is element-wise and never a matrix product. 📖 Definition · einsum np.einsum describes a tensor operation by naming the axes . Letters that appear in the inputs but not in the output are summed over. einsum Same as 'ij,jk->ik' A @ B 'bij,bjk->bik' batched A @ B 'ij->j' X.sum(axis=0) 'i,i->' dot product a @ b 'ij->ji' X.T 'bhqd,bhkd->bhqk' attention scores QK^top per batch and head (module 08) 6 · Numbers on a computer: dtypes Floating-point numbers have limited precision and range. In DL you trade precision for speed and memory, so you need to know the limits. 🧮 Formula · Common float types dtype Bits Machine epsilon (relative precision) Largest value Typical use float64 64 2.2times10^-16 1.8times10^308 NumPy default, gradient checks float32 32 1.2times10^-7 3.4times10^38 PyTorch default, training float16 16 9.8times10^-4 65\\,504 fast GPU math (needs loss scaling) bfloat16 16 7.8times10^-3 approx3.4times10^38 fast GPU math, same range as float32 ⚠️ Common mistake · Mixing dtypes NumPy defaults to float64 and PyTorch to float32 . Mixing them silently upcasts (NumPy) or raises an error (PyTorch). Integer arrays divide differently too: np.array([1, 2]) / 2 gives floats but // floors. Check x.dtype when results look odd. 💡 Key idea · Overflow and cancellation np.exp(1000) is inf in any float type, which is why softmax subtracts the max (module 01). Subtracting two nearly equal numbers loses precision (\"catastrophic cancellation\"), which is why finite-difference gradient checks use float64. 7 · Tooling: environments, notebooks, git 📖 Definition · Virtual environment An isolated folder of packages for one project, so projects with different versions don't clash. python -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate # or: uv venv && source .venv/bin/activate pip install numpy matplotlib jupyter # or: uv pip install ... pip freeze > requirements.txt # record exact versions 📖 Definition · Notebooks vs scripts Jupyter (or Google Colab , which gives a free GPU) is ideal for exploring: run a cell, look at a plot, try again. Notebooks hide state (cells run out of order), so move reusable code into .py files and import it. Use VS Code (or any real editor) for those files. 📖 Definition · git essentials git records snapshots ( commits ) of your project so you can go back, compare, and work on branches. git init # start tracking a folder git add model.py && git commit -m \"Add MLP\" # snapshot git switch -c experiment # new branch for a risky change git log --oneline; git diff # history; what changed git push -u origin main # upload to GitHub A .gitignore lists files git should never track: .venv/ , __pycache__/ , *.ckpt , data/ , .ipynb_checkpoints/ . 📚 Go deeper (optional) ⭐ The Missing Semester (MIT) : shell, git, editors. Do the git and shell lectures. Practice (answers hidden) Shapes: (8,1,6,1) * (7,1,5) ? (3,4) + (3,) ? (64,10).mean(axis=1, keepdims=True) ? Answer (8,7,6,5) · error (4 vs 3, neither is 1; use (3,1) ) · (64,1) . One-hot encode integer labels y of shape (N,) with C classes in one line. Answer np.eye(C)[y] → (N, C) . (Fancy indexing picks rows of the identity matrix.) Count live neighbors for every cell of a Game-of-Life board G (H,W) with no Python loop over cells (wrap-around edges are fine). Answer N = sum(np.roll(np.roll(G, i, 0), j, 1) for i in (-1,0,1) for j in (-1,0,1) if (i,j) != (0,0)) G = ((N == 3) | ((G == 1) & (N == 2))).astype(int) The 8-term sum loops over offsets , not cells. scipy.signal.convolve2d with a 3×3 kernel of ones (center 0) does the same thing. loss = ((y_pred - y)**2).mean() with y_pred (N,1) and y (N,) gives a suspiciously small loss and the model won't train. Why? Answer (N,1) - (N,) broadcasts to (N,N) , so the loss averages every prediction against every target. Fix: y_pred.squeeze(1) or y[:, None] , and add assert y_pred.shape == y.shape . Rewrite np.einsum('bij,bjk->bik', A, B) and np.einsum('ij->j', X) with ordinary NumPy calls. Answer A @ B (batched matmul) and X.sum(axis=0) . X has shape (N, D) . Standardize every column to mean 0 and standard deviation 1 in one line. Answer (X - X.mean(axis=0)) / (X.std(axis=0) + 1e-8) . The (D,) statistics broadcast over the rows; the small constant avoids division by zero for constant columns. After x = np.arange(12).reshape(3, 4) , is x.T.reshape(-1) a view? What are its first four elements? Answer It's a copy : x.T is not contiguous, so flattening it in row order needs new memory. The first four elements are [0, 4, 8, 1] (reading x.T row by row walks down the columns of x ). Build Vectorized k-nearest-neighbors classifier on MNIST or CIFAR-10 (CS231n assignment 1 style). Time the loop version against the vectorized one. Conway's Game of Life with NumPy only (convolution-like neighbor counts, no loops over cells). Traps Silent broadcasting bugs: (N,) - (N,1) becomes (N,N) . Assert shapes in your code. a = b for arrays doesn't copy (it's another name for the same object). Use b.copy() when you need one. In-place ops on a view ( v += 1 ) change the original array too. Living in notebooks forever. Move reusable code into .py files early. Go deeper (all optional) ⭐ NumPy: the absolute basics + Broadcasting (official docs) ⭐ CS231n Python/NumPy tutorial : short and DL-oriented. From Python to NumPy , N. Rougier (vectorization mindset). 100 NumPy exercises : do ~40 for fluency. The Missing Semester (MIT) : shell, git, editors. Plotting: Matplotlib quick start . Done when k-NN runs fully vectorized · project lives in a git repo with a README"},{"t":"03 · Machine Learning Fundamentals","u":"deep-learning/03-ml-fundamentals.html","s":"Deep Learning","h":[{"n":"Skip test","id":"skip-test"},{"n":"1 · What \"learning\" means","id":"1-what-learning-means"},{"n":"2 · Linear regression","id":"2-linear-regression"},{"n":"3 · Logistic and softmax regression","id":"3-logistic-and-softmax-regression"},{"n":"4 · Generalization: underfitting and overfitting","id":"4-generalization-underfitting-and-overfitting"},{"n":"5 · Regularization","id":"5-regularization"},{"n":"6 · Honest evaluation: cross-validation and leakage","id":"6-honest-evaluation-cross-validation-and-leakage"},{"n":"7 · Metrics: is the model actually good?","id":"7-metrics-is-the-model-actually-good"},{"n":"8 · The classical toolbox (know what they are)","id":"8-the-classical-toolbox-know-what-they-are"},{"n":"Practice (answers hidden)","id":"practice-answers-hidden"},{"n":"Build","id":"build"},{"n":"Traps","id":"traps"},{"n":"Go deeper (all optional)","id":"go-deeper-all-optional"},{"n":"Done when","id":"done-when"}],"x":"03 · Machine Learning Fundamentals Module 03 ⏱ 14 min read View Markdown source Goal: the concepts every DL model inherits: loss, generalization, evaluation. · Time: 20–30 h · Needs: 01, 02 🎯 What you'll learn By the end of this chapter you can: state the supervised-learning setup (data, model, loss, risk) and why we split data three ways; fit linear and logistic regression, derive their gradients, and see that a neural net is stacked logistic regressions; diagnose underfitting vs overfitting and fix each one, using the bias–variance decomposition and regularization; evaluate honestly: cross-validation, data leakage , and the right metric (precision, recall, F1, ROC-AUC, PR curves). Skip test Explain train / validation / test splits and why tuning on the test set is cheating. Derive the logistic regression gradient. Explain why its loss is cross-entropy. Explain the bias–variance trade-off, and how L2 regularization changes it. For an imbalanced dataset (1% positive), explain why accuracy is misleading, and define precision, recall, F1, and ROC-AUC. Explain k-fold cross-validation and data leakage, with an example. Say what ROC-AUC measures as a probability, and why it doesn't change when the class balance changes but precision does. 🎯 Not sure? Take the auto-graded Foundations Quiz (filter by module) before deciding. 1 · What \"learning\" means A machine-learning model is a function with adjustable knobs ( parameters ). Learning means turning the knobs so the function does well on examples, and, more importantly, on examples it has never seen . 📖 Definition · Supervised learning You are given N labelled examples (mathbf x_i, y_i): inputs mathbf x_i (features) and the correct outputs y_i (labels). You choose a model f_theta with parameters theta, which maps an input to a prediction hat y = f_theta(mathbf x); a loss ell(hat y, y)ge0 that measures how wrong one prediction is. Regression predicts a number (yinmathbb R); classification predicts a class (yin\\1,dots,C\\). 🧮 Formula · Empirical risk minimization hattheta = argmin_theta\\; underbracefrac1Nsum_i=1^N ellbig(f_theta(mathbf x_i), y_ibig)_training loss (empirical risk) What we actually care about is the expected risk mathbb E_(mathbf x,y)simdata\\,ell(f_theta(mathbf x),y) on new data from the same source. The gap between the two is the generalization gap . 💡 Key idea · Learning = optimization + generalization Driving training loss down is optimization (module 01, section 6). Making sure that also lowers the loss on new data is generalization, and it is the hard part. 📖 Definition · Train / validation / test split Training set: used to fit the parameters. Validation set: used to make decisions (model size, learning rate, regularization strength, when to stop). Test set: used once , at the very end, to report performance. Every time you look at a score and change something, that data stops being \"unseen\". Tuning on the test set makes the test score optimistic, which is why it is cheating. 2 · Linear regression The simplest model: a weighted sum of the features plus a constant. 📖 Definition · Linear regression hat y = mathbf w^topmathbf x + b trained with the mean squared error frac1Nsum_i(hat y_i-y_i)^2. Adding a constant-1 feature to every mathbf x lets you fold b into mathbf w. 🧮 Formula · Closed form (normal equations) With all inputs stacked as rows of X (Ntimes d): mathbf w^* = (X^top X)^-1X^topmathbf y derived in module 01, section 5 by setting nabla_mathbf w\\|Xmathbf w-mathbf y\\|^2 = 2X^top(Xmathbf w-mathbf y)=0. In practice use np.linalg.lstsq , or gradient descent when d is huge. 💡 Key idea · Why MSE? MSE is the negative log-likelihood under Gaussian noise (module 01, section 7). If your errors have heavy outliers, the mean absolute error (Laplace noise) or the Huber loss is more robust. 📖 Definition · Feature scaling (standardization) Replace each feature by (x-mu)/sigma, using the mean and standard deviation of the training set . Features on wildly different scales make the loss surface a long thin valley (high condition number, module 01), so gradient descent crawls. Scaling makes it rounder. 3 · Logistic and softmax regression For classification we want a probability , not an unbounded number. Logistic regression squashes the linear score through the sigmoid. 📖 Definition · Sigmoid and logistic regression sigma(z) = frac11+e^-z in (0,1) qquad p(y=1midmathbf x) = sigma(mathbf w^topmathbf x) The decision boundary mathbf w^topmathbf x = 0 (where p=0.5) is a straight line (hyperplane), so logistic regression is a linear classifier . Useful identity: sigma'(z) = sigma(z)\\,(1-sigma(z)). 🧮 Formula · Binary cross-entropy (log loss) ell = -big[ylog p + (1-y)log(1-p)big], qquad p = sigma(mathbf w^topmathbf x) It is the negative log-likelihood of a Bernoulli model (module 01, section 8). The total loss is convex in mathbf w, so gradient descent finds the global optimum. (If the classes are perfectly separable, \\|mathbf w\\|toinfty without regularization.) ✍️ Worked example · The logistic-regression gradient in three lines One example, z=mathbf w^topmathbf x, p=sigma(z). Using sigma'(z)=p(1-p): fracpartialellpartial p=-frac yp+frac1-y1-p, so fracpartialellpartial z=fracpartialellpartial p\\,p(1-p)=p-y, and nabla_mathbf well=(p-y)mathbf x. Stack the rows: nabla = X^top(hatmathbf y-mathbf y) for the summed loss (divide by N for the mean). Same \"prediction minus target\" form as linear regression. That isn't luck: both are generalized linear models with their canonical link. Numbers: mathbf x=(1,2), mathbf w=mathbf 0, y=1 → p=0.5, gradient =(0.5-1)(1,2)=(-0.5,-1). A GD step increases both weights, pushing p toward 1 ✓. 📖 Definition · Softmax regression (multinomial logistic regression) For C classes, keep one weight vector per class, compute scores z_k = mathbf w_k^topmathbf x, and turn them into probabilities with softmax: p_k = e^z_k/sum_j e^z_j. The loss is categorical cross-entropy -log p_y, and the gradient with respect to the scores is again mathbf p - mathbf y_one-hot. 💡 Key idea · A neural network is stacked logistic regressions A hidden unit computes sigma(mathbf w^topmathbf x+b), exactly a logistic regression. A network feeds the outputs of many such units into the next layer, and the last layer is a softmax regression on top of features the network learned itself (module 04). ▶ Logistic Regression ▶️ Logistic Regression · StatQuest · 9 min (plays here; or open on YouTube) 4 · Generalization: underfitting and overfitting 📖 Definition · Underfitting and overfitting Underfitting (high bias): the model is too simple to capture the pattern. Training error is high. → Bigger model, better features, train longer, less regularization. Overfitting (high variance): the model memorizes noise in the training set. Training error is low but validation error is much higher. → More data, data augmentation, regularization, early stopping, smaller model. 🧮 Formula · Bias–variance decomposition (squared loss) Imagine retraining the model on many different training sets drawn from the same source. At a point mathbf x with true function f and noise variance sigma^2: mathbb Ebig[(y-hat f(mathbf x))^2big] = underbracebig(mathbb Ehat f(mathbf x)-f(mathbf x)big)^2_bias^2 + underbraceVarbig(hat f(mathbf x)big)_variance + underbracesigma^2_noise Bias: how far the average fit is from the truth. Variance: how much the fit wobbles from one training set to another. Noise: irreducible; no model beats it. ✍️ Worked example · Polynomial degree Fit polynomials to 20 noisy points from a sine curve. Degree 1: a line, can't bend → high bias, train and validation error both high. Degree 3: follows the curve → both low. Degree 15: wiggles through every point → train error ≈ 0, validation error huge (high variance). Plot validation error against degree and you get the classic U-curve . 📝 Note · Deep nets and double descent Very large neural networks can fit their training data perfectly and generalize well, and test error can drop again past the point of perfect fit ( double descent , module 12). The diagnosis above (compare train and validation error) still guides day-to-day practice. 🎛️ Try it · Generalization Lab Slide the polynomial degree and the number of training points. Watch the train and validation curves split apart as the model overfits, and close up again with more data or regularization. ▶ Machine Learning Fundamentals: Bias and Variance ▶️ Machine Learning Fundamentals: Bias and Variance · StatQuest · 6 min (plays here; or open on YouTube) 5 · Regularization Regularization means anything that discourages the model from fitting noise, usually by preferring \"simpler\" parameters. 🧮 Formula · L2 (ridge) and L1 (lasso) L2:  min_mathbf w\\;loss + lambda\\|mathbf w\\|_2^2 qquadqquad L1:  min_mathbf w\\;loss + lambda\\|mathbf w\\|_1 - L2 shrinks all weights smoothly toward zero. It equals a Gaussian prior on the weights (MAP, module 01). In neural nets it is called weight decay . - L1 drives many weights to exactly zero, which selects features (a Laplace prior). - lambda trades fit for simplicity: larger lambda → more bias, less variance. Choose it on the validation set. 📖 Definition · Early stopping Watch the validation loss during training and keep the parameters from the epoch where it was lowest. For gradient descent from small weights this acts much like L2 regularization: training longer lets the weights grow. ⚠️ Common mistake · Don't regularize the bias term Penalizing b just drags predictions toward 0 for no reason. Libraries exclude it by default; if you write your own, exclude it too. ▶ Regularization Part 1: Ridge (L2) Regression ▶️ Regularization Part 1: Ridge (L2) Regression · StatQuest · 20 min (plays here; or open on YouTube) 6 · Honest evaluation: cross-validation and leakage 📖 Definition · k-fold cross-validation Split the (non-test) data into k equal folds. Train on k-1 folds, validate on the remaining one, and rotate so each fold is the validation set once. Average the k scores. It gives a more reliable estimate than one split when data is small, at k times the cost. k=5 or 10 is typical. 📖 Definition · Data leakage Leakage is any way information about the validation/test data (or the future) gets into training. It produces scores that look great and collapse in the real world. It is the #1 source of fake results. Common causes: fitting the scaler or feature selection on all the data before splitting; the same patient/user in train and test; random splits of time-ordered data; duplicated examples across splits. ✍️ Worked example · Leakage you won't notice You standardize features with the mean/std of the whole dataset, then split. The validation rows influenced the scaling, so the validation score is slightly optimistic. With feature selection on all data (\"keep the 20 features most correlated with y\") the effect can be huge: on pure noise you can get well-above-chance CV accuracy. Fix: put every fitted step inside the CV loop ( sklearn.pipeline.Pipeline ). flowchart LR D[All data] --> Ssplit first S --> TR[train] S --> VA[validation] S --> TE[test: touch once] TR --> F[\"fit scaler / features / model\"] F -->|transform only| VA F -->|transform only| TE VA --> H[choose hyperparameters] 💡 Key idea · Always beat a baseline Before any model, compute a trivial baseline: predict the training mean (regression) or the majority class (classification). A model that can't beat it has learned nothing. 7 · Metrics: is the model actually good? Accuracy (fraction correct) is misleading when classes are imbalanced. For a binary problem, count the four kinds of outcome. 📖 Definition · Confusion matrix predicted positive predicted negative actually positive true positive (TP) false negative (FN) actually negative false positive (FP) true negative (TN) 🧮 Formula · Precision, recall, F1 precision = fracTPTP+FPqquad recall = fracTPTP+FNqquad F_1 = frac2cdotprecisioncdotrecallprecision+recall Precision: of the cases you flagged, how many were real? Recall (sensitivity, true-positive rate): of the real cases, how many did you catch? F1 is their harmonic mean, which is low if either is low. ✍️ Worked example · The accuracy trap, in numbers 1000 patients, 10 sick. A model flags 30 people, 8 of them sick: TP = 8, FN = 2, FP = 22, TN = 968. Accuracy =976/1000=97.6\\%, which is worse than \"everyone is healthy\" (99%). But recall =8/10=80\\%, precision =8/30approx27\\%, F1 =2cdot0.27cdot0.8/(0.27+0.8)approx0.40. For screening, this model is useful and the baseline is useless. Accuracy hides that. 📖 Definition · Thresholds, ROC curve and AUC A classifier outputs a score; you choose a threshold above which you say \"positive\". Lowering it catches more positives (recall ↑) but raises false alarms. The ROC curve plots the true-positive rate against the false-positive rate fracFPFP+TN for every threshold. ROC-AUC (area under it) = the probability that a random positive gets a higher score than a random negative. 1.0 is perfect, 0.5 is random guessing. It ignores the threshold and the class balance. The precision–recall curve (and its area, average precision ) is more informative when positives are rare, because precision reacts to the number of false positives among many negatives. 🧮 Formula · Regression metrics MSE = frac1Nsum(hat y_i-y_i)^2qquad MAE = frac1Nsum|hat y_i-y_i|qquad R^2 = 1-fracsum(hat y_i-y_i)^2sum(y_i-bar y)^2 R^2=1 is a perfect fit; R^2=0 means no better than predicting the mean. 🎛️ Try it · Generalization Lab · threshold simulator Move the decision threshold and watch the confusion matrix, the ROC point and the PR point move together. Then make the positive class rare and see AUC stay put while precision drops. ▶ ROC and AUC, Clearly Explained! ▶️ ROC and AUC, Clearly Explained! · StatQuest · 16 min (plays here; or open on YouTube) 8 · The classical toolbox (know what they are) You don't need to master these, but you should recognize them and know when they win. 📖 Definition · Classical models at a glance Model Idea Good for k-nearest neighbours predict from the k closest training points baselines, small low-dim data decision tree a flowchart of feature thresholds interpretable rules random forest average many trees on random subsets strong default, little tuning gradient boosting (XGBoost, LightGBM) add small trees that fix the previous ones' errors medium-sized tabular data SVM the widest-margin separating boundary, kernels for curves small, clean datasets k-means group points around k centroids clustering (no labels) PCA project onto the top singular vectors (module 01) compression, visualization 💡 Key idea · Tabular data is different Boosted trees are still the default for medium-sized tabular data ( Grinsztajn et al., 2022 ), although tabular foundation models such as TabPFN (Nature, 2025) now match or beat them on small datasets. Deep learning dominates images, audio, text and other unstructured data. Practice (answers hidden) Train error 2%, validation error 15%. Train error 18%, validation error 19% (human-level ≈ 2%). Diagnose each and give two fixes. Answer First: high variance (overfitting). More data, augmentation, regularization (weight decay, dropout), early stopping, or a smaller model. Second: high bias (underfitting; the gap to human level is 16 points). Bigger model, better features, train longer, less regularization. Prove that ROC-AUC doesn't change if you apply any strictly increasing function to the scores (e.g. sigmoid, or ×10). Answer AUC =P(s(x^+)>s(x^-)) depends only on the ranking of scores, and a strictly increasing g preserves every pairwise comparison. (So AUC also says nothing about calibration.) Why can't you get the logistic-regression weights in closed form like linear regression? Answer Setting X^top(sigma(Xmathbf w)-mathbf y)=0 gives equations that are nonlinear in mathbf w (through sigma). You solve them iteratively: gradient descent, or Newton's method (= IRLS), which uses the Hessian X^top SX with S=diag(p_i(1-p_i)). You have 500 labelled examples. Describe 5-fold CV for choosing the L2 strength lambda, then how you report the final number. Answer First set aside a test set (e.g. 100). On the remaining 400: for each lambda on a log grid, train on 4 folds and validate on the 5th, rotating through all 5, and average the 5 scores. Pick the best lambda, retrain on all 400, and evaluate once on the test set. (Or use nested CV if the data are too few for a test set.) A time-series model predicts tomorrow's sales. You shuffle the rows and do 5-fold CV, and it looks great. What's wrong? Answer Leakage from the future: training folds contain days after the validation days. Use a forward-chaining split (train on the past, validate on the next block), e.g. TimeSeriesSplit . A spam filter has precision 0.95 and recall 0.60. What does each number mean for the user, and which would you raise for an email client? Answer Precision 0.95: 5% of emails sent to spam are actually legitimate. Recall 0.60: 40% of spam still reaches the inbox. Losing a real email is worse than seeing some spam, so an email client usually keeps precision high and accepts lower recall. Compute sigma(0), sigma(2) and sigma'(0). What is the largest possible value of sigma'? Answer sigma(0)=0.5, sigma(2)=1/(1+e^-2)approx0.881, sigma'(0)=0.5cdot0.5=0.25. Since p(1-p)le 1/4, the maximum is 0.25, which is one reason deep stacks of sigmoids have vanishing gradients (module 04). Build Logistic regression from scratch in NumPy (gradient descent, no sklearn) on a binary dataset. Plot the loss curve and the decision boundary. Same dataset with sklearn : compare against yours, then run 5-fold cross-validation and a grid search over the regularization strength. A Kaggle \"Getting Started\" competition (Titanic or House Prices): full pipeline with a gradient-boosting baseline. Traps Celebrating 99% accuracy on a 99%-majority dataset. Fitting the scaler on all the data before splitting (leakage). Skipping baselines. Always compare against a trivial baseline (predict the mean or the majority class). Reporting the validation score you tuned on as your final result. It's optimistically biased. Report the untouched test score. Random splits on grouped or time-ordered data (same patient in train and val, future in train). Split by group or by time. Go deeper (all optional) ⭐ Machine Learning Specialization (Andrew Ng, Coursera) : courses 1–2. Coursera's free-audit and financial-aid options change over time, so check the course page. ⭐ (alt) An Introduction to Statistical Learning, with Python (ISLP) : free PDF, ch. 2–6, 8, 10, 12. Stanford CS229 lecture notes : the math-heavy version (GLMs, the exponential family). scikit-learn user guide : the practical API and a great reference. Google ML Crash Course : quick, interactive refresher. StatQuest : short, friendly videos on every classical ML idea (several are embedded above). Done when Scratch and sklearn logistic regression agree · you can explain every number on your confusion matrix"},{"t":"04 · Neural Networks from Scratch","u":"deep-learning/04-neural-nets-from-scratch.html","s":"Deep Learning","h":[{"n":"Skip test","id":"skip-test"},{"n":"1 · Neurons, layers and the forward pass","id":"1-neurons-layers-and-the-forward-pass"},{"n":"2 · Activation functions","id":"2-activation-functions"},{"n":"3 · What a network can represent","id":"3-what-a-network-can-represent"},{"n":"4 · Computational graphs and the chain rule","id":"4-computational-graphs-and-the-chain-rule"},{"n":"5 · Backprop for a whole MLP","id":"5-backprop-for-a-whole-mlp"},{"n":"6 · Automatic differentiation","id":"6-automatic-differentiation"},{"n":"7 · Initialization","id":"7-initialization"},{"n":"Practice (answers hidden)","id":"practice-answers-hidden"},{"n":"Build","id":"build"},{"n":"Traps","id":"traps"},{"n":"Go deeper (all optional)","id":"go-deeper-all-optional"},{"n":"Done when","id":"done-when"}],"x":"04 · Neural Networks from Scratch Module 04 ⏱ 13 min read View Markdown source Goal: understand backprop so well you could build PyTorch's core yourself. · Time: 15–20 h · Needs: 01, 03 The most important module. Everything after this is architecture choices on top of the same engine. 🎯 What you'll learn By the end of this chapter you can: write the forward pass of a multi-layer perceptron (MLP) in matrix form, with every shape; explain what activations do, and why sigmoid stacks stop learning (vanishing gradients); run backpropagation by hand on a computational graph and derive the gradients of a whole MLP; explain reverse-mode autodiff, gradient checking, and why initialization scale matters (LeCun, Xavier, He); train a NumPy MLP on MNIST to ≥ 97% accuracy. Skip test Draw a 2-layer MLP. Write the forward pass in matrix form with shapes. Derive backprop for Linear → ReLU → Linear → softmax-cross-entropy by hand. Show that partial L/partial mathbf z = hatmathbf y - mathbf y for softmax + cross-entropy. Explain why stacked linear layers without nonlinearities collapse to one linear layer. Explain reverse-mode autodiff and why it suits \"many params → one scalar loss\". Explain vanishing gradients with sigmoid, and why ReLU helps. Say what the initial loss of a 10-class classifier should be, and derive the 2 in He initialization. 🎯 Not sure? Take the auto-graded Foundations Quiz (filter by module) before deciding. 1 · Neurons, layers and the forward pass 📖 Definition · Neuron A neuron (unit) takes inputs mathbf x, computes a weighted sum plus a bias, and passes it through an activation function phi: a = phi(mathbf w^topmathbf x + b) z=mathbf w^topmathbf x+b is the pre-activation ; a is the activation . With phi=sigma this is exactly logistic regression (module 03). 📖 Definition · Layer and multi-layer perceptron (MLP) A dense (fully connected) layer is many neurons side by side, all reading the same input: mathbf h = phi(Wmathbf x+mathbf b), qquad Winmathbb R^Htimes D,\\ mathbf binmathbb R^H An MLP stacks layers: the output of one is the input of the next. The last layer produces logits (raw scores), which a softmax turns into class probabilities. The layers in between are hidden layers ; their size H is the width , their count the depth . 🧮 Formula · Forward pass of a 2-layer MLP (batch form) For a batch X of N examples with D features, H hidden units and C classes: Z_1 = XW_1^top + mathbf b_1\\;(Ntimes H)quad A_1 = ReLU(Z_1)quad Z_2 = A_1W_2^top + mathbf b_2\\;(Ntimes C)quad P = softmax(Z_2) L = -frac1Nsum_n=1^Nlog P_n,\\,y_n Parameter count: W_1 has Hcdot D, mathbf b_1 has H, W_2 has Ccdot H, mathbf b_2 has C. 💡 Key idea · Why nonlinearity is essential Without phi, two layers give W_2(W_1mathbf x) = (W_2W_1)mathbf x: still one linear map, no matter how many layers you stack. The nonlinearity lets each layer bend the space, so the network can carve out curved decision boundaries and compose simple features into complex ones. ✍️ Worked example · Counting parameters MNIST: D=784 pixels, H=128, C=10. W_1: 128cdot784=100\\,352; mathbf b_1: 128; W_2: 10cdot128=1\\,280; mathbf b_2: 10. Total 101 770 parameters. ▶ But what is a neural network? ▶️ But what is a neural network? · 3Blue1Brown · 19 min (chapter 1 of the series) (plays here; or open on YouTube) 2 · Activation functions 🧮 Formula · Common activations and their derivatives Name phi(z) phi'(z) Notes sigmoid frac11+e^-z sigma(z)(1-sigma(z))lefrac14 saturates at both ends tanh frace^z-e^-ze^z+e^-z 1-tanh^2 zle1 zero-centred, still saturates ReLU max(0,z) 1 if z>0, else 0 cheap, doesn't saturate for z>0 Leaky ReLU max(alpha z, z), e.g. alpha=0.01 1 or alpha never fully \"dies\" GELU z\\,Phi(z) (Phi = normal CDF) smooth used in transformers (BERT, GPT) SiLU / Swish z\\,sigma(z) smooth used in many modern nets (e.g. Llama's SwiGLU) 📖 Definition · Saturation and vanishing gradients An activation saturates where it is flat (sigmoid for large |z|). There its derivative is ≈ 0, so almost no gradient flows back. Backprop multiplies one such factor per layer, so with many layers the gradient reaching the first layers shrinks exponentially: vanishing gradients . (If the factors are > 1 it grows exponentially instead: exploding gradients .) ⚠️ Common mistake · Dead ReLUs If a ReLU unit's pre-activation is negative for every input, its gradient is always 0 and it never recovers. A large learning rate or a big negative bias can kill many units. Leaky ReLU, GELU or careful initialization reduce the risk. 3 · What a network can represent 📖 Definition · Universal approximation theorem One hidden layer that is wide enough, with any non-polynomial activation, can approximate any continuous function on a bounded region as closely as you like (Cybenko 1989; Hornik 1991; Leshno et al. 1993). 💡 Key idea · Why depth anyway? The theorem says nothing about how many units you need or whether gradient descent will find the right weights. Depth makes representation efficient : some functions need exponentially many units in a shallow net but only a few in a deep one (e.g. Telgarsky 2016). Deep nets also build features in stages: edges → textures → parts → objects. 🎛️ Try it · Neural Network Playground Train a tiny MLP on 2D datasets (moons, spirals, circles). Start with 0 hidden layers (a straight boundary), then add units and layers and watch the boundary bend. Switch activations and compare. 4 · Computational graphs and the chain rule To train, we need partial L/partialtheta for every parameter. Doing that by hand for millions of parameters is impossible; backpropagation does it automatically by breaking the computation into small steps. 📖 Definition · Computational graph Write the computation as a graph: each node is one simple operation (add, multiply, ReLU, matmul…), and edges carry values. The forward pass computes every node's value from its inputs and caches it. The backward pass sends gradients from the loss back through the graph. 🧮 Formula · The backprop rule at one node If a node computes y = f(x_1,dots,x_k) and you already know the upstream gradient bar y = partial L/partial y, then for each input bar x_i mathrel+= bar ycdotfracpartial fpartial x_i (for vectors: barmathbf x mathrel+= J^topbarmathbf y, a vector–Jacobian product). Every node only needs its local derivative and the upstream gradient. Process nodes in reverse topological order, starting from bar L = 1. 💡 Key idea · Gradients accumulate If a value feeds into several places, its gradient is the sum of the gradients from each use (the multivariable chain rule). That's why implementations use += , and why you must zero the gradients before each training step. ✍️ Worked example · Backprop through a single neuron, by hand L = (sigma(wx+b) - y)^2 with w=2, x=1, b=-1, y=1. Forward: z = 2cdot1-1 = 1; a=sigma(1)approx0.731; L=(0.731-1)^2approx0.072. Backward: bar a = 2(a-y) approx -0.538; bar z = bar acdot a(1-a)approx -0.538cdot0.197approx-0.106; bar w = bar zcdot x approx -0.106; bar b = bar z approx -0.106; bar x = bar zcdot wapprox-0.212. Both bar w and bar b are negative, so a gradient step increases w and b, raising a toward the target 1 ✓. 🧮 Formula · Local gradients you'll use constantly Operation Backward rule y = a+b bar a mathrel+= bar y, bar b mathrel+= bar y (copies the gradient) y = acdot b bar a mathrel+= b\\,bar y, bar b mathrel+= a\\,bar y (swaps the inputs) y = ReLU(z) bar z mathrel+= bar ycdotmathbb 1[z>0] (a gate) y=sigma(z) bar z mathrel+= bar ycdot y(1-y) y = e^z bar z mathrel+= bar ycdot y Y = XW^top + mathbf b bar W = bar Y^top X, bar X = bar YW, barmathbf b = sum_n bar Y_n,: softmax + cross-entropy barmathbf z = hatmathbf y-mathbf y (per example) 🎛️ Try it · Backprop Step-Through Step through the forward and backward pass node by node on four small graphs (a neuron, a shared variable, a vanishing chain, softmax-CE), and run the built-in gradient check. ▶ The spelled-out intro to neural networks and backpropagation: building micrograd ▶️ The spelled-out intro to neural networks and backpropagation: building micrograd · Andrej Karpathy · 2 h 25 min. Code along. (plays here; or open on YouTube) 5 · Backprop for a whole MLP Put the local rules together for the 2-layer MLP from section 1. flowchart LR X[\"X (N×D)\"] --> L1[\"Linear W1, b1\"] --> H1[\"Z1 (N×H)\"] --> R[ReLU] --> A1[\"A1 (N×H)\"] --> L2[\"Linear W2, b2\"] --> Z2[\"logits (N×C)\"] --> SM[\"softmax + CE\"] --> Loss((L)) Loss -. \"dZ2 = (P − Y)/N\" .-> Z2 Z2 -. \"dW2 = dZ2ᵀA1, dA1 = dZ2 W2\" .-> A1 A1 -. \"dZ1 = dA1 ⊙ 1[Z1 > 0]\" .-> H1 H1 -. \"dW1 = dZ1ᵀX\" .-> X Solid arrows: forward pass (values cached). Dotted arrows: backward pass, right to left. Every arrow only needs the cached values next to it. 🧮 Formula · The full backward pass With Y the one-hot labels (Ntimes C): bar Z_2 = tfrac1N(P-Y)quad bar W_2 = bar Z_2^top A_1quad barmathbf b_2=textstylesum_nbar Z_2quad bar A_1 = bar Z_2W_2 bar Z_1 = bar A_1odotmathbb 1[Z_1>0]quad bar W_1 = bar Z_1^top Xquad barmathbf b_1=textstylesum_nbar Z_1 (odot = element-wise product.) Then update every parameter: W leftarrow W - etabar W. ✍️ Worked example · Softmax + cross-entropy gives hatmathbf y-mathbf y p_i=e^z_i/sum_k e^z_k, L=-sum_i y_ilog p_i with one-hot mathbf y. Since log p_i = z_i-logsum_k e^z_k, L = -sum_i y_i z_i + logsum_k e^z_k (using sum_i y_i=1). So fracpartial Lpartial z_j = -y_j + frace^z_jsum_k e^z_k = p_j - y_j. Numbers: mathbf z=(2, 0.5), true class 1 → p_1=e^2/(e^2+e^0.5)approx0.818, so nabla_mathbf zLapprox(-0.182,\\ +0.182). (This is the \"softmax\" graph in the step-through tool.) ✍️ Worked example · Linear-layer gradients by the shape trick Batch form Y = XW^top+mathbf b with X\\,(Ntimes D), W\\,(Htimes D), Y\\,(Ntimes H), upstream G=partial L/partial Y\\,(Ntimes H). partial L/partial W must be Htimes D, and the only product of G and X with that shape is G^top X. partial L/partial X must be Ntimes D: GW. partial L/partialmathbf b must be H: sum_n G_n,: (sum over the batch, because mathbf b was broadcast). Verified numerically with finite differences ✓. 💻 In code · A NumPy training step # forward Z1 = X @ W1.T + b1; A1 = np.maximum(Z1, 0) Z2 = A1 @ W2.T + b2 Z2 -= Z2.max(1, keepdims=True); P = np.exp(Z2); P /= P.sum(1, keepdims=True) loss = -np.log(P[np.arange(N), y]).mean() # backward dZ2 = P.copy(); dZ2[np.arange(N), y] -= 1; dZ2 /= N dW2 = dZ2.T @ A1; db2 = dZ2.sum(0); dA1 = dZ2 @ W2 dZ1 = dA1 * (Z1 > 0) dW1 = dZ1.T @ X; db1 = dZ1.sum(0) # update for p, g in [(W1, dW1), (b1, db1), (W2, dW2), (b2, db2)]: p -= lr * g 🎛️ Try it · Neural Network Playground Watch the loss fall and the decision boundary form as this exact loop runs. Raise the learning rate until training becomes unstable. 6 · Automatic differentiation 📖 Definition · Reverse-mode and forward-mode autodiff Automatic differentiation (autodiff) applies the local rules above mechanically to any program. Reverse mode (backprop): one backward pass gives the gradient of one scalar output with respect to all inputs, at about 2–3× the cost of the forward pass, whatever the number of parameters. Perfect for \"millions of parameters → one loss\". Forward mode: carries derivatives forward alongside values; one pass gives the derivative of all outputs along one input direction. Better when there are few inputs and many outputs. PyTorch, JAX and TensorFlow build the graph as your code runs and use reverse mode for .backward() . 💡 Key idea · Why billion-parameter training is possible Because reverse mode's cost doesn't grow with the number of parameters, computing the gradient of a 70-billion-parameter model costs only a few forward passes, not 70 billion of them. 📖 Definition · Gradient checking Compare each analytic gradient with a numerical estimate fracL(theta+h)-L(theta-h)2h (central difference, happrox10^-5, float64 ). Report the relative error frac|g_a-g_n|max(|g_a|,|g_n|): around 10^-7 or smaller is correct; 10^-2 means a bug. Check a few random entries of every parameter. 7 · Initialization Before training, the weights are random. Their scale decides whether signals survive the trip through many layers. 🧮 Formula · Variance of a pre-activation For z=sum_i=1^n_in w_ix_i with independent zero-mean weights: Var(z) = n_in\\,Var(w)\\,mathbb E[x^2] To keep the signal's scale constant from layer to layer, choose Var(w) to cancel the factor n_in. 📖 Definition · LeCun, Xavier/Glorot and He/Kaiming initialization Scheme Var(w) Use with LeCun 1/n_in SELU, or linear/tanh as a simple default Xavier / Glorot 2/(n_in+n_out) tanh, sigmoid: a compromise between the forward and backward pass He / Kaiming 2/n_in ReLU and its variants Biases are usually initialized to 0. ✍️ Worked example · Why He init has the 2 With Var(w)=2/n and ReLU inputs (mathbb E[x^2]=tfrac12Var(z_prev), because ReLU zeroes half of a symmetric input), Var(z)=ncdottfrac2ncdottfrac12Var(z_prev)=Var(z_prev): the signal keeps its scale through any depth. A quick simulation (n = 512, unit-variance pre-activations) gives Var(z)approx1.02 ✓. With 1/n instead, the variance halves at every layer: after 20 layers it is 2^-20approx10^-6. 💡 Key idea · Sanity-check the initial loss With small random weights the network predicts roughly uniform probabilities, so the initial cross-entropy should be about ln C: 2.30 for 10 classes. A much larger value means the logits are too big at init (weights too large, or unnormalized inputs). Practice (answers hidden) A freshly initialized 10-class classifier reports an initial loss of 9.7. What should it be, and what's likely wrong? Answer About ln 10approx2.30 (uniform predictions). 9.7 means the logits are far too large at init, so the net is confidently wrong. Typical causes: weights too big (wrong init scale), or unnormalized inputs. Fix: scale the last layer's weights down (e.g. ×0.01) and standardize inputs. (Karpathy's \"makemore part 3\" covers exactly this.) Derive sigma'(z) and show that a stack of 10 sigmoid layers multiplies the gradient by at most 0.25^10approx10^-6 (ignoring weights). Answer sigma'(z)=sigma(z)(1-sigma(z))letfrac14, with the max at z=0. Each layer's backward pass multiplies by sigma'(z_ell) (times a weight), so 10 layers give at most 4^-10approx9.5times10^-7 unless the weights are large. ReLU's derivative is exactly 1 on the active side, which is why it trains deep nets better. In micrograd, c = a + a; c.backward() . What must a.grad be, and which bug gives 1 instead? Answer partial c/partial a=2. Writing self.grad = out.grad instead of self.grad += out.grad in __add__ 's backward overwrites the first contribution with the second, giving 1. Your NumPy MLP's gradient check gives relative error 10^-2 for W_1 but 10^-9 for W_2. Where is the bug, most likely? Answer Between W_2 and W_1 in the backward pass: the ReLU mask ( dZ1 = dA1 * (Z1 > 0) ), a missing transpose in dA1 = dZ2 @ W2 , or forgetting to divide by N consistently. Also check that the check isn't landing exactly on a ReLU kink (use float64 and a few random points). Why is reverse mode the right choice for training, and when would forward mode win? Answer Reverse mode gives the gradient of one output w.r.t. all n inputs in one backward pass (≈ a small constant × the forward cost). Forward mode gives the derivative of all outputs w.r.t. one input direction per pass, so a full gradient would take n passes. Forward mode wins when there are few inputs and many outputs (e.g. a Jacobian-vector product for a handful of directions). In the single-neuron example (section 4), change the target to y=0. What are the signs of bar w and bar b now, and why does that make sense? Answer bar a = 2(0.731-0) approx 1.462 > 0, so bar z = 1.462cdot0.197approx0.288 and bar w=bar bapprox0.288 > 0. A gradient step now decreases w and b, lowering a toward the new target 0 ✓. How many parameters does an MLP 784to256to256to10 have? Answer (784cdot256+256)+(256cdot256+256)+(256cdot10+10) = 200\\,960+65\\,792+2\\,570 = 269\\,322. Build micrograd : a scalar autograd engine (~150 lines) plus a tiny MLP that learns a 2D dataset (moons). Follow Karpathy, then rewrite it from memory . NumPy MLP on MNIST with hand-derived matrix backprop. Target ≥ 97% test accuracy. Gradient-check every layer. 💡 C++ bonus: port micrograd to C++ (a Value class with shared_ptr children and a topological sort). It cements the graph idea and uses your STL skills. Traps \"I understand it from the video.\" You don't until you've written it from a blank file. Forgetting to zero gradients between steps (they accumulate). Using float32 for gradient checks. Use float64. Computing softmax then log separately. Use log-softmax / log-sum-exp, or exp overflows and log(0) gives -inf . Forgetting that the bias gradient sums over the batch ( db = dY.sum(axis=0) ). Go deeper (all optional) ⭐ Neural Networks: Zero to Hero (Andrej Karpathy) : videos 1–5 (micrograd → makemore with MLPs, BatchNorm, \"Becoming a Backprop Ninja\"). Code along with every line. The best single resource in the whole curriculum. 3Blue1Brown: Neural Networks : the intuition (~1.5 h). The full series: ▶ Playlist · Neural networks (series) ▶️ Neural networks (series) · 3Blue1Brown · gradient descent, backpropagation, backprop calculus (plays here; or open on YouTube) Neural Networks and Deep Learning (Michael Nielsen) : free book. Ch. 1–3 for a clear backprop derivation. CS231n: Backprop notes + Yes you should understand backprop . Reference code: karpathy/micrograd (compare after you've written your own). Done when micrograd rewritten from memory · NumPy MLP ≥ 97% on MNIST · all skip-test derivations done on paper"},{"t":"05 · PyTorch & the Craft of Training","u":"deep-learning/05-pytorch-training.html","s":"Deep Learning","h":[{"n":"Skip test","id":"skip-test"},{"n":"1 · Tensors and autograd","id":"1-tensors-and-autograd"},{"n":"2 · Models, data and the training loop","id":"2-models-data-and-the-training-loop"},{"n":"3 · Optimizers","id":"3-optimizers"},{"n":"4 · The learning rate and its schedule","id":"4-the-learning-rate-and-its-schedule"},{"n":"5 · Normalization and residual connections","id":"5-normalization-and-residual-connections"},{"n":"6 · Regularization in deep nets","id":"6-regularization-in-deep-nets"},{"n":"7 · Debugging: when training goes wrong","id":"7-debugging-when-training-goes-wrong"},{"n":"8 · Speed and memory","id":"8-speed-and-memory"},{"n":"Practice (answers hidden)","id":"practice-answers-hidden"},{"n":"Build","id":"build"},{"n":"Traps","id":"traps"},{"n":"Go deeper (all optional)","id":"go-deeper-all-optional"},{"n":"Done when","id":"done-when"}],"x":"05 · PyTorch & the Craft of Training Module 05 ⏱ 13 min read View Markdown source Goal: train any model reliably, and know what to do when it doesn't train. · Time: 20–30 h · Needs: 04 🎯 What you'll learn By the end of this chapter you can: write a complete PyTorch training loop from memory (tensors, autograd, nn.Module , DataLoader , optimizer); choose and explain SGD + momentum, Adam and AdamW , and design a learning-rate schedule ; explain BatchNorm, LayerNorm, RMSNorm , residual connections, dropout and label smoothing; debug a model that won't train (the NaN checklist, overfitting one batch, reading loss curves); speed training up with mixed precision, gradient clipping and gradient accumulation. Skip test Write a full training loop from memory: Dataset / DataLoader , nn.Module , loss, optimizer, zero_grad → backward → step , eval with torch.no_grad() + model.eval() . Explain SGD + momentum vs Adam vs AdamW (and why decoupled weight decay matters). Explain what BatchNorm and LayerNorm normalize over, and the train/eval difference for BatchNorm. Explain why a LR warmup + cosine decay schedule is standard. Your loss is NaN at step 200. List five things to check. Explain mixed precision (bf16/fp16) and gradient clipping. Write the Adam update from memory, and explain why plain Adam + L2 is not the same as AdamW. 1 · Tensors and autograd PyTorch is NumPy (module 02) plus two things: arrays that can live on a GPU , and automatic differentiation (module 04) built in. 📖 Definition · Tensor A torch.Tensor is an n-dimensional array with a shape , a dtype (default float32 ) and a device ( \"cpu\" or \"cuda\" ). Almost every NumPy operation has a PyTorch twin: x.sum(dim=0) , x @ y , x.view(...) , broadcasting works the same way. 📖 Definition · Autograd If a tensor has requires_grad=True , PyTorch records every operation on it into a computational graph. Calling .backward() on a scalar result runs reverse-mode autodiff and adds the gradient into each leaf tensor's .grad . 💻 In code · Autograd in four lines w = torch.tensor([2.0], requires_grad=True) x, y = torch.tensor([1.0]), torch.tensor([1.0]) loss = (torch.sigmoid(w * x - 1) - y) ** 2 loss.backward(); print(w.grad) # tensor([-0.1058]), the single-neuron example from module 04 ⚠️ Common mistake · Things that silently cut the graph .item() , .detach() , .numpy() , and doing math in NumPy or Python floats all produce values with no gradient history. If a parameter's .grad stays None after backward() , one of these is usually why. 2 · Models, data and the training loop 📖 Definition · nn.Module The base class for every layer and model. Create sub-layers in __init__ and describe the computation in forward . Any nn.Parameter or sub-module you assign as an attribute is registered automatically, so model.parameters() finds them all. class MLP(nn.Module): def __init__(self, d_in, d_hidden, n_classes): super().__init__() self.net = nn.Sequential(nn.Linear(d_in, d_hidden), nn.ReLU(), nn.Linear(d_hidden, n_classes)) def forward(self, x): return self.net(x) # returns logits 📖 Definition · Dataset and DataLoader A Dataset knows how many examples there are ( __len__ ) and how to fetch example i ( __getitem__ ). A DataLoader wraps it to produce shuffled mini-batches, optionally using several worker processes to load data in parallel. 💻 In code · The canonical training loop device = \"cuda\" if torch.cuda.is_available() else \"cpu\" model = MLP(784, 256, 10).to(device) opt = torch.optim.AdamW(model.parameters(), lr=1e-3, weight_decay=0.01) loss_fn = nn.CrossEntropyLoss() # expects logits train_dl = DataLoader(train_ds, batch_size=128, shuffle=True) for epoch in range(10): model.train() # dropout/BatchNorm in training mode for x, y in train_dl: x, y = x.to(device), y.to(device) loss = loss_fn(model(x), y) opt.zero_grad(set_to_none=True) # clear old gradients loss.backward() # compute new ones opt.step() # update the parameters model.eval() # inference mode for dropout/BatchNorm with torch.no_grad(): # no graph: faster, less memory correct = sum((model(x.to(device)).argmax(1) == y.to(device)).sum().item() for x, y in val_dl) print(epoch, correct / len(val_ds)) 💡 Key idea · train() vs eval(), and devices model.train() / model.eval() switch dropout and BatchNorm between their training and inference behaviour. torch.no_grad() separately turns off graph building. The model and every batch must be on the same device: most \"Expected all tensors to be on the same device\" errors come from forgetting one .to(device) . 📝 Note · torch.compile model = torch.compile(model) often speeds training up with a one-line change by fusing operations into optimized GPU kernels. The first few steps are slow while it compiles. 🧮 Formula · Which loss for which task Task Final layer PyTorch loss multi-class classification C logits nn.CrossEntropyLoss (log-softmax inside; pass logits ) binary / multi-label 1 logit per label nn.BCEWithLogitsLoss (sigmoid inside) regression linear output nn.MSELoss , nn.L1Loss , nn.HuberLoss 3 · Optimizers 🧮 Formula · SGD with momentum mathbf v leftarrow betamathbf v + mathbf g,qquad thetaleftarrowtheta-eta\\,mathbf v qquad(betaapprox0.9) A heavy ball rolling downhill 🔭: it smooths out zig-zags and builds speed along consistent directions. Still the strongest choice for many CNNs when well tuned. 🧮 Formula · Adam Keep running averages of the gradient and of its square, per parameter: mathbf mleftarrowbeta_1mathbf m+(1-beta_1)mathbf g,qquad mathbf vleftarrowbeta_2mathbf v+(1-beta_2)mathbf g^2 hatmathbf m=fracmathbf m1-beta_1^t,qquad hatmathbf v=fracmathbf v1-beta_2^t,qquad thetaleftarrowtheta-eta\\,frachatmathbf msqrthatmathbf v+epsilon Defaults beta_1=0.9,\\ beta_2=0.999,\\ epsilon=10^-8. Dividing by sqrthatmathbf v gives every parameter its own step size: parameters with consistently large gradients take smaller steps. The hats correct the bias from starting mathbf m,mathbf v at zero. ✍️ Worked example · Adam's first step has size ≈ η, whatever the gradient's scale Take one parameter, g_1=0.01. Then m=0.1cdot0.01=0.001, v=0.001cdot10^-4=10^-7, hat m=0.001/0.1=0.01, hat v=10^-7/0.001=10^-4, step =etacdot0.01/(0.01+10^-8)approxeta. With g_1=100 you get the same step. That's why Adam's LR is a step size in parameter units, why it needs less tuning than SGD's, and why early steps can be too big without warmup. 📖 Definition · AdamW (decoupled weight decay) thetaleftarrowtheta-etaBig(frachatmathbf msqrthatmathbf v+epsilon+lambdathetaBig) The decay is applied directly to the weights ( Loshchilov & Hutter, 2019 ). With plain Adam + an L2 term in the loss, the penalty's gradient lambdatheta gets divided by sqrthatmathbf v, so weights with large gradients are barely regularized. AdamW is the default for transformers. Don't decay biases and normalization gains. 🎛️ Try it · Optimizer Landscape Race SGD, momentum and Adam on a narrow valley and on a saddle. Notice how Adam's per-parameter scaling straightens the path. 4 · The learning rate and its schedule 💡 Key idea · The learning rate is the #1 hyperparameter If you tune only one thing, tune the LR, on a log scale (e.g. 1e-4, 3e-4, 1e-3, 3e-3). A good LR is usually a bit below the value where training becomes unstable. 🧮 Formula · Warmup + cosine decay Linear warmup for the first T_w steps, then cosine decay to eta_min at step T: eta_t = eta_maxfractT_w\\ \\ (t<T_w),qquad eta_t = eta_min+tfrac12(eta_max-eta_min)Big(1+cosfracpi(t-T_w)T-T_wBig)\\ \\ (tge T_w) Warmup avoids huge early steps while Adam's statistics are still noisy and the weights are random. Decay lets the model settle into a minimum at the end. Linear decay and \"warmup–stable–decay\" schedules are common alternatives. 📖 Definition · LR range test Train for a few hundred steps while increasing the LR exponentially from tiny to huge, and plot loss against LR. Pick a value somewhat below where the loss is falling fastest, well before it blows up. 📖 Definition · Batch size and the learning rate Larger batches mean less gradient noise, so you can take bigger steps. Rules of thumb: scale the LR linearly with batch size for SGD ( Goyal et al., 2017 ) and roughly with √(batch size) for Adam. Both stop working past a critical batch size , after which bigger batches stop speeding up training. 🎛️ Try it · LR Schedule Designer Shape a warmup + decay schedule, compare cosine, linear and step decay, and copy the generated PyTorch code. 5 · Normalization and residual connections Deep networks train much better when activations keep a sensible scale. Normalization layers enforce that explicitly. 🧮 Formula · Batch normalization For each channel, compute the mean mu_B and variance sigma_B^2 over the batch (and over spatial positions in a CNN), then hat x = fracx-mu_Bsqrtsigma_B^2+epsilon,qquad y = gammahat x + beta gamma,beta are learned, so the layer can undo the normalization if that helps. At eval time BatchNorm uses running averages of mu,sigma^2 collected during training. Common in CNNs; fragile with tiny batches. 🧮 Formula · Layer normalization and RMSNorm LayerNorm normalizes over the features of each example (token) , independently of the rest of the batch: y = gammaodotfracmathbf x-mu(mathbf x)sqrtsigma^2(mathbf x)+epsilon+beta RMSNorm skips the mean: y = mathbf godotmathbf x\\,/sqrttfrac1dsum_i x_i^2+epsilon. Both behave the same in training and eval and don't care about batch size, which is why transformers use them (module 08). 📖 Definition · Residual (skip) connection Instead of mathbf y = F(mathbf x), compute mathbf y = mathbf x + F(mathbf x). The layer only has to learn a correction to its input, and in the backward pass the gradient flows straight through the \"+\" (its local gradient is 1), giving it a highway back to early layers. This is what makes 100+ layer networks trainable (ResNets, module 06; transformers, module 08). 🎛️ Try it · Normalization Explorer Click through a batch tensor and highlight exactly which numbers BatchNorm, LayerNorm, RMSNorm and GroupNorm average over. ▶ Building makemore Part 3: Activations & Gradients, BatchNorm ▶️ Building makemore Part 3: Activations & Gradients, BatchNorm · Andrej Karpathy · 1 h 56 min (plays here; or open on YouTube) 6 · Regularization in deep nets 🧮 Formula · Dropout (inverted dropout) During training, zero each activation with probability p and scale the survivors up: tilde h = fracmcdot h1-p,qquad msimBernoulli(1-p) The scaling keeps the expected value unchanged, so at eval time dropout simply does nothing. It stops units from relying on each other and acts like averaging many thinned networks. 🧮 Formula · Label smoothing Replace the one-hot target by mathbf y' = (1-varepsilon)\\,mathbf y + varepsilon/C (e.g. varepsilon=0.1). The model is no longer pushed to infinite logits, which often improves calibration and generalization. In PyTorch: nn.CrossEntropyLoss(label_smoothing=0.1) . 📖 Definition · Other regularizers Weight decay (AdamW's lambda, section 3). Data augmentation: random crops, flips, colour jitter, mixup… new training examples for free (module 06). Early stopping: keep the checkpoint with the best validation score. 7 · Debugging: when training goes wrong 💡 Key idea · Karpathy's recipe Get to know the data: look at samples and label distributions. Build a dumb baseline, fix random seeds, check the initial loss (≈ ln C for C classes). Overfit a single batch to ~0 loss. If you can't, there's a bug. Then add regularization and scale up. Change one thing at a time . Track everything: loss curves (train and val), LR, gradient norms. Use Weights & Biases or TensorBoard. ✍️ Worked example · \"Loss is NaN at step 200\": the checklist Work through the flowchart below from the top. flowchart TD A[NaN or inf loss] --> B\"NaN in the inputs or labels?\" B -- yes --> B1[\"Fix data: assert torch.isfinite(x).all()\"] B -- no --> C\"Loss rising before the NaN?\" C -- yes --> C1[\"LR too high: lower it, add warmup, clip grads\"] C -- no --> D\"Sudden, in fp16?\" D -- yes --> D1[\"Overflow: use bf16 or a GradScaler\"] D -- no --> E[\"Unsafe op: log(0), sqrt(neg), x/0, exp(big).nUse log_softmax, add eps, torch.autograd.set_detect_anomaly(True)\"] 📖 Definition · Reading loss curves What you see Likely cause loss flat from the start LR far too small, gradients not flowing (detached tensor), or wrong labels loss explodes or oscillates wildly LR too high; add warmup or clipping train ↓, validation ↑ after a while overfitting: regularize, augment, stop early both high and close together underfitting: bigger model, train longer regular sawtooth pattern data not shuffled 🎛️ Try it · Training Doctor Diagnose 11 realistic failure modes from their loss curves alone, then check your answer. 8 · Speed and memory 📖 Definition · Mixed precision Run most of the forward and backward pass in 16-bit floats ( torch.autocast ) while keeping a float32 copy of the weights. On GPUs with tensor cores this often gives up to ~2× speed and roughly halves activation memory. Prefer bf16 : it has float32's exponent range, so it needs no loss scaling. fp16 has a tiny range (max 65 504) and needs torch.amp.GradScaler to avoid underflow and overflow. 🧮 Formula · Gradient clipping (by global norm) mathbf gleftarrowmathbf gcdotminBig(1,\\ fracc\\|mathbf g\\|Big) If the gradient's total norm exceeds c (e.g. 1.0), scale it down to length c; the direction is unchanged. It stops one bad batch from wrecking the weights. torch.nn.utils.clip_grad_norm_(model.parameters(), 1.0) . ✍️ Worked example · Gradient accumulation (effective batch = 4 × 32 = 128) accum = 4 opt.zero_grad(set_to_none=True) for i, (x, y) in enumerate(loader): # loader batch size 32 loss = loss_fn(model(x), y) / accum # average over the 4 micro-batches loss.backward() # grads add up in .grad if (i + 1) % accum == 0: torch.nn.utils.clip_grad_norm_(model.parameters(), 1.0) opt.step(); sched.step() opt.zero_grad(set_to_none=True) Not identical to a real batch of 128 if the model has BatchNorm (its statistics still see 32 at a time). 💻 In code · Checkpointing Save everything needed to resume, not just the weights: torch.save(\"model\": model.state_dict(), \"opt\": opt.state_dict(), \"sched\": sched.state_dict(), \"step\": step, \"ckpt.pt\") Practice (answers hidden) You evaluate a CNN with BatchNorm and get 10% accuracy, though training accuracy was 95%. One line fixes it. Which? Answer model.eval() before evaluating (and model.train() before training again). In train mode BatchNorm normalizes with the current test batch's statistics and dropout stays on. Also wrap evaluation in torch.no_grad() (or torch.inference_mode() ) to save memory. AdamW with lr = 1e-3 and weight_decay = 0.1. By what factor does weight decay alone shrink a weight each step? Answer PyTorch's AdamW multiplies the weight by 1-etalambda = 1-10^-4 per step. Over 10 000 steps that's (1-10^-4)^10^4approx e^-1 if nothing pushed back. The decay strength depends on the product etalambda, so changing the LR also changes the effective regularization. Your training loss is a perfectly regular sawtooth and validation loss stays above ln C. What's wrong? Answer The training data isn't shuffled (it's sorted by class): DataLoader(..., shuffle=True) . Why can't you overfit a single batch of 32 examples, and what does that tell you? Answer A healthy model + pipeline should drive the loss on 32 fixed examples to ~0 within a few hundred steps. If it can't, the bug is in the code, not the hyperparameters: labels misaligned, a detached tensor ( .detach() , .item() , NumPy round-trip) blocking gradients, wrong loss input (probabilities fed to CrossEntropyLoss , which expects logits), or the optimizer built on the wrong parameters. nn.CrossEntropyLoss()(torch.softmax(logits, -1), y) : what's wrong? Answer CrossEntropyLoss applies log-softmax internally and expects raw logits . Applying softmax first squashes the inputs to [0, 1], so gradients become tiny and training stalls. Pass logits directly. With dropout p=0.5, an activation is h=3. What values can tilde h take during training, and what is its expected value? Answer 0 (probability 0.5) or 3/0.5=6 (probability 0.5). Expected value 0.5cdot6=3=h, which is why no rescaling is needed at eval time. A cosine schedule with eta_max=10^-3, eta_min=0, no warmup and T=1000 steps. What is the LR at steps 0, 500 and 1000? Answer 10^-3cdottfrac12(1+cos 0)=10^-3; at 500: tfrac12(1+costfracpi2)cdot10^-3=5times10^-4; at 1000: tfrac12(1+cospi)cdot10^-3=0. Build Re-implement your MNIST MLP in PyTorch. Compare it to your NumPy version. Fashion-MNIST/CIFAR-10 ablation study: run SGD vs Adam vs AdamW, ± BatchNorm, ± dropout, three LR schedules. Plot everything in W&B and write a one-page conclusion. Deliberately break training (huge LR, no normalization, bad init, forgetting zero_grad ) and record what each failure looks like on the curves. Traps Forgetting model.eval() → BatchNorm/dropout are wrong at test time. Calling .item() or moving tensors to the CPU every step slows everything down. Tuning ten things at once. You learn nothing from that. Passing probabilities (after softmax) to CrossEntropyLoss , which expects logits. Calling sched.step() per epoch for a schedule defined in steps (or vice versa). Plot the LR you actually used. Go deeper (all optional) ⭐ Dive into Deep Learning (d2l.ai) : free, PyTorch, math + code. Ch. 2–5, 8 (modern CNNs), 12 (optimization). ⭐ A Recipe for Training Neural Networks (Karpathy) : read twice. Understanding Deep Learning (Simon Prince, 2023) : free, beautifully illustrated, and strong on theory. Ch. 6–9 (training, init, regularization). PyTorch: Learn the Basics + Zero to Mastery PyTorch . Deep Learning Tuning Playbook (Google) : a systematic way to tune hyperparameters. Alt. top-down style: fast.ai Practical Deep Learning . Great if you want quick wins before theory. Done when Training loop written from memory · ablation report done · you recognize failure modes from the loss curves alone"},{"t":"06 · CNNs & Computer Vision","u":"deep-learning/06-cnns-vision.html","s":"Deep Learning","h":[{"n":"Skip test","id":"skip-test"},{"n":"1 · Why not just use an MLP on images?","id":"1-why-not-just-use-an-mlp-on-images"},{"n":"2 · The properties that make CNNs work","id":"2-the-properties-that-make-cnns-work"},{"n":"3 · Architectures: the ideas that mattered","id":"3-architectures-the-ideas-that-mattered"},{"n":"4 · Data augmentation and transfer learning","id":"4-data-augmentation-and-transfer-learning"},{"n":"5 · Beyond classification","id":"5-beyond-classification"},{"n":"Practice (answers hidden)","id":"practice-answers-hidden"},{"n":"Build","id":"build"},{"n":"Traps","id":"traps"},{"n":"Go deeper (all optional)","id":"go-deeper-all-optional"},{"n":"Done when","id":"done-when"}],"x":"06 · CNNs & Computer Vision Module 06 ⏱ 9 min read View Markdown source Goal: understand convolution, the classic architectures, and modern vision. · Time: 20–25 h · Needs: 05 🎯 What you'll learn By the end of this chapter you can: compute a convolution by hand, and its output size and parameter count; explain weight sharing, translation equivariance, receptive field and pooling; tell the story of vision architectures from LeNet to ResNet, ConvNeXt and the Vision Transformer ; use data augmentation and transfer learning effectively; describe detection and segmentation, and the vision foundation models you can download today. Skip test Compute the output size of a conv: input 32, kernel 5, stride 2, padding 2. Count the parameters of Conv2d(64, 128, kernel_size=3) and explain why it's tiny next to a dense layer. Explain translation equivariance , weight sharing , and receptive field . Explain why ResNet's skip connections made 100+ layer nets trainable. Explain transfer learning: when to freeze, when to fine-tune. Explain how a Vision Transformer (ViT) turns an image into tokens. 1 · Why not just use an MLP on images? A 224×224 colour image has 224cdot224cdot3approx150\\,000 numbers. A dense layer with 1000 hidden units on it would need 150 million weights for one layer, and it would have to learn \"a cat in the top-left\" and \"a cat in the bottom-right\" as unrelated patterns. Images have structure an MLP ignores: Locality: nearby pixels are related; a useful pattern (an edge, an eye) is small. Translation: the same pattern can appear anywhere. Convolutional networks (CNNs) build both facts into the architecture. 📖 Definition · Convolution (as used in DL) Slide a small filter (kernel) K of size ktimes k over the image. At each position, multiply the filter with the patch underneath element-wise and sum: Y[i,j] = sum_u=0^k-1sum_v=0^k-1 K[u,v]\\;X[i+u,\\,j+v] + b With C_in input channels the filter is C_intimes ktimes k and the sum also runs over channels. A layer has C_out such filters, producing C_out output feature maps . (Strictly this is cross-correlation; true convolution flips the kernel. With learned kernels the difference doesn't matter.) ✍️ Worked example · A vertical-edge detector Image patch (left half dark, right half bright) and kernel: X=beginpmatrix0&0&9\\\\0&0&9\\\\0&0&9endpmatrix,quad K=beginpmatrix-1&0&1\\\\-1&0&1\\\\-1&0&1endpmatrix\\ Rightarrow\\ sum Kodot X = 27 On a flat region (all 9s) the same kernel gives 0. The filter \"lights up\" exactly where there is a vertical edge, wherever it is in the image. 🧮 Formula · Output size and parameter count For input size n, kernel k, padding p, stride s: n_out = Biglfloorfracn+2p-ksBigrfloor+1 qquad \\#params = C_incdot C_outcdot k^2 + C_out Padding adds zeros around the border (with p=(k-1)/2 and s=1 the size is preserved: \"same\" padding). Stride s moves the filter s pixels at a time, shrinking the output. ✍️ Worked example · The skip-test numbers Input 32, k=5, s=2, p=2: lfloor(32+4-5)/2rfloor+1 = 15+1 = 16. Conv2d(64, 128, 3) : 64cdot128cdot9+128 = 73\\,856 parameters, independent of the image size. A dense layer from a 64times32times32 map to a 128times32times32 map would need 65\\,536times131\\,072approx8.6 billion weights. 🔭 Intuition · Physics view A convolution is the discrete version of filtering a signal with an impulse response (or a Green's function). The Fourier transform turns it into multiplication, which is why the convolution theorem and FFT-based convolution exist. ▶ But what is a convolution? ▶️ But what is a convolution? · 3Blue1Brown · 23 min (plays here; or open on YouTube) 2 · The properties that make CNNs work 📖 Definition · Weight sharing and translation equivariance The same filter weights are used at every position ( weight sharing ), which is why the parameter count is tiny. A consequence: if you shift the input, the feature map shifts by the same amount. That is translation equivariance : conv(shift(X)) = shift(conv(X)). Pooling and global averaging then make the final prediction approximately invariant to small shifts. 📖 Definition · Receptive field The receptive field of a unit is the region of the input image that can affect it. Each 3×3 conv layer (stride 1) grows it by 2: after L such layers it is (2L+1)times(2L+1). Strides and pooling make it grow much faster. Deep layers therefore \"see\" large parts of the image. 📖 Definition · Pooling Max pooling (e.g. 2×2, stride 2) keeps the largest value in each window; average pooling keeps the mean. Both halve the height and width, have no parameters, and add some robustness to small shifts. Modern nets often use strided convolutions instead, and global average pooling at the end (average each channel over all positions) before the classifier. 💡 Key idea · The feature hierarchy Early layers detect edges and colour blobs; middle layers combine them into textures and parts; late layers respond to whole objects. Each stage works at lower resolution but with more channels: a typical shape path is 3times224times224 to 64times112times112 todotsto 512times7times7. ✍️ Worked example · Two 3×3 convs vs one 5×5 Two stacked 3×3 convs have a 5×5 receptive field, using 2cdot9C^2=18C^2 weights (for C channels in and out) versus 25C^2 for one 5×5 conv, and they add an extra nonlinearity in between. This is VGG's key insight. 3 · Architectures: the ideas that mattered 📖 Definition · Architecture timeline (know the idea of each) Year Model Key idea 1998 LeNet conv + pool + dense, for digits 2012 AlexNet ReLU, GPUs, dropout: the \"ImageNet moment\" 2014 VGG only 3×3 convs, stacked deep 2014 GoogLeNet / Inception parallel branches; cheap 1×1 convs to mix channels 2015 ResNet residual connections mathbf y=mathbf x+F(mathbf x): 100+ layers train 2017–19 MobileNet / EfficientNet depthwise-separable convs; scale width, depth and resolution together 2020 ViT image patches as tokens → a plain transformer 2022 ConvNeXt a CNN modernized with transformer-era design and training tricks 📖 Definition · Residual block (ResNet) mathbf y = ReLUbig(mathbf x + F(mathbf x)big),qquad F = conv-BN-ReLU-conv-BN Before ResNet, adding layers to a deep plain net made even the training error worse: optimization, not overfitting, was the problem. With the skip, a block can represent the identity trivially (set F=0), and gradients reach early layers through the \"+\" unchanged. The most important architecture idea in DL : transformers use the same trick (module 08). 🎛️ Try it · Normalization Explorer Every ResNet block uses BatchNorm. Highlight which numbers in a (N, C, H, W) batch it averages over, and compare with LayerNorm and GroupNorm. 📖 Definition · 1×1 convolutions and depthwise-separable convolutions A 1×1 conv mixes channels at each pixel without looking at neighbours: a cheap way to change the channel count. A depthwise-separable conv splits a normal conv into a per-channel ktimes k conv (spatial) followed by a 1×1 conv (channels), cutting cost by roughly a factor of k^2. MobileNet and EfficientNet are built from them. 📖 Definition · Vision Transformer (ViT) Cut the image into non-overlapping 16times16 patches, flatten each one and map it with a linear layer to a d-dimensional token , add position embeddings (and a learnable [CLS] token), then run a standard transformer encoder (module 08). A 224×224 image becomes 14times14=196 tokens. ViTs have weaker built-in priors than CNNs, so they need more data or pretraining, but they scale extremely well. 4 · Data augmentation and transfer learning 📖 Definition · Data augmentation Create new training examples by transforming existing ones in ways that don't change the label: random crops and resizes, horizontal flips, colour jitter, rotation; stronger recipes like RandAugment ; and mixing methods like Mixup (blend two images and their labels) and CutMix (paste a patch of one image into another). It is often worth more than any architecture change. ⚠️ Common mistake · Augment only the training set Evaluate on clean, centre-cropped images. Leaving random augmentation on at test time makes your scores noisy and lower. 📖 Definition · Transfer learning Start from a network pretrained on a large dataset (ImageNet, or a foundation model), replace its final classification layer (the head ) with one for your classes, and train on your data. Small dataset (hundreds of images): freeze the backbone, train only the head (or the last block). More data / a different domain: fine-tune everything at a low learning rate (e.g. 10× lower than from scratch), often with a smaller LR for early layers. ⚠️ Common mistake · Match the pretraining preprocessing Pretrained models expect inputs normalized the way they were trained (for ImageNet: per-channel mean (0.485, 0.456, 0.406) and std (0.229, 0.224, 0.225), at the right resolution). Forgetting this silently costs a lot of accuracy. 💻 In code · Fine-tuning a pretrained ResNet with torchvision from torchvision.models import resnet18, ResNet18_Weights weights = ResNet18_Weights.DEFAULT model = resnet18(weights=weights) for p in model.parameters(): p.requires_grad = False # freeze the backbone model.fc = nn.Linear(model.fc.in_features, 5) # new head for 5 classes (trainable) preprocess = weights.transforms() # the matching resize + normalization 5 · Beyond classification 📖 Definition · Object detection Predict a bounding box and a class for every object. Two-stage detectors (Faster R-CNN) first propose regions, then classify them. One-stage detectors (the YOLO family) predict boxes directly on a grid, which is fast. DETR uses a transformer to output a set of boxes directly. 🧮 Formula · Intersection over union (IoU) IoU(A,B) = fracarea(Acap B)area(Acup B) How much a predicted box overlaps the true box (1 = perfect). A detection usually counts as correct if IoU ≥ 0.5. Non-maximum suppression removes duplicate boxes that overlap a higher-scoring one. 📖 Definition · Segmentation Classify every pixel . U-Net is an encoder–decoder: the encoder downsamples to capture context, the decoder upsamples back to full resolution, and skip connections carry fine detail across. (U-Nets are also the backbone of many diffusion models, module 10.) Mask R-CNN adds a mask per detected object; SAM (Segment Anything) segments any object from a click or box prompt. 📖 Definition · Vision foundation models Large pretrained models you can download and reuse: CLIP (images and text in a shared embedding space; zero-shot classification by comparing an image to text prompts), DINOv2 (self-supervised features that work well for almost any task), SAM (segmentation). Get them from timm or Hugging Face. 🎛️ Try it · Flashcards Choose the 06 CNN deck and drill this chapter's key facts with spaced repetition, then do the practice questions below. Practice (answers hidden) Input 64times64, conv with k=3, s=1, p=1, then 2times2 max pool, then conv with k=3, s=2, p=0. What is the final spatial size? Answer 64 to 64 (same padding) to 32 (pool) to lfloor(32-3)/2rfloor+1 = 14+1 = 15. How many parameters does Conv2d(3, 32, kernel_size=5) have? And a depthwise-separable version (5×5 depthwise on 3 channels, then 1×1 to 32), ignoring biases? Answer Standard: 3cdot32cdot25+32 = 2\\,432. Separable: 3cdot25 + 3cdot32 = 75+96 = 171 weights. What is the receptive field after five 3×3 convolutions with stride 1? Answer 2cdot5+1 = 11, i.e. 11times11 pixels. You have 300 labelled photos of 3 kinds of leaves. What's your plan? Answer Transfer learning: take a pretrained backbone (e.g. ResNet-50 or a DINOv2 ViT), use its preprocessing, freeze it and train a new 3-way head with strong augmentation. Hold out a validation set. If accuracy plateaus, unfreeze the last block (or everything) at a low LR. Training from scratch on 300 images would badly overfit. Two boxes: A = [0,0]–[4,4] and B=[2,2]–[6,6]. What is their IoU? Answer Intersection [2,2]–[4,4] has area 4. Union =16+16-4=28. IoU =4/28approx0.14: not a match at the usual 0.5 threshold. How many tokens does a ViT with 14times14 patches produce for a 224times224 image (plus [CLS] )? Answer 224/14=16 patches per side, so 16^2=256 patch tokens + 1 [CLS] = 257. Build ResNet-18 from scratch on CIFAR-10. Target ≥ 93% with augmentation and a good LR schedule. Transfer learning: fine-tune a pretrained model on a small custom dataset (e.g. 5 classes, ~100 photos each). Compare frozen vs full fine-tuning. Visualize filters, feature maps, and a saliency map / Grad-CAM for your model's predictions. Stretch: a small U-Net for segmentation (e.g. Oxford-IIIT Pets masks). Traps Off-by-one shape errors. Print shapes after every block while developing. Forgetting ImageNet normalization (mean/std) when using a pretrained model. Evaluating with train-time augmentation turned on. Go deeper (all optional) ⭐ Stanford CS231n : course notes + lecture videos (YouTube) + assignments 1–2 . A gold standard. d2l.ai ch. 7–8 (CNNs, modern CNNs) and ch. 14 (computer vision). CNN Explainer (interactive) : see a conv net run in your browser. Feature Visualization (Distill) : what CNN neurons actually detect. PyTorch Image Models (timm) : pretrained models for transfer learning. Done when ResNet on CIFAR-10 ≥ 93% · fine-tuned model works on your own photos"},{"t":"07 · Sequences, Embeddings & RNNs","u":"deep-learning/07-sequences-rnns.html","s":"Deep Learning","h":[{"n":"Skip test","id":"skip-test"},{"n":"1 · Language modelling","id":"1-language-modelling"},{"n":"2 · Tokens and embeddings","id":"2-tokens-and-embeddings"},{"n":"3 · Recurrent neural networks","id":"3-recurrent-neural-networks"},{"n":"4 · Seq2seq and the birth of attention","id":"4-seq2seq-and-the-birth-of-attention"},{"n":"5 · Generating text","id":"5-generating-text"},{"n":"Practice (answers hidden)","id":"practice-answers-hidden"},{"n":"Build","id":"build"},{"n":"Go deeper (all optional)","id":"go-deeper-all-optional"},{"n":"Done when","id":"done-when"}],"x":"07 · Sequences, Embeddings & RNNs Module 07 ⏱ 7 min read View Markdown source Goal: learn how to model sequences, and why attention replaced recurrence. · Time: 10–15 h · Needs: 05 This module is deliberately short . RNNs are mostly history now, but the problems they ran into explain every transformer design choice. (Modern state-space models such as Mamba bring recurrence back. See module 12.) 🎯 What you'll learn By the end of this chapter you can: state the language-modelling objective and turn text into tokens and embeddings ; write the RNN update and explain why gradients vanish or explode through time; explain how LSTM/GRU gates fix that; explain the seq2seq bottleneck and how attention removes it, leading straight to the transformer; generate text autoregressively with greedy, temperature, top-k and top-p sampling. Skip test Explain tokenization and embeddings: why one-hot is bad and what an embedding table is. Write the vanilla RNN update. Explain why gradients vanish or explode through time. Explain how the LSTM gates (forget/input/output) fix this. Explain the seq2seq encoder-decoder bottleneck and how attention removes it. Explain teacher forcing and autoregressive generation. 1 · Language modelling 📖 Definition · Language model A language model assigns a probability to a sequence of tokens. By the chain rule of probability it can always be factored into next-token predictions: p(x_1,dots,x_T) = prod_t=1^T p(x_tmid x_1,dots,x_t-1) So a model that predicts \"the next token given everything so far\" is a complete language model. This single objective powers GPT (modules 08–09). 🧮 Formula · Training loss and perplexity Train by minimizing the average cross-entropy of the next token: L = -frac1Tsum_t=1^Tlog p_theta(x_tmid x_<t),qquad perplexity = e^L Perplexity is the \"effective number of choices\" the model is torn between at each step. A uniform guess over a vocabulary of V tokens has perplexity V. ✍️ Worked example · A bigram model The simplest LM looks only at the previous character: count how often each pair occurs in the training text, normalize each row into probabilities, and sample. On a list of names it produces name-like gibberish (\"kalia\", \"jonen\"). Everything after this (MLP, RNN, transformer) is a better way to use more context. ▶ The spelled-out intro to language modeling: building makemore ▶️ The spelled-out intro to language modeling: building makemore · Andrej Karpathy · 1 h 57 min (plays here; or open on YouTube) 2 · Tokens and embeddings 📖 Definition · Tokenization Text is split into tokens and each token gets an integer id from a fixed vocabulary of size V. Tokens can be characters (small V, long sequences), words (huge V, unknown words are a problem) or subwords (the standard: frequent words stay whole, rare ones split into pieces; byte-pair encoding, module 09). 📖 Definition · Embedding One-hot vectors (length V, a single 1) treat every pair of tokens as equally different and are huge. Instead, keep a learned table Einmathbb R^Vtimes d and represent token i by its row E_i, a dense d-dimensional vector (e.g. d=768). Looking up a row is the same as multiplying a one-hot vector by E, so the table is trained by backprop like any other weight. Tokens used in similar contexts end up with nearby vectors. 📖 Definition · word2vec A 2013 method that learns word embeddings by predicting the words around each word (skip-gram) with a cheap trick ( negative sampling : tell real context words from random ones). The resulting vectors show striking structure, like vecking-vecman+vecwomanapproxvecqueen. Similarity between embeddings is measured with cosine similarity (module 01). 3 · Recurrent neural networks An RNN reads a sequence one step at a time, carrying a hidden state that summarizes everything seen so far. 🧮 Formula · Vanilla RNN mathbf h_t = tanhbig(W_hmathbf h_t-1 + W_xmathbf x_t + mathbf bbig),qquad hatmathbf y_t = softmax(W_ymathbf h_t) The same weights are used at every time step (weight sharing over time, like a convolution shares over space). 📖 Definition · Backpropagation through time (BPTT) Unroll the RNN over the T steps of a sequence into one long feed-forward graph and run ordinary backprop on it. Long sequences are often cut into chunks ( truncated BPTT ) to save memory. 💡 Key idea · Why gradients vanish or explode The gradient from step t back to step t-k passes through k copies of the Jacobian partialmathbf h_t/partialmathbf h_t-1 = diag(tanh')\\,W_h. Roughly, it scales like lambda_max^k, where lambda_max is the largest eigenvalue magnitude of W_h (times the tanh slopes, which are ≤ 1). If that is below 1 the gradient vanishes exponentially and the network can't learn long-range dependencies; above 1 it explodes (fix: gradient clipping, module 05). 🧮 Formula · LSTM (long short-term memory) Add a separate cell state mathbf c_t that is updated additively , controlled by gates (sigmoids between 0 and 1): mathbf f_t,mathbf i_t,mathbf o_t = sigma(cdot),quad tildemathbf c_t = tanh(cdot)qquad(each a linear function of  mathbf h_t-1,mathbf x_t) mathbf c_t = mathbf f_todotmathbf c_t-1 + mathbf i_todottildemathbf c_t,qquad mathbf h_t = mathbf o_todottanh(mathbf c_t) Forget gate mathbf f: how much old memory to keep. Input gate mathbf i: how much new content to write. Output gate mathbf o: how much memory to expose. When mathbf fapprox1 the gradient flows along mathbf c almost unchanged: a gradient highway, the same idea as residual connections. The GRU is a simpler two-gate variant. 4 · Seq2seq and the birth of attention 📖 Definition · Sequence-to-sequence (seq2seq) For tasks like translation, an encoder RNN reads the whole input sentence into its final hidden state; a decoder RNN then generates the output one token at a time starting from that state. The bottleneck: one fixed-size vector must hold the meaning of an entire sentence, and quality drops for long inputs. 🧮 Formula · Attention (Bahdanau et al., 2014) At each decoder step t, look back at all encoder states mathbf h_1,dots,mathbf h_S: alpha_t,s = softmax_sbig(score(mathbf s_t,mathbf h_s)big),qquad mathbf c_t = sum_salpha_t,s\\,mathbf h_s The attention weights alpha_t,s (summing to 1) say how much the decoder focuses on each input position; the context vector mathbf c_t is their weighted average. No more single-vector bottleneck, and the weights are interpretable (they align words across languages). 💡 Key idea · Why transformers won RNNs must process tokens one after another, so they can't use GPU parallelism over time, and information between distant tokens passes through many steps. \"Attention Is All You Need\" (2017) dropped recurrence entirely: every token attends to every other token directly, in one parallel step. That is module 08. 5 · Generating text 📖 Definition · Teacher forcing and autoregressive generation Training (teacher forcing): feed the true previous tokens and predict each next one, so all positions train in parallel. Generation (autoregressive): feed the model's own sampled token back in as the next input, one step at a time. 📖 Definition · Decoding strategies Method Rule Effect greedy always take the most likely token deterministic, often repetitive temperature T sample from softmax(mathbf z/T) T<1 sharper, T>1 more random top-k sample only among the k most likely tokens cuts off the long tail of nonsense top-p (nucleus) sample among the smallest set whose probability sums to p (e.g. 0.9) adapts the cut-off to the model's confidence 🎛️ Try it · Flashcards Choose the 07 Seq deck and drill this chapter's key facts with spaced repetition, then do the practice questions below. Practice (answers hidden) A model's average next-token cross-entropy on validation text is 1.2 nats. What is its perplexity, and how does it compare to uniform guessing over 65 characters? Answer e^1.2approx3.3: the model is about as uncertain as a choice between 3.3 equally likely characters, versus 65 for uniform guessing (ln65approx4.17 nats). An embedding table has V=50\\,000 tokens and d=768. How many parameters, and what does looking up token 42 do? Answer 50\\,000cdot768 = 38.4 million parameters. The lookup returns row 42 of the table, equivalent to multiplying the one-hot vector for token 42 by E. An RNN's recurrent Jacobian has largest eigenvalue magnitude 0.9. By roughly what factor is a gradient scaled after 50 steps? And with 1.1? Answer 0.9^50approx0.005 (vanishing) and 1.1^50approx117 (exploding). In an LSTM, what forget-gate value lets information survive for many steps, and why does that help the gradient? Answer mathbf f_tapprox1. Then mathbf c_tapproxmathbf c_t-1+dots, so partialmathbf c_t/partialmathbf c_t-1approx I and the gradient passes back without shrinking, like a residual connection. Logits for three tokens are (2, 1, 0). Give the sampling probabilities at temperature 1 and at temperature 0.5. Answer T=1: e^(2,1,0)/Sigma = (7.39, 2.72, 1)/11.11 approx (0.67, 0.24, 0.09). T=0.5: logits become (4,2,0): (54.6, 7.39, 1)/63.0approx(0.87, 0.12, 0.02). Lower temperature concentrates probability on the top token. Build Character-level LM (makemore): bigram → MLP → small RNN/LSTM on a names or Shakespeare dataset. Compare samples and validation loss. Train word2vec (skip-gram with negative sampling) on a small corpus. Show nearest neighbors and analogies. Stretch: a seq2seq + attention model for date-format translation (\"3rd of May 2021\" → \"2021-05-03\"). Plot the attention heatmap. Go deeper (all optional) ⭐ Karpathy Zero to Hero: makemore series : you'll already be partway through it from module 04. It builds bigram → MLP → WaveNet-style LMs. ⭐ Understanding LSTM Networks (colah) : the classic diagrams. The Unreasonable Effectiveness of RNNs (Karpathy) . Stanford CS224n : lectures on word vectors, RNNs, seq2seq + attention. d2l.ai ch. 9–11 (RNNs, modern RNNs, attention). Done when Char-LM produces plausible samples · you can explain the path from RNN → attention → transformer"},{"t":"08 · Transformers","u":"deep-learning/08-transformers.html","s":"Deep Learning","h":[{"n":"Skip test","id":"skip-test"},{"n":"1 · Self-attention","id":"1-self-attention"},{"n":"2 · Causal masking","id":"2-causal-masking"},{"n":"3 · Multi-head attention","id":"3-multi-head-attention"},{"n":"4 · The transformer block","id":"4-the-transformer-block"},{"n":"5 · Positional information","id":"5-positional-information"},{"n":"6 · Cost and efficiency","id":"6-cost-and-efficiency"},{"n":"7 · Three families of transformers","id":"7-three-families-of-transformers"},{"n":"8 · Build a GPT","id":"8-build-a-gpt"},{"n":"Practice (answers hidden)","id":"practice-answers-hidden"},{"n":"Build","id":"build"},{"n":"Traps","id":"traps"},{"n":"Go deeper (all optional)","id":"go-deeper-all-optional"},{"n":"Done when","id":"done-when"}],"x":"08 · Transformers Module 08 ⏱ 10 min read View Markdown source Goal: be able to write a GPT from a blank file and explain every line. · Time: 20–30 h · Needs: 05, 07 🎯 What you'll learn By the end of this chapter you can: compute scaled dot-product attention by hand and in code, with every shape, and explain the sqrtd_k; explain causal masking and multi-head attention ; draw a modern (pre-norm) transformer block and count its parameters; compare sinusoidal, learned and RoPE position encodings; explain attention's O(T^2) cost, FlashAttention and the KV cache; tell encoder-only, decoder-only and encoder–decoder models apart, and build a small GPT. Skip test Write scaled dot-product attention with shapes, and explain the sqrtd_k. Explain causal masking and why GPT needs it. Explain multi-head attention: why multiple heads, and how shapes reshape. Draw one (pre-norm) transformer block: LN → attention → residual → LN → MLP → residual. Explain why positional information is needed. Compare sinusoidal, learned, and RoPE . Give the time and memory complexity of attention in sequence length T. Explain encoder-only (BERT) vs decoder-only (GPT) vs encoder-decoder (T5). 1 · Self-attention In module 07, a decoder attended to encoder states. Self-attention lets every token in a sequence attend to every other token of the same sequence, so each token's vector can absorb information from wherever it is relevant (\"it\" looks at the noun it refers to). 📖 Definition · Queries, keys and values From each token's vector mathbf x_t, compute three vectors with learned matrices: mathbf q_t = W_Qmathbf x_t\\ (what am I looking for?),quad mathbf k_t = W_Kmathbf x_t\\ (what do I contain?),quad mathbf v_t = W_Vmathbf x_t\\ (what do I pass on?) Token t compares its query with every key (dot product = similarity, module 01), turns the scores into weights with softmax, and takes the weighted average of the values. 🧮 Formula · Scaled dot-product attention Stacking all tokens as rows (X is Ttimes d; Q=XW_Q^top, K=XW_K^top, V=XW_V^top): Attention(Q,K,V) = softmax\\!Big(fracQK^topsqrtd_k+MBig)V Shapes: QK^top is Ttimes T (every query against every key); softmax runs along each row ; the result is Ttimes d_v. M is an optional mask (section 2). 💡 Key idea · Why divide by sqrtd_k? If the entries of mathbf q and mathbf k are independent with variance 1, then mathbf qcdotmathbf k=sum_i=1^d_kq_ik_i has variance d_k. For d_k=64 the scores would be ~8× too spread out, pushing softmax into a nearly one-hot, saturated regime where gradients vanish. Dividing by sqrtd_k brings the variance back to 1. ✍️ Worked example · Attention by hand One query mathbf q=(2,0); three keys mathbf k_1=(1,0), mathbf k_2=(0,1), mathbf k_3=(1,1) with values mathbf v_1=(1,0), mathbf v_2=(0,1), mathbf v_3=(1,1); d_k=2. Scores mathbf qcdotmathbf k_j/sqrt2 = (1.414,\\ 0,\\ 1.414) → softmax weights (0.446,\\ 0.108,\\ 0.446) → output 0.446\\,mathbf v_1+0.108\\,mathbf v_2+0.446\\,mathbf v_3 = (0.892,\\ 0.554). The query \"points along the first axis\", so it attends mostly to keys 1 and 3 and pulls in a mix of their values. 💡 Key idea · Attention is a set operation Nothing in the formula knows the order of the tokens: shuffle the rows of X and the outputs shuffle the same way (permutation equivariance). Word order has to be injected separately (section 5). 🎛️ Try it · Attention Visualizer Edit queries and keys, toggle the causal mask and the sqrtd_k scaling, and watch the attention matrix and the outputs change. ▶ Attention in transformers, step-by-step ▶️ Attention in transformers, step-by-step · 3Blue1Brown · 26 min (plays here; or open on YouTube) 2 · Causal masking 📖 Definition · Causal mask A GPT predicts token t+1 from tokens 1..t, so token t must not see the future. Set M_ij=-infty for j>i (above the diagonal) and 0 elsewhere. After softmax, e^-infty=0, so every token attends only to itself and earlier tokens. 💡 Key idea · Training on all positions at once With the mask, one forward pass over a sequence of T tokens produces T next-token predictions, each using only its own past. Training on all of them in parallel is what makes transformers so much faster to train than RNNs. ⚠️ Common mistake · Mask before the softmax The -infty must be added to the scores , before softmax. Zeroing weights after softmax leaves rows that no longer sum to 1, and the future has already leaked into the normalization. 3 · Multi-head attention 📖 Definition · Multi-head attention Instead of one attention with d_k=d_model, run h smaller attentions (\"heads\") in parallel, each with its own W_Q,W_K,W_V and head size d_k=d_model/h. Concatenate their outputs and mix them with an output matrix W_O: MHA(X) = Concat(head_1,dots,head_h)\\,W_O^top Different heads learn different relations (previous token, syntax, coreference, position…) at the same total cost as one big head. 💻 In code · Causal multi-head attention in PyTorch class CausalSelfAttention(nn.Module): def __init__(self, d, h): super().__init__() self.h = h self.qkv = nn.Linear(d, 3 * d) # W_Q, W_K, W_V in one matmul self.proj = nn.Linear(d, d) # W_O def forward(self, x): # x: (B, T, d) B, T, d = x.shape q, k, v = self.qkv(x).split(d, dim=-1) # (B, T, d) -> (B, h, T, d/h): each head attends independently q, k, v = (t.view(B, T, self.h, d // self.h).transpose(1, 2) for t in (q, k, v)) y = F.scaled_dot_product_attention(q, k, v, is_causal=True) # (B, h, T, d/h) y = y.transpose(1, 2).contiguous().view(B, T, d) # concat heads return self.proj(y) 4 · The transformer block 🧮 Formula · A modern (pre-norm, GPT-style) block x = x + MultiHeadAttention(LayerNorm(x)) # tokens exchange information x = x + MLP(LayerNorm(x)) # each token is processed on its own The MLP is two linear layers with a nonlinearity, applied to every token separately: dto4dto d with GELU (or a SwiGLU variant in Llama-style models). A GPT is: token embedding (+ positions) → L blocks → final LayerNorm → linear layer to V logits. 📖 Definition · The residual stream Because every sub-layer adds its output to mathbf x, you can view the model as one vector per token (the residual stream ) that each attention and MLP layer reads from and writes into. Attention moves information between positions; the MLP transforms information within a position. This view is the basis of mechanistic interpretability (module 12). 🧮 Formula · Parameter count (decoder-only) Per block: attention 4d^2 (W_Q,W_K,W_V,W_O) + MLP 8d^2 (dtimes4d and 4dtimes d) =12d^2 (ignoring biases and norms). Total: Napprox12\\,L\\,d^2 + V d GPT-2 small: L=12, d=768, V=50\\,257: 12cdot12cdot768^2approx84.9M + Vdapprox38.6M + positions 1024cdot768approx0.8M approx 124M ✓. 💡 Key idea · Why pre-norm? The original Transformer put LayerNorm after each residual addition (post-norm), which is hard to train when deep without careful warmup. Putting it before each sub-layer (pre-norm) leaves a clean identity path through the whole network, so modern LLMs almost all use pre-norm (often with RMSNorm, module 05). ▶ But what is a GPT? Visual intro to transformers ▶️ But what is a GPT? Visual intro to transformers · 3Blue1Brown · 27 min (plays here; or open on YouTube) 5 · Positional information 🧮 Formula · Sinusoidal positions (original Transformer) Add a fixed vector to each token embedding: PE(pos,2i)=sinfracpos10000^2i/d,qquad PE(pos,2i+1)=cosfracpos10000^2i/d Each pair of dimensions is a clock ticking at a different frequency, so every position gets a unique pattern. Learned absolute positions (GPT-2) simply train a table of T_max vectors instead, but can't go beyond T_max. 🧮 Formula · RoPE (rotary position embedding) Instead of adding anything, rotate each pair of dimensions (x_2i,x_2i+1) of the query and key at position m by an angle mtheta_i, with theta_i = 10000^-2i/d. Rotations preserve dot products up to the angle difference, so langle R_mmathbf q,\\ R_nmathbf krangle\\ depends only on\\ mathbf q,mathbf k\\ and\\ m-n Attention therefore sees relative positions. Used by Llama, Qwen and most modern LLMs, and it extends to longer contexts with tricks that rescale the angles. 🔭 In complex notation it is multiplication by e^imtheta: relative phase = relative position. 6 · Cost and efficiency 🧮 Formula · Complexity of attention For sequence length T and width d: computing QK^top costs O(T^2d) time, and the score matrix needs O(T^2) memory per head. Doubling the context quadruples that part of the cost. The MLPs cost O(Td^2), which dominates for short contexts. 📖 Definition · FlashAttention An exact attention algorithm that computes softmax in tiles inside the GPU's small, fast on-chip memory (SRAM) and never writes the full Ttimes T matrix to slow memory. Same result, much less memory traffic: a large speed-up and memory that grows linearly with T. PyTorch's scaled_dot_product_attention uses it automatically when it can. 📖 Definition · KV cache When generating token by token, the keys and values of earlier tokens never change, so store them instead of recomputing them. Each new token then needs only one new query against the cached keys. The cache costs 2cdot Lcdot Tcdot d numbers per sequence: for 32 layers, d=4096, T=4096 in 16-bit floats that is about 2.1 GB . Variants like grouped-query attention (GQA) share keys/values across heads to shrink it. 📝 Note · Other ways around T^2 Sparse or sliding-window attention, linear-attention approximations, and state-space models such as Mamba (module 12) trade some flexibility for cost that grows linearly with T. 7 · Three families of transformers 📖 Definition · Encoder-only, decoder-only, encoder–decoder Family Attention Training objective Examples Used for encoder-only bidirectional (no mask) predict masked tokens BERT, RoBERTa classification, embeddings, search decoder-only causal next token GPT, Llama, Claude-style LLMs generation, chat, dominant today encoder–decoder encoder bidirectional + decoder causal with cross-attention predict the output sequence T5, original Transformer, Whisper translation, summarization, speech 💡 Key idea · The same block everywhere Text, images (ViT, module 06), audio (Whisper), proteins (AlphaFold-style components) and robot actions all use the same block with a different tokenizer. Learn it once and it applies everywhere. 8 · Build a GPT ▶ Let's build GPT: from scratch, in code, spelled out ▶️ Let's build GPT: from scratch, in code, spelled out · Andrej Karpathy · 1 h 56 min. Code along, then rebuild it from a blank file. (plays here; or open on YouTube) 🎛️ Try it · Attention Visualizer Before you code, predict the attention matrix for a short sentence with and without the causal mask, then check. 🎛️ Try it · Flashcards Choose the 08 Transformer deck and drill this chapter's key facts with spaced repetition, then do the practice questions below. Practice (answers hidden) X is (B=8, T=128, d=512) with h=8 heads. What is the shape of Q per head, of the score matrix, and of the output? Answer Per head d_k=512/8=64: Q,K,V are (8, 8, 128, 64) (batch, heads, T, d_k); scores (8, 8, 128, 128); output after concatenation (8, 128, 512). Why would attention without the sqrtd_k train badly at d_k=128? Answer Scores would have standard deviation sqrt128approx11, so softmax would be almost one-hot: tiny gradients for all but one key (saturation), slow and unstable training. Write the causal mask for T=3, and the attention weights of token 1 (the first token). Answer M=beginpmatrix0&-infty&-infty\\\\0&0&-infty\\\\0&0&0endpmatrix. Token 1 can only see itself, so its weights are (1,0,0). Estimate the parameter count of a decoder with L=24, d=1024, V=50\\,000. Answer 12cdot24cdot1024^2approx302M + 50\\,000cdot1024approx51M approx353M (GPT-2 medium is ~350M). Going from 4k to 32k context, how do the attention score memory and the KV-cache size change? Answer Context grows 8×. Score memory (without FlashAttention) grows 8^2=64×; the KV cache grows linearly, 8×. Show that attention without positions can't tell \"dog bites man\" from \"man bites dog\". Answer Both sentences have the same set of token embeddings. Attention is permutation-equivariant, so each word's output vector is the same in both orders (just in different positions), and any order-independent readout gives the same answer. Positional encodings break the symmetry. Build Single-head attention in NumPy , checked against torch.nn.functional.scaled_dot_product_attention . nanoGPT from a blank file on Tiny Shakespeare (char-level). Then add: multi-head, pre-norm, dropout, weight tying, LR warmup + cosine, AdamW. Swap learned positions for RoPE and compare validation loss. Try a longer context. Stretch: reproduce GPT-2 124M on FineWeb-Edu following build-nanogpt (needs a rented GPU: roughly 1–2 h on 8×A100, or a day-plus on a single GPU). Traps Mixing up (B, T, C) and (B, heads, T, head_dim) reshapes/transposes. Write the shapes in comments. A causal mask applied after the softmax (wrong) instead of before (right). Thinking you understand it after watching. Close the video and rebuild from nothing. Go deeper (all optional) ⭐ nanoGPT / build-nanogpt (the \"Let's reproduce GPT-2 (124M)\" video). ⭐ The Illustrated Transformer (Jay Alammar) . Attention Is All You Need (2017) : read the paper after building it. It'll be easy by then. The Annotated Transformer (Harvard NLP) : the paper line by line in PyTorch. 3Blue1Brown: Neural Networks (chapters 5–7): superb visual intuition. Transformer Explainer (interactive) : GPT-2 running live in the browser. Understanding Deep Learning (Prince) ch. 12. Done when GPT written from a blank file without referencing code · every skip-test item answered on paper"},{"t":"09 · Large Language Models","u":"deep-learning/09-llms.html","s":"Deep Learning","h":[{"n":"Skip test","id":"skip-test"},{"n":"1 · Tokenization","id":"1-tokenization"},{"n":"2 · Pretraining and scaling laws","id":"2-pretraining-and-scaling-laws"},{"n":"3 · Post-training: from base model to assistant","id":"3-post-training-from-base-model-to-assistant"},{"n":"4 · Efficient fine-tuning: LoRA and QLoRA","id":"4-efficient-fine-tuning-lora-and-qlora"},{"n":"5 · Inference","id":"5-inference"},{"n":"6 · Using and extending LLMs","id":"6-using-and-extending-llms"},{"n":"7 · Evaluation and safety","id":"7-evaluation-and-safety"},{"n":"Practice (answers hidden)","id":"practice-answers-hidden"},{"n":"Build","id":"build"},{"n":"Traps","id":"traps"},{"n":"Go deeper (all optional)","id":"go-deeper-all-optional"},{"n":"Done when","id":"done-when"}],"x":"09 · Large Language Models Module 09 ⏱ 10 min read View Markdown source Goal: understand the full LLM pipeline (data → pretraining → post-training → inference → evaluation) and be able to do each step at small scale. · Time: 25–40 h · Needs: 08 🎯 What you'll learn By the end of this chapter you can: train a BPE tokenizer and explain the odd behaviours tokenization causes; explain pretraining, compute (Capprox6ND) and scaling laws (Chinchilla's ~20 tokens per parameter); walk through post-training: SFT → reward model → RLHF / DPO → RL for reasoning ; fine-tune cheaply with LoRA / QLoRA ; explain why inference is memory-bandwidth-bound, and the tricks that speed it up; choose between prompting, RAG and fine-tuning, and evaluate an LLM honestly. Skip test Explain how BPE tokenization is trained, and give two LLM quirks it causes. State what scaling laws predict, and the Chinchilla rule of thumb (~20 tokens per parameter). Explain the pipeline: pretraining → SFT → preference tuning (RLHF / DPO) → (RL for reasoning). Explain LoRA and why it cuts fine-tuning memory. Explain the KV cache and why inference is memory-bandwidth-bound. Explain RAG and when to use it instead of fine-tuning. Explain why benchmark scores can mislead (contamination, saturation). 1 · Tokenization 📖 Definition · Byte-pair encoding (BPE) A way to build a subword vocabulary from data: Start with the 256 possible bytes as tokens (so any text can be encoded). Count every pair of adjacent tokens in the training corpus. Merge the most frequent pair into a new token and record the merge. Repeat until the vocabulary reaches the target size (~32k–200k tokens). To encode new text, apply the recorded merges in order. Frequent words become single tokens; rare words split into pieces. ✍️ Worked example · Two BPE merges Corpus: low low low lower newest . The most frequent adjacent pair is l o (4 times) → new token lo . Next, lo w (4 times) → low . Now \"low\" is one token, while \"newest\" is still split into smaller pieces. ⚠️ Common mistake · Quirks caused by tokenization Arithmetic and digits: numbers split into irregular chunks (\"1234\" might be \"12\" + \"34\"), making digit-level reasoning harder. Spelling and letter counting: the model sees \"strawberry\" as a few tokens, not letters. Other languages: text in languages under-represented in the tokenizer's training data costs more tokens per word (slower, more expensive, shorter effective context). Spaces: \"hello\" and \" hello\" are different tokens. ▶ Let's build the GPT Tokenizer ▶️ Let's build the GPT Tokenizer · Andrej Karpathy · 2 h 13 min (plays here; or open on YouTube) 2 · Pretraining and scaling laws 📖 Definition · Pretraining Train a decoder-only transformer (module 08) with next-token cross-entropy on trillions of tokens of filtered and deduplicated web text, code, books and math. The result is a base model : it continues text, but it isn't yet a helpful assistant. Data quality matters more than data quantity (e.g. FineWeb-Edu keeps the most \"educational\" web pages). 🧮 Formula · Training compute C approx 6\\,N\\,D\\ FLOPs for a model with N parameters trained on D tokens: about 2N FLOPs per token for the forward pass and 4N for the backward pass. 📖 Definition · Scaling laws Loss falls smoothly as a power law in the number of parameters N, the amount of data D and the compute C (Kaplan et al., 2020). Chinchilla (Hoffmann et al., 2022) found that for a fixed compute budget, N and D should grow together, at roughly D approx 20\\,N\\ (tokens per parameter) Modern models train far past this point, because a smaller model trained longer is cheaper to serve to millions of users. ✍️ Worked example · Chinchilla's own numbers 70B parameters × 1.4T tokens (= 20 tokens per parameter). Compute approx6cdot70times10^9cdot1.4times10^12approx5.9times10^23 FLOPs. It beat the 280B-parameter Gopher, trained with the same compute on far fewer tokens. 🔭 Intuition · Physics view Power laws, universality and debates about sudden \"emergent\" abilities look a lot like critical phenomena in statistical physics. 📖 Definition · Llama-style architecture upgrades Compared with GPT-2: RMSNorm (module 05), SwiGLU MLPs, RoPE positions (module 08), grouped-query attention (fewer key/value heads, a smaller KV cache), and no bias terms. Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) layers replace one MLP with many \"expert\" MLPs and a router that sends each token to only a few of them: many parameters, but cheap compute per token. 📝 Note · Systems Training at this scale needs data, tensor and pipeline parallelism, sharded optimizers (FSDP/ZeRO), bf16 and FlashAttention across thousands of GPUs (module 12). 3 · Post-training: from base model to assistant 📖 Definition · Supervised fine-tuning (SFT) Continue training on (instruction, good response) pairs written or curated by people, with the loss applied only to the response tokens. SFT mostly teaches format and behaviour (answering, following instructions, chat turns), not new knowledge. 🧮 Formula · Reward model (Bradley–Terry) Collect comparisons: for a prompt x, people say which of two responses, y_w (winner) or y_l (loser), is better. Train a model r_phi(x,y) so that P(y_wsucc y_l) = sigmabig(r_phi(x,y_w)-r_phi(x,y_l)big),qquad mathcal L = -logsigmabig(r_phi(x,y_w)-r_phi(x,y_l)big) 🧮 Formula · RLHF objective Optimize the policy (the LLM) pi_theta to get high reward without drifting too far from the SFT model pi_ref: max_theta\\ mathbb E_ysimpi_theta(cdotmid x)big[r_phi(x,y)big] - beta\\,D_KLbig(pi_theta(cdotmid x)\\,\\|\\,pi_ref(cdotmid x)big) usually with PPO (module 11). The KL term stops the model from exploiting flaws in the reward model ( reward hacking ). 🧮 Formula · DPO (direct preference optimization) Skip the reward model and the RL loop: train directly on the preference pairs with a classification-style loss mathcal L_DPO = -logsigmaBig(betaBig[logfracpi_theta(y_wmid x)pi_ref(y_wmid x) - logfracpi_theta(y_lmid x)pi_ref(y_lmid x)Big]Big) It raises the probability of preferred answers relative to the reference model and lowers the rejected ones. Simpler and very common. 📖 Definition · RL for reasoning (verifiable rewards) For tasks with checkable answers (math results, passing unit tests), reward the model only for being right and let RL discover long chain-of-thought reasoning on its own. GRPO (used by DeepSeek-R1) samples a group of answers per question and scores each relative to the group average, A_i = (r_i-mean)/std, removing the need for a separate value network. This is the research frontier. 4 · Efficient fine-tuning: LoRA and QLoRA 🧮 Formula · LoRA (low-rank adaptation) Freeze the pretrained weight Winmathbb R^dtimes k and learn a low-rank update: W' = W + fracalphar\\,BA,qquad Binmathbb R^dtimes r,\\ Ainmathbb R^rtimes k,\\ rll d B starts at zero, so training begins from the original model. Only r(d+k) numbers are trained per matrix, and the optimizer state (Adam keeps two extra copies) shrinks by the same factor. After training, BA can be merged into W, so inference costs nothing extra. ✍️ Worked example · How small is LoRA? A 4096times4096 projection has 16.8M weights. LoRA with r=8 trains 8cdot(4096+4096)=65\\,536 of them: 0.39% . 📖 Definition · QLoRA LoRA on top of a base model stored in 4-bit precision. The frozen weights take a quarter of the 16-bit memory, so fine-tuning a 7B model fits on a single consumer GPU. 5 · Inference 📖 Definition · Autoregressive decoding and the KV cache Generating n tokens takes n forward passes, one token at a time. The KV cache (module 08) stores past keys and values, so each step only processes the new token instead of the whole prefix. 💡 Key idea · Inference is memory-bandwidth-bound At batch size 1, each new token requires reading every weight (plus the KV cache) from GPU memory, but only does ~2 FLOPs per weight. GPUs can compute far faster than they can read memory, so memory bandwidth sets the speed. Rough numbers: a 7B model in 16-bit is 14 GB. A GPU reading ~1 TB/s can stream it at most ~70 times per second, i.e. ≲70 tokens/s for one sequence. 📖 Definition · Speed-ups Quantization: store weights in 8 or 4 bits → less to read, faster and smaller (small quality loss). Batching: serve many users at once; each weight read is shared across the batch. Systems like vLLM manage the KV cache in pages (PagedAttention). Speculative decoding: a small fast model drafts several tokens; the big model checks them all in one pass and keeps the ones it agrees with. Exact same output distribution, fewer big-model steps. 6 · Using and extending LLMs 📖 Definition · Prompting Give clear instructions and context, few-shot examples of the desired input→output, ask for step-by-step reasoning ( chain-of-thought ) on hard problems, and request structured output (e.g. JSON) when a program will read the answer. 📖 Definition · Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) Split your documents into chunks and embed each chunk (module 07). For a question, embed it and retrieve the most similar chunks (vector search). Put those chunks in the prompt and ask the model to answer from them, with citations. Use RAG for knowledge (facts that change, private documents); use fine-tuning for behaviour (style, format, a skill). 📖 Definition · Agents and tool use The model can call tools (run code, search the web, query a database) by emitting a structured request; the program runs it and feeds the result back, in a loop until the task is done. 7 · Evaluation and safety 📖 Definition · Evaluating LLMs Perplexity on held-out text (for base models). Benchmarks: MMLU (knowledge), GSM8K (math word problems), HumanEval / SWE-bench (code), and many more. LLM-as-judge: a strong model grades outputs; cheap, but biased (e.g. toward longer answers). Your own task-specific eval: the only number that tells you whether the model works for your use case. Always build one. ⚠️ Common mistake · Why benchmark scores mislead Contamination: benchmark questions leak into the training data, so the model has seen the answers. Saturation: top models all score ~90%+, so the benchmark no longer separates them. Scores also depend heavily on prompt format. 💡 Key idea · Safety and alignment Jailbreaks, reward hacking, honesty and understanding what models are doing internally (interpretability, module 12) are serious open problems worth caring about. ▶ Deep Dive into LLMs like ChatGPT ▶️ Deep Dive into LLMs like ChatGPT · Andrej Karpathy · 3 h 31 min, the whole pipeline for a general audience (plays here; or open on YouTube) 🎛️ Try it · Flashcards Choose the 09 LLM deck and drill this chapter's key facts with spaced repetition, then do the practice questions below. Practice (answers hidden) Using Capprox6ND, how much compute does it take to train a 1B-parameter model Chinchilla-optimally? Answer Dapprox20N=20B tokens. Capprox6cdot10^9cdot2times10^10=1.2times10^20 FLOPs. Why does SFT alone not make a model reliably prefer better answers among plausible ones? Answer SFT only imitates the demonstrations; it never sees a comparison saying one answer is worse than another. Preference tuning (RLHF/DPO) uses those comparisons directly, so it can push down bad-but-plausible answers. A reward model gives r(x,y_w)=2 and r(x,y_l)=0.5. What probability does it assign to y_w being preferred, and what is its loss? Answer sigma(1.5)approx0.82; loss =-log0.82approx0.20. LoRA with r=16 on all four attention projections (4096times4096) of 32 layers: how many trainable parameters? Answer Per matrix 16cdot(4096+4096)=131\\,072; × 4 matrices × 32 layers =16.8M trainable parameters (vs ~7B in the model). Your company's assistant must answer questions about internal documents that change weekly. Fine-tuning or RAG? Answer RAG: the knowledge changes often, must be up to date and citable. Fine-tune only if you also need a particular behaviour or format that prompting can't achieve. A 13B model in 4-bit precision, on a GPU with 1 TB/s of memory bandwidth. Rough upper bound on tokens/s at batch size 1? Answer 13times10^9cdot0.5 bytes =6.5 GB per token, so at most approx1000/6.5approx150 tokens/s (ignoring the KV cache and overheads). Build BPE tokenizer from scratch (train, encode, decode). Match GPT-2's tokenizer ( tiktoken ) output. Fine-tune with LoRA a small open model (~0.5–3B, e.g. Qwen/Llama/SmolLM) on a custom instruction dataset in Colab. Evaluate before and after on your own eval set. DPO on a small preference dataset with Hugging Face trl . Compare outputs with the SFT model. RAG app: embed your own notes (e.g. this repo!), retrieve, answer questions with citations. Stretch: implement a KV cache in your nanoGPT and measure the tokens/sec speedup. Run nanochat end to end. Traps Fine-tuning to add knowledge (use RAG) instead of behavior . Trusting public benchmarks over your own evals. Chasing every new model release. Fundamentals change slowly, so read papers selectively. Go deeper (all optional) ⭐ Stanford CS336: Language Modeling from Scratch : lectures + assignments (tokenizer, transformer, systems, scaling, data, alignment). The deepest modern course. Do it after nanoGPT. ⭐ Karpathy: nanochat (a full ChatGPT-style pipeline, from tokenizer through SFT/RL to a web UI, in one small repo). Hugging Face LLM Course : the transformers , datasets , and PEFT ecosystem in practice. Build a Large Language Model (From Scratch) , Sebastian Raschka (book + repo ). The Smol Training Playbook (HF) and The Ultra-Scale Playbook : how real LLMs get trained. Key papers (in order): GPT-2 → GPT-3 → Scaling Laws → Chinchilla → InstructGPT → LoRA → Llama → DPO → DeepSeek-R1. Links in resources.md . Stay current: Sebastian Raschka's newsletter , Lilian Weng's blog . Done when Tokenizer matches tiktoken · LoRA fine-tune shows a measurable gain on your eval · you can explain the whole ChatGPT pipeline end to end"},{"t":"10 · Generative Models","u":"deep-learning/10-generative-models.html","s":"Deep Learning","h":[{"n":"Skip test","id":"skip-test"},{"n":"1 · What a generative model does","id":"1-what-a-generative-model-does"},{"n":"2 · Autoencoders and VAEs","id":"2-autoencoders-and-vaes"},{"n":"3 · GANs","id":"3-gans"},{"n":"4 · Diffusion models","id":"4-diffusion-models"},{"n":"5 · Flow matching (current state of the art)","id":"5-flow-matching-current-state-of-the-art"},{"n":"6 · Also know","id":"6-also-know"},{"n":"Practice (answers hidden)","id":"practice-answers-hidden"},{"n":"Build","id":"build"},{"n":"Go deeper (all optional)","id":"go-deeper-all-optional"},{"n":"Done when","id":"done-when"}],"x":"10 · Generative Models Module 10 ⏱ 8 min read View Markdown source Goal: learn the four big ideas for modeling p(x): autoregressive, VAE, GAN, diffusion/flow. · Time: 25–35 h · Needs: 01, 05 🔭 This module is where physics pays off most: diffusion is Langevin dynamics, score matching is about gradients of log-densities, flow matching moves probability mass by ODEs (continuity equation), and energy-based models are Boltzmann distributions. 🎯 What you'll learn By the end of this chapter you can: compare the four families of generative models and say when each is used; derive the ELBO , explain the reparameterization trick , and train a VAE; write the GAN objective and explain mode collapse; explain diffusion models in both the noise-prediction and the score/SDE views, including classifier-free guidance and latent diffusion; explain flow matching , the current state of the art, in one paragraph. Skip test Derive the ELBO : log p(x) ge mathbb E_q(z|x)[log p(x|z)] - D_KL(q(z|x)\\,\\|\\,p(z)). Explain the reparameterization trick and why it's needed. Write the GAN min-max objective. Explain mode collapse. Describe the DDPM forward process q(x_tmid x_0) in closed form, and what the network predicts. Explain classifier-free guidance. Explain the idea of flow matching / rectified flow in one paragraph. 1 · What a generative model does A classifier learns p(ymid x). A generative model learns the data distribution p(x) itself, so it can produce new samples that look like the training data (images, audio, text, molecules), and sometimes evaluate how likely a sample is. 📖 Definition · The four families Family Idea Pros Cons Used in Autoregressive p(x)=prod_t p(x_tmid x_<t) exact likelihood, simple slow sequential sampling GPT, audio LMs VAE latent z, maximize the ELBO fast, smooth latent space blurry samples the latent space of Stable Diffusion GAN generator vs discriminator game sharp, fast sampling unstable, mode collapse StyleGAN, super-resolution Diffusion / flow learn to reverse a noising process top quality, stable training many sampling steps Stable Diffusion, image/video/audio models Autoregressive models were covered in modules 07–09. This chapter covers the other three. 2 · Autoencoders and VAEs 📖 Definition · Autoencoder An encoder compresses x into a small code z (the bottleneck ); a decoder reconstructs hat x from z; train on reconstruction error. It learns compression, but it isn't a generative model: random codes z decode to garbage, because nothing organizes the code space. 📖 Definition · Variational autoencoder (VAE) Make the code space a probability distribution: prior p(z)=mathcal N(0,I): how we'll sample new codes; encoder q_phi(zmid x) = mathcal N(boldsymbolmu_phi(x),\\ diag\\,boldsymbolsigma^2_phi(x)): a fuzzy region of code space for each input; decoder p_theta(xmid z): turns a code into data. To generate, sample zsimmathcal N(0,I) and decode. 🧮 Formula · The ELBO (evidence lower bound) log p_theta(x) \\;ge\\; underbracemathbb E_q_phi(zmid x)big[log p_theta(xmid z)big]_reconstruction \\;-\\; underbraceD_KLbig(q_phi(zmid x)\\,\\|\\,p(z)big)_stay close to the prior Maximize the right-hand side over theta and phi. The loss is −ELBO = reconstruction error + KL penalty. ✍️ Worked example · Deriving the ELBO log p(x) = logint p(x,z)\\,dz = logmathbb E_q(zmid x)Big[fracp(x,z)q(zmid x)Big] ge mathbb E_qBig[logfracp(xmid z)\\,p(z)q(zmid x)Big] by Jensen's inequality (log is concave) = mathbb E_q[log p(xmid z)] - D_KL(q(zmid x)\\,\\|\\,p(z)). The gap is exactly D_KL(q(zmid x)\\,\\|\\,p(zmid x)), so the bound is tight when the encoder matches the true posterior. 🧮 Formula · KL between Gaussians (closed form) For one latent dimension: D_KLbig(mathcal N(mu,sigma^2)\\,\\|\\,mathcal N(0,1)big) = tfrac12big(mu^2+sigma^2-1-logsigma^2big) Sum over dimensions. It is 0 only when mu=0,sigma=1. 📖 Definition · Reparameterization trick We need gradients of mathbb E_zsim q_phi[cdot] with respect to phi, but you can't backpropagate through \"draw a random sample\". Rewrite the sample as a deterministic function of the parameters plus independent noise: z = boldsymbolmu_phi(x) + boldsymbolsigma_phi(x)odotboldsymbolepsilon,qquadboldsymbolepsilonsimmathcal N(0,I) Now the randomness sits in boldsymbolepsilon, and gradients flow through boldsymbolmu and boldsymbolsigma normally. 🔭 Intuition · Physics view The negative ELBO is the variational free energy of statistical mechanics: an \"energy\" term (reconstruction) minus an \"entropy\" term, minimized over a family of trial distributions. 3 · GANs 🧮 Formula · The GAN game A generator G turns noise z into fake samples; a discriminator D outputs the probability that its input is real. They play min_Gmax_D\\ mathbb E_xsim p_data[log D(x)] + mathbb E_zsim p(z)[log(1-D(G(z)))] For a fixed G, the best discriminator is D^*(x)=fracp_data(x)p_data(x)+p_G(x), and the generator's objective then becomes (up to constants) the Jensen–Shannon divergence between p_data and p_G. ⚠️ Common mistake · Mode collapse and instability The generator can \"win\" by producing only a few kinds of convincing samples ( mode collapse : e.g. only one digit). Training is a moving target for both players and can oscillate or diverge. Fixes: Wasserstein GANs (WGAN), spectral normalization, careful architectures (StyleGAN). GANs are mostly displaced by diffusion, but adversarial losses live on (sharper VAE decoders, distilling diffusion models into one-step generators). 4 · Diffusion models Idea: destroying data by adding noise is easy. Learn to undo it , one small step at a time. Then start from pure noise and run the undo process to create new data. 🧮 Formula · Forward (noising) process Over t=1..T (e.g. T=1000) add a little Gaussian noise with variance beta_t. With alpha_t=1-beta_t and baralpha_t=prod_sle talpha_s, you can jump straight to any step: q(x_tmid x_0) = mathcal Nbig(sqrtbaralpha_t\\,x_0,\\ (1-baralpha_t)Ibig)quadLongleftrightarrowquad x_t = sqrtbaralpha_t\\,x_0 + sqrt1-baralpha_t\\,boldsymbolepsilon,\\ \\ boldsymbolepsilonsimmathcal N(0,I) As tto T, baralpha_tto0 and x_T is pure noise. 🧮 Formula · Training (DDPM) Pick a random image x_0, a random step t and random noise boldsymbolepsilon; build x_t; train a network to predict the noise : mathcal L = big\\|boldsymbolepsilon - boldsymbolepsilon_theta(x_t, t)big\\|^2 Literally an MSE regression. That simplicity and stability is why diffusion overtook GANs. 🧮 Formula · Sampling Start from x_Tsimmathcal N(0,I) and step backwards: x_t-1 = frac1sqrtalpha_tBig(x_t - fracbeta_tsqrt1-baralpha_t\\,boldsymbolepsilon_theta(x_t,t)Big) + sigma_t\\,mathbf z,qquad mathbf zsimmathcal N(0,I) Each step removes a little predicted noise and adds back a little fresh noise. Many steps (hundreds) are needed in the original form. 📖 Definition · The score view The score of a distribution is nabla_xlog p(x): the direction in which the data becomes more likely. The noise predictor is a scaled score estimate, boldsymbolepsilon_theta(x_t,t)approx-sqrt1-baralpha_t\\,nabla_xlog p_t(x_t). Sampling then looks like Langevin dynamics (follow the score uphill plus noise) and can be written as a reverse-time stochastic differential equation (Song et al., 2021). 🔭 Brownian motion forwards, a drift toward the data backwards; the densities evolve by a Fokker–Planck equation. 📖 Definition · Faster sampling DDIM turns sampling into a deterministic ODE that works with far fewer steps (e.g. 20–50). Distillation and consistency models train a student to jump in one to a few steps. 🧮 Formula · Classifier-free guidance (CFG) Train one network both with the condition c (e.g. a text prompt) and without it (by randomly dropping c). At sampling time, extrapolate away from the unconditional prediction: hatboldsymbolepsilon = boldsymbolepsilon_theta(x_t,varnothing) + wbig(boldsymbolepsilon_theta(x_t,c)-boldsymbolepsilon_theta(x_t,varnothing)big) w=1 is plain conditioning; w>1 (e.g. 3–8) follows the prompt more closely at the cost of diversity. 📖 Definition · Latent diffusion and DiT Running diffusion on full-resolution pixels is expensive. Latent diffusion (Stable Diffusion) first compresses images with a VAE (e.g. 512×512×3 → 64×64×4) and runs diffusion in that latent space; the VAE decoder turns the result back into pixels. Text conditions the denoiser through cross-attention on text-encoder embeddings (CLIP/T5). The denoiser was a U-Net (module 06); modern models use a Diffusion Transformer (DiT) . ▶ But how do AI images and videos actually work? ▶️ But how do AI images and videos actually work? · Welch Labs on 3Blue1Brown · 37 min (plays here; or open on YouTube) 5 · Flow matching (current state of the art) 🧮 Formula · Flow matching / rectified flow Learn a velocity field v_theta(x,t) so that following the ODE dot x = v_theta(x,t) from t=0 to 1 carries noise to data. Training is again a plain regression: take noise x_0, data x_1, a random t, the straight-line point x_t = (1-t)\\,x_0 + t\\,x_1, and minimize mathcal L = big\\|v_theta(x_t,t) - (x_1 - x_0)big\\|^2 Paths are close to straight, so sampling needs few steps. Used in Stable Diffusion 3, Flux and many video models. 🔭 Intuition · Physics view The density moves by a continuity equation, partial_t p + nablacdot(p\\,v)=0: the network learns the velocity field that transports probability mass from the noise distribution to the data distribution. 6 · Also know 🧮 Formula · Normalizing flows Build x = f(z) from a stack of invertible layers with easy Jacobian determinants. Then the exact likelihood follows from the change-of-variables formula: log p(x) = log p(z) + logBig|detfracpartial zpartial xBig|,qquad z=f^-1(x) 📖 Definition · Energy-based models p(x) = e^-E_theta(x)/Z with a learned energy E_theta (🔭 a Boltzmann distribution). Very flexible, but the normalizer Z is intractable, so training and sampling rely on MCMC or score-based tricks. 🎛️ Try it · Flashcards Choose the 10 GenAI deck and drill this chapter's key facts with spaced repetition, then do the practice questions below. Practice (answers hidden) Compute D_KL(mathcal N(1, 0.5^2)\\,\\|\\,mathcal N(0,1)) in one dimension. Answer tfrac12(1+0.25-1-ln0.25)=tfrac12(0.25+1.386)approx0.82 nats. Why can't you just backpropagate through z = torch.normal(mu, sigma) , and how does the reparameterization fix it? Answer Sampling is not a differentiable function of mu and sigma (the gradient of a random draw is undefined). Writing z = mu + sigma * eps with eps = torch.randn_like(mu) makes z a smooth function of the parameters, and the randomness comes from an input that needs no gradient. With baralpha_t=0.25, write x_t in terms of x_0 and boldsymbolepsilon. How much of the signal is left? Answer x_t=0.5\\,x_0+sqrt0.75\\,boldsymbolepsilonapprox0.5\\,x_0+0.866\\,boldsymbolepsilon. Half the amplitude (a quarter of the variance) of the original image remains. At the GAN optimum, p_G=p_data. What does the optimal discriminator output? Answer D^*(x)=fracpp+p=tfrac12 everywhere: it can't tell real from fake. CFG with w=3: the unconditional prediction is 0.2 and the conditional one 0.5 (one coordinate). What is the guided prediction? Answer 0.2+3(0.5-0.2)=1.1: pushed well past the conditional prediction, in the direction the prompt points. In flow matching with x_0=(0,0) (noise) and x_1=(2,4) (data), what is x_0.25 and the regression target? Answer x_0.25=0.75\\,(0,0)+0.25\\,(2,4)=(0.5,1); target x_1-x_0=(2,4) (the same for every t along this straight path). Build VAE on MNIST: visualize the 2D latent space and interpolate between digits. DCGAN on a small image dataset. Observe instabilities and mode collapse. DDPM from scratch (small U-Net) on MNIST/Fashion-MNIST, then add class conditioning + classifier-free guidance . Flow matching on 2D toy data (e.g. moons → spiral), then on MNIST. Compare sampling steps with DDPM. Stretch: fine-tune Stable Diffusion with LoRA/DreamBooth on your own images using diffusers . Go deeper (all optional) ⭐ Understanding Deep Learning (Prince) ch. 14–18 (unsupervised, GANs, flows, VAEs, diffusion). The clearest unified treatment, with notebooks. ⭐ MIT 6.S184: Introduction to Flow Matching and Diffusion Models : lecture notes + labs. Built on SDEs/ODEs, perfect for a physics mind. Lilian Weng: What are Diffusion Models? · From Autoencoder to Beta-VAE · From GAN to WGAN . Yang Song: Generative Modeling by Estimating Gradients of the Data Distribution : the score/SDE view. Hugging Face Diffusion Models Course + the diffusers library. Papers: VAE · GAN · DDPM · Score SDE · Latent Diffusion · CFG · DiT · Flow Matching . Done when ELBO derived on paper · your DDPM generates recognizable digits · you can explain diffusion in both the noise-prediction and the score/SDE views"},{"t":"11 · Reinforcement Learning","u":"deep-learning/11-reinforcement-learning.html","s":"Deep Learning","h":[{"n":"Skip test","id":"skip-test"},{"n":"1 · The reinforcement-learning problem","id":"1-the-reinforcement-learning-problem"},{"n":"2 · Value functions and the Bellman equations","id":"2-value-functions-and-the-bellman-equations"},{"n":"3 · Learning from experience: TD and Q-learning","id":"3-learning-from-experience-td-and-q-learning"},{"n":"4 · Policy gradients","id":"4-policy-gradients"},{"n":"5 · RL for large language models","id":"5-rl-for-large-language-models"},{"n":"Practice (answers hidden)","id":"practice-answers-hidden"},{"n":"Build","id":"build"},{"n":"Go deeper (all optional)","id":"go-deeper-all-optional"},{"n":"Done when","id":"done-when"}],"x":"11 · Reinforcement Learning Module 11 ⏱ 8 min read View Markdown source Goal: learn the core ideas of RL, deep RL algorithms, and RL's role in modern LLMs. · Time: 25–35 h · Needs: 01, 05 🎯 What you'll learn By the end of this chapter you can: define a Markov decision process, returns, policies and value functions; write the Bellman equations and solve small problems with value iteration; learn from experience with TD learning and Q-learning , and scale it up with DQN ; derive the policy gradient , and explain baselines, actor-critic, GAE and PPO ; explain how RL post-trains LLMs (RLHF, GRPO) and why reward hacking is a risk. Skip test Define an MDP (S, A, P, R, gamma), return, policy, V^pi, Q^pi. Write the Bellman expectation and optimality equations. Explain exploration vs exploitation and ε-greedy. Explain Q-learning's update, and what DQN adds (replay buffer, target network). Derive the policy gradient nabla J = mathbb E[nablalogpi_theta(a|s)\\,A(s,a)]. Explain why baselines reduce variance. Explain PPO's clipped objective, and how RLHF/GRPO use it for LLMs. 1 · The reinforcement-learning problem In supervised learning someone tells the model the right answer. In reinforcement learning (RL) an agent acts in an environment and only receives a reward signal saying how good things went, often much later. It must discover good behaviour by trial and error. 📖 Definition · Markov decision process (MDP) An MDP is a tuple (S, A, P, R, gamma): S: states, A: actions; P(s'mid s,a): the probability of moving to s' after taking a in s; R(s,a): the expected reward; gammain[0,1): the discount factor . Markov means the next state depends only on the current state and action, not on the earlier history. At each step: observe s_t, choose a_t, receive r_t and s_t+1. 📖 Definition · Policy and return A policy pi(amid s) is the agent's behaviour: the probability of each action in each state. The return from time t is the discounted sum of future rewards: G_t = r_t + gamma r_t+1 + gamma^2 r_t+2+dots = sum_kge0gamma^k r_t+k The goal is a policy that maximizes the expected return. gamma makes near rewards count more than far ones and keeps infinite sums finite. ✍️ Worked example · A return Rewards 1, 0, 2 then nothing, gamma=0.9: G_0 = 1 + 0.9cdot0 + 0.81cdot2 = 2.62. 📖 Definition · Exploration vs exploitation To do well you must exploit what you know (pick the best-looking action) but also explore (try others, which might be better). ε-greedy: with probability varepsilon take a random action, otherwise the greedy one; decay varepsilon over time. ▶ RL Course by David Silver, Lecture 1: Introduction to Reinforcement Learning ▶️ RL Course by David Silver, Lecture 1: Introduction to Reinforcement Learning · David Silver (UCL / DeepMind), the first of 10 lectures (plays here; or open on YouTube) 2 · Value functions and the Bellman equations 📖 Definition · Value functions and advantage V^pi(s) = mathbb E_pi[G_tmid s_t=s]qquad Q^pi(s,a) = mathbb E_pi[G_tmid s_t=s,\\,a_t=a]qquad A^pi(s,a)=Q^pi(s,a)-V^pi(s) V: how good is this state (following pi)? Q: how good is this action here? The advantage A: how much better is this action than the policy's average? 🧮 Formula · Bellman equations Expectation (for a given policy): the value of a state is the immediate reward plus the discounted value of where you end up: V^pi(s) = sum_api(amid s)Big[R(s,a)+gammasum_s'P(s'mid s,a)\\,V^pi(s')Big] Optimality (for the best policy): Q^*(s,a) = R(s,a) + gammasum_s'P(s'mid s,a)\\,max_a'Q^*(s',a') Once you know Q^*, the optimal policy is simply greedy: pi^*(s)=argmax_a Q^*(s,a). 🔭 The same principle of optimality underlies the Hamilton–Jacobi–Bellman equation in control. 📖 Definition · Dynamic programming (when the model is known) Value iteration: start with V=0 and repeatedly apply V(s)leftarrowmax_abig[R(s,a)+gammasum_s'P(s'mid s,a)V(s')big] until it stops changing. Policy iteration: alternate evaluating the current policy and making it greedy with respect to its values. Both need P and R, which real problems rarely give you. ✍️ Worked example · Value iteration on a tiny chain States A → B → goal. Moving right gives reward 0, except B → goal which gives 1. gamma=0.9. After convergence: V(B)=1, V(A)=0+0.9cdot1=0.9. Values \"flow back\" from the reward, discounted at each step. 3 · Learning from experience: TD and Q-learning 🧮 Formula · Temporal-difference (TD) learning After one step (s, r, s'), move V(s) toward a bootstrapped target, using your own estimate of the next state: V(s)leftarrow V(s)+alphabig[underbracer+gamma V(s')_TD target-V(s)big] The bracket is the TD error delta. Unlike Monte Carlo (wait for the full return), TD learns after every step. ✍️ Worked example · One TD update V(s)=0.5, r=1, gamma=0.9, V(s')=2, alpha=0.1. Target =1+1.8=2.8, error =2.3, new V(s)=0.5+0.23=0.73. 🧮 Formula · Q-learning Q(s,a)leftarrow Q(s,a)+alphaBig[r+gammamax_a'Q(s',a')-Q(s,a)Big] Off-policy: the target uses the best next action regardless of what the agent actually does next, so it learns Q^* even while exploring with ε-greedy. (SARSA uses the action actually taken instead: on-policy.) 📖 Definition · Deep Q-network (DQN) Replace the table with a neural net Q_theta(s,a) (Atari from pixels, 2013/2015) and minimize big(r+gammamax_a'Q_bartheta(s',a')-Q_theta(s,a)big)^2. Two tricks make it stable: Replay buffer: store past transitions and train on random mini-batches of them, breaking the correlation between consecutive steps. Target network Q_bartheta: a slowly updated copy used for the target, so the network isn't chasing its own moving prediction. 4 · Policy gradients Value-based methods struggle with continuous or huge action spaces. Policy-gradient methods instead parameterize the policy directly, pi_theta(amid s) (e.g. a network outputting action probabilities), and do gradient ascent on the expected return J(theta). 🧮 Formula · The policy gradient theorem nabla_theta J(theta) = mathbb E_pi_thetaBig[sum_tnabla_thetalogpi_theta(a_tmid s_t)\\;A_tBig] Meaning: raise the log-probability of actions that turned out better than average (A_t>0) and lower it for worse ones. ✍️ Worked example · Where it comes from (the log-derivative trick) For a trajectory tau with probability p_theta(tau) and return R(tau): nabla_thetamathbb E[R] = int R\\,nabla_theta p_theta = int R\\,p_thetanabla_thetalog p_theta = mathbb E[R\\,nabla_thetalog p_theta(tau)]. The environment's transition probabilities don't depend on theta, so nabla_thetalog p_theta(tau)=sum_tnabla_thetalogpi_theta(a_tmid s_t). No model of the environment needed. 📖 Definition · REINFORCE and baselines REINFORCE uses the sampled return G_t in place of A_t: unbiased but very noisy. Subtracting a baseline b(s_t) that doesn't depend on the action (usually V(s_t)) keeps the gradient unbiased, because mathbb E[nablalogpicdot b(s)]=b(s)nablasum_api(amid s)=0, while greatly reducing variance. 📖 Definition · Actor-critic and GAE The actor is the policy; the critic learns V(s) to provide the baseline. Generalized advantage estimation blends one-step and long-horizon estimates: delta_t = r_t+gamma V(s_t+1)-V(s_t),qquad hat A_t = sum_lge0(gammalambda)^l\\,delta_t+l lambda=0: low variance, more bias; lambda=1: Monte Carlo. lambdaapprox0.95 is typical. 🧮 Formula · PPO (proximal policy optimization) With the probability ratio r_t(theta)=pi_theta(a_tmid s_t)/pi_old(a_tmid s_t): L^CLIP(theta)=mathbb E_tBig[minbig(r_that A_t,\\ clip(r_t,\\,1-epsilon,\\,1+epsilon)\\,hat A_tbig)Big],qquadepsilonapprox0.2 Collect a batch with the old policy, then take several gradient steps on it; the clip removes any incentive to move the policy too far in one update. Simple, robust: the workhorse algorithm of deep RL and RLHF. 📖 Definition · Other important ideas SAC (soft actor-critic): maximize reward plus policy entropy, for continuous control. 🔭 Free energy at a temperature again. Model-based RL: learn a model of the environment and plan with it (Dreamer, MuZero). AlphaGo / AlphaZero: Monte-Carlo tree search guided by policy and value networks, trained by self-play. 5 · RL for large language models 📖 Definition · The LLM as a policy State = the prompt plus the tokens so far; action = the next token; the episode ends when the answer is complete, and a reward scores the whole answer. RLHF: reward from a learned human-preference model, optimized with PPO plus a KL penalty to the SFT model (module 09). RL with verifiable rewards: reward = the math answer is correct, or the code passes the unit tests. GRPO samples a group of answers per prompt and uses each answer's reward relative to the group, A_i=(r_i-bar r)/std(r), in a PPO-style update with no critic network. This is what produces \"reasoning\" models. ⚠️ Common mistake · Reward hacking The policy maximizes the reward you wrote, not the one you meant: e.g. a reward model that likes long answers gets padded answers; a test-based reward gets code that special-cases the tests. KL penalties, better reward models and careful evaluation are the defences. 💡 Key idea · Why RL is hard Sparse and delayed rewards (credit assignment), data that changes as the policy changes, high-variance gradients, and extreme sensitivity to hyperparameters and random seeds. Always run several seeds and compare against a known-good implementation. 🎛️ Try it · Flashcards Choose the 11 RL deck and drill this chapter's key facts with spaced repetition, then do the practice questions below. Practice (answers hidden) Rewards 0, 0, 0, 10 (then the episode ends), gamma=0.9. What is G_0? And with gamma=0.5? Answer 0.9^3cdot10=7.29; 0.5^3cdot10=1.25. A smaller gamma makes the agent short-sighted. Q-learning: Q(s,a)=2, r=-1, gamma=0.9, max_a'Q(s',a')=5, alpha=0.5. What is the new Q(s,a)? Answer Target =-1+4.5=3.5; error =1.5; new Q=2+0.75=2.75. Why does DQN need a replay buffer and a target network, when tabular Q-learning doesn't? Answer With a neural net, updating on one transition changes Q everywhere. Consecutive transitions are highly correlated (breaking the i.i.d. assumption of SGD), and the target moves every time the network updates, which can make learning oscillate or diverge. The buffer decorrelates the data; the frozen target stabilizes the regression target. Show that subtracting a baseline b(s) doesn't bias the policy gradient. Answer mathbb E_asimpi[nabla_thetalogpi_theta(amid s)\\,b(s)] = b(s)sum_api_theta(amid s)nabla_thetalogpi_theta(amid s) = b(s)nabla_thetasum_api_theta(amid s)=b(s)nabla_theta1=0. In PPO with epsilon=0.2, an action has hat A=+1 and the ratio is already 1.5. What is its contribution to the objective, and its gradient? Answer min(1.5cdot1,\\ 1.2cdot1)=1.2. It's on the clipped branch, which is constant in theta, so the gradient is 0: no further push to increase that action's probability in this update. GRPO samples four answers with rewards 1, 0, 0, 1. What are the advantages? Answer Mean 0.5, standard deviation 0.5: advantages +1, -1, -1, +1. Correct answers are reinforced, incorrect ones discouraged, with no critic. Build Tabular Q-learning on FrozenLake / CliffWalking. Visualize the value function. DQN on CartPole (then Atari Pong if you have a GPU). REINFORCE → actor-critic → PPO on CartPole/LunarLander, each written by you and compared against CleanRL. Stretch: a GRPO-style RL fine-tune of a tiny LM on arithmetic problems with a verifiable reward ( trl has a GRPOTrainer ). 🔭 Fun: train an agent on a physics environment (MuJoCo via Gymnasium: Hopper, Ant). Go deeper (all optional) ⭐ Sutton & Barto: Reinforcement Learning, an Introduction (2nd ed.) : free. Ch. 1–6, 9, 13. The foundational text. ⭐ OpenAI Spinning Up in Deep RL : the bridge to deep RL. Read \"Introduction to RL\" parts 1–3, then the algorithm docs. David Silver's RL course (UCL/DeepMind) : classic lectures that pair with Sutton & Barto (lecture 1 is embedded above). Hugging Face Deep RL Course : hands-on and free. CleanRL : single-file, readable implementations to study. Gymnasium for environments. The 37 Implementation Details of PPO : why your PPO doesn't work. Advanced: UC Berkeley CS285 (Sergey Levine) . Done when Your PPO solves LunarLander · you can explain how RL is used to post-train LLMs"},{"t":"12 · Advanced Frontiers & Specializations","u":"deep-learning/12-advanced-frontiers.html","s":"Deep Learning","h":[{"n":"How to read papers (do this first)","id":"how-to-read-papers-do-this-first"},{"n":"Track A · Deep learning theory 🔭","id":"track-a-deep-learning-theory"},{"n":"Track B · Mechanistic interpretability & AI safety","id":"track-b-mechanistic-interpretability-ai-safety"},{"n":"Track C · Scientific ML & physics-informed DL 🔭","id":"track-c-scientific-ml-physics-informed-dl"},{"n":"Track D · ML systems, GPUs & efficiency 💻","id":"track-d-ml-systems-gpus-efficiency"},{"n":"Track E · Self-supervised, multimodal & representation learning","id":"track-e-self-supervised-multimodal-representation-learning"},{"n":"Track F · Graphs & beyond-transformer architectures","id":"track-f-graphs-beyond-transformer-architectures"},{"n":"Track G · Other frameworks worth knowing","id":"track-g-other-frameworks-worth-knowing"},{"n":"Becoming a contributor (high-school senior edition)","id":"becoming-a-contributor-high-school-senior-edition"}],"x":"12 · Advanced Frontiers & Specializations Module 12 ⏱ 8 min read View Markdown source Goal: move from student to researcher/engineer : read papers, go deep in one area. · Time: open-ended · Needs: 08 (+ 10/11 for some tracks) There's no skip test here. Pick 1–2 tracks that excite you and go deep, and treat the rest as awareness. Each track explains its core ideas on this page, then gives the ⭐ entry point for going further. 🎯 What you'll learn By the end of this chapter you can: read a research paper efficiently and reproduce its key result; explain the central idea of each frontier area in a few sentences (theory, interpretability, scientific ML, systems, self-supervised learning, graphs and state-space models); choose a specialization and know exactly where to start. How to read papers (do this first) 📖 Definition · The three-pass method (Keshav, \"How to Read a Paper\" ) 10 minutes: title, abstract, figures, conclusion. What problem, what claim? 1 hour: the main method, skipping proofs. Could you explain it to a friend? Re-derive and re-implement the key result at small scale. 💡 Key idea · Reimplement > reread The best way to understand a paper is to reproduce its key result at small scale. Keep a paper log in progress.md : one line per paper (problem → idea → result → my doubt). 📚 Go deeper (optional) Find papers and code: arXiv (cs.LG, cs.CL, cs.CV), Hugging Face Papers (daily trending, with linked code), alphaXiv (discussion), and authors' blogs. Track A · Deep learning theory 🔭 A great fit for a math/physics mind: why does deep learning work at all? 📖 Definition · Generalization puzzles Over-parameterized networks can fit random labels perfectly, yet generalize on real data, so classical \"count the parameters\" bounds can't explain them. Double descent: as model size grows, test error first follows the classic U-curve, peaks near the point where the model can just fit the training data, then falls again. Grokking: on some small tasks, a network memorizes the training set and only much later, with continued training and weight decay, suddenly generalizes. Implicit bias: gradient descent itself prefers certain solutions (e.g. low-norm or \"flat\" minima) among the many that fit the data. 🧮 Formula · Neural tangent kernel (NTK) Theta(mathbf x,mathbf x') = nabla_theta f_theta(mathbf x)cdotnabla_theta f_theta(mathbf x') In the infinite-width limit with standard initialization, Theta stays fixed during training, and the network behaves like kernel regression (the \"lazy\" regime: features don't change). Real networks do learn features; the μP / mean-field parameterization keeps feature learning at large width and lets you transfer tuned hyperparameters from small to large models. 📖 Definition · Optimization landscape High-dimensional losses have far more saddle points than bad local minima. Training often runs at the edge of stability , where the sharpness (largest Hessian eigenvalue, module 01) hovers around 2/eta. 📚 Go deeper (optional) ⭐ Understanding Deep Learning (Prince) ch. 20 (\"Why does deep learning work?\") → The Principles of Deep Learning Theory (Roberts, Yaida & Hanin) (effective field theory for neural nets, written by physicists). Also: Deep Learning, Bishop & Bishop (2024) , Telgarsky's Deep Learning Theory notes . Track B · Mechanistic interpretability & AI safety Reverse-engineer how a trained network computes, the way you'd reverse-engineer a compiled program. 📖 Definition · Core concepts Circuits: small subgraphs of a network (specific heads and neurons) that implement an understandable algorithm. Induction heads: a two-head circuit in transformers that finds an earlier occurrence of the current token and copies what came next (\"…A B … A → B\"). It is a key mechanism of in-context learning. Superposition: networks represent more features than they have neurons by storing them as nearly-orthogonal directions, so single neurons are often \"polysemantic\". Attribution graphs: trace which features caused a given output. 🧮 Formula · Sparse autoencoder (SAE) To pull interpretable features out of superposition, train an overcomplete autoencoder on a layer's activations mathbf x with a sparsity penalty: mathbf f = ReLU(W_emathbf x+mathbf b_e),qquad mathcal L = \\|mathbf x - W_dmathbf f\\|^2 + lambda\\|mathbf f\\|_1 Each column of W_d is then a candidate \"feature direction\", often with a clear meaning. 📖 Definition · Safety Alignment (getting systems to do what we intend), reward hacking (module 11), scalable oversight (supervising systems smarter than the supervisor), and evaluations for dangerous capabilities. 📚 Go deeper (optional) ⭐ ARENA curriculum (hands-on, free notebooks) + TransformerLens . Read: Transformer Circuits Thread (A Mathematical Framework, Toy Models of Superposition, Scaling Monosemanticity), Neel Nanda's getting-started guide , BlueDot Impact courses . Track C · Scientific ML & physics-informed DL 🔭 🧮 Formula · Physics-informed neural networks (PINNs) Fit a network u_theta(x,t) to a solution of a differential equation mathcal N[u]=0 by putting the equation itself into the loss, evaluated at random \"collocation\" points: mathcal L = underbracesum_ibig(u_theta(x_i,t_i)-u_ibig)^2_data / boundary conditions + lambdaunderbracesum_jbig(mathcal N[u_theta](x_j,t_j)big)^2_PDE residual Derivatives in mathcal N come from autodiff. 📖 Definition · Neural operators Learn a map between function spaces (e.g. from an initial condition to the solution at time T), so one trained model solves a whole family of PDE instances. Examples: Fourier Neural Operator (FNO), DeepONet. 🧮 Formula · Equivariance A network f is equivariant to a group of transformations g (rotations, translations, permutations) if f(gcdot x) = gcdot f(x) CNNs are translation-equivariant (module 06); equivariant GNNs build in rotations for molecules and particle physics. Geometric deep learning organizes architectures by their symmetries. 📖 Definition · Energy-conserving and continuous-time models Neural ODEs treat depth as continuous time, dotmathbf h=f_theta(mathbf h,t). Hamiltonian / Lagrangian neural networks learn an energy function and derive the dynamics from it, so energy is conserved by construction. Applications: AlphaFold (proteins), GraphCast (weather), ML for particle physics and cosmology, molecular dynamics potentials. 📚 Go deeper (optional) ⭐ Geometric Deep Learning (Bronstein et al.) (book + lectures) and Steve Brunton's Physics-Informed ML lectures (YouTube) . Also DeepXDE for PINNs. Track D · ML systems, GPUs & efficiency 💻 Where your C++ shines. 📖 Definition · GPU fundamentals A GPU has many streaming multiprocessors (SMs) , each running groups of 32 threads ( warps ) in lockstep. Memory is a hierarchy, from large and slow to small and fast: HBM (GPU main memory) → L2 cache → shared memory/SRAM (per SM) → registers. Fast kernels keep data in the fast levels as long as possible. 🧮 Formula · Roofline model Arithmetic intensity I = FLOPs performed per byte moved from memory. Then attainable FLOP/s = minbig(peak FLOP/s,\\ memory bandwidthtimes Ibig) Low-intensity operations (element-wise ops, LLM decoding at batch 1, module 09) are memory-bound ; large matrix multiplications are compute-bound . Kernel fusion and FlashAttention raise intensity. 📖 Definition · Distributed training Data parallel (DDP): every GPU holds the whole model and a different slice of the batch; gradients are averaged with an all-reduce . FSDP / ZeRO: shard parameters, gradients and optimizer state across GPUs to fit bigger models. Tensor parallel: split individual matrix multiplications across GPUs. Pipeline parallel: put different layers on different GPUs. 📖 Definition · Efficiency techniques Quantization (int8/int4, GPTQ/AWQ), pruning, distillation into smaller models, speculative decoding (module 09), torch.compile , and custom kernels in CUDA (C++) or Triton (Python): tiled matmul, fused softmax, FlashAttention. 📚 Go deeper (optional) ⭐ Programming Massively Parallel Processors (Hwu, Kirk, El Hajj) + the GPU MODE lecture series & Discord. Also: How to Scale Your Model (Google DeepMind, JAX/TPU book) , Triton tutorials , Horace He: Making Deep Learning Go Brrrr , The Ultra-Scale Playbook , llm.c (Karpathy, GPT-2 in raw C/CUDA) . Track E · Self-supervised, multimodal & representation learning Learn useful representations without labels, by inventing a prediction task from the data itself. 🧮 Formula · Contrastive learning (InfoNCE) Make two augmented views of the same example (a positive pair) close in embedding space and different examples far apart: mathcal L_i = -logfracexp(sim(mathbf z_i,mathbf z_i^+)/tau)sum_kexp(sim(mathbf z_i,mathbf z_k)/tau) sim is cosine similarity, tau a temperature, and the sum runs over the positive and all negatives in the batch. It is a softmax classification: \"which of these is my partner?\" (SimCLR, MoCo.) CLIP applies the same loss to matching images with their captions. 📖 Definition · Other self-supervised objectives Masked modelling: hide parts of the input and predict them (BERT for text, MAE for images). Self-distillation: a student network matches a slowly updated teacher on different views (DINO / DINOv2). JEPA: predict the representation of a hidden part rather than its pixels. 📖 Definition · Multimodal models Vision-language models (LLaVA-style) connect a vision encoder to an LLM through a small projector, so image patches become tokens the LLM can read. Similar recipes give audio (Whisper) and video models. 📚 Go deeper (optional) ⭐ Lilian Weng: Contrastive Representation Learning + the CLIP paper . Course: CMU Multimodal ML . Track F · Graphs & beyond-transformer architectures 🧮 Formula · Graph neural networks (message passing) Each node updates its vector from its own state and an aggregate (sum, mean or max, written bigoplus) of messages from its neighbours N(v): mathbf h_v' = phiBig(mathbf h_v,\\ bigoplus_uin N(v)psi(mathbf h_u)Big) Stack k layers and each node sees its k-hop neighbourhood. Variants: GCN, GAT (attention over neighbours), graph transformers. Used for molecules, social networks and physics simulation. 🧮 Formula · State-space models (SSMs) A linear recurrence, discretized from a continuous linear ODE: mathbf h_t = bar A\\,mathbf h_t-1+bar B\\,mathbf x_t,qquad mathbf y_t = C\\,mathbf h_t Because it is linear, it can be computed in parallel during training (as a convolution or a parallel scan) and as a cheap recurrence during generation: linear in sequence length, unlike attention's T^2. Mamba makes bar A,bar B,C depend on the input (a selective SSM), so it can choose what to remember. Hybrids mix SSM and attention layers. 🔭 Discretized linear ODEs. 📚 Go deeper (optional) ⭐ Stanford CS224W: ML with Graphs + PyTorch Geometric . For SSMs: The Annotated S4 , the Mamba paper . Track G · Other frameworks worth knowing 📖 Definition · JAX NumPy with composable function transformations: grad (derivatives), jit (compile with XLA), vmap (automatic batching) and pmap /sharding (multiple devices). Functional style: models are pure functions of their parameters. Popular in research and on TPUs. Try it once you know PyTorch well: JAX docs . Becoming a contributor (high-school senior edition) Portfolio: 3–5 polished GitHub projects with READMEs and results (e.g. GPT from scratch, a DDPM, a paper reproduction). These count more than certificates. Competitions: the International Olympiad in AI (IOAI) is a science olympiad for high-school students , so it's worth checking whether your country runs a national selection. Also Kaggle and AIcrowd . Write: blog your reimplementations. Teaching forces understanding (the Feynman technique). Community: EleutherAI, GPU MODE, and Hugging Face Discords. Open-source contributions (docs, bug fixes) to transformers , diffusers , TransformerLens . Research programs for students: look for summer research programs at universities, MATS / ARENA (safety), and reaching out to grad students with a concrete reproduction you've done."},{"t":"Deep Learning Cheatsheet","u":"deep-learning/cheatsheet.html","s":"Deep Learning","h":[{"n":"Math (→ 01)","id":"math-01"},{"n":"ML basics (→ 03)","id":"ml-basics-03"},{"n":"Neural nets (→ 04)","id":"neural-nets-04"},{"n":"Training (→ 05)","id":"training-05"},{"n":"CNNs (→ 06)","id":"cnns-06"},{"n":"Sequences (→ 07)","id":"sequences-07"},{"n":"Transformers (→ 08)","id":"transformers-08"},{"n":"LLMs (→ 09)","id":"llms-09"},{"n":"Generative (→ 10)","id":"generative-10"},{"n":"RL (→ 11)","id":"rl-11"}],"x":"Deep Learning Cheatsheet Cheatsheet ⏱ 2 min read View Markdown source Everything key on one page. Skim this to recap, and open the module (→ NN) if a line feels unfamiliar. Math (→ 01) Concept Formula Linear-regression gradient nabla_mathbf w\\|Xmathbf w-mathbf y\\|^2 = 2X^top(Xmathbf w-mathbf y) Quadratic form gradient nabla(mathbf x^top Amathbf x) = (A+A^top)mathbf x Chain rule (Jacobians) J_fcirc g = J_f J_g; backprop = vector-Jacobian products, right to left SVD A = USigma V^top (rotate · scale · rotate) GD stability (quadratic) eta < 2/lambda_max(H) Bayes p(thetamid D)propto p(Dmidtheta)p(theta) Entropy / CE / KL H(p)=-sum plog p · H(p,q)=-sum plog q · D_KL=H(p,q)-H(p)ge0 Softmax sigma(mathbf z)_i = e^z_i/T/sum_j e^z_j/T (Boltzmann, temperature T) Losses as likelihoods Gaussian NLL → MSE · Categorical NLL → cross-entropy · Gaussian prior → L2 ML basics (→ 03) Logistic regression: hat y=sigma(mathbf w^topmathbf x), gradient X^top(hatmathbf y-mathbf y) High train error → underfit (bigger model). Train ≪ val → overfit (data, regularization). Precision =fracTPTP+FP · Recall =fracTPTP+FN · F1 = harmonic mean Split: train (fit) / val (decide) / test (report once). Fit preprocessing on train only. Neural nets (→ 04) Layer: mathbf h=phi(Wmathbf x+mathbf b). Batch linear Y=XW^top: partial L/partial W = (partial L/partial Y)^top X, partial L/partial X = (partial L/partial Y)W Softmax + CE: partial L/partialmathbf z = hatmathbf y-mathbf y Init: Xavier/Glorot Var=2/(n_in+n_out) (tanh) · LeCun 1/n_in · He 2/n_in (ReLU) Initial loss sanity check: approxln C for C balanced classes Training (→ 05) for x, y in loader: x, y = x.to(dev), y.to(dev) with torch.autocast(device_type=\"cuda\", dtype=torch.bfloat16): loss = loss_fn(model(x), y) opt.zero_grad(set_to_none=True) loss.backward() torch.nn.utils.clip_grad_norm_(model.parameters(), 1.0) opt.step(); sched.step() Defaults that usually work: AdamW (β=(0.9, 0.95–0.999), wd 0.01–0.1), warmup + cosine, bf16, grad clip 1.0. Debug order: check the data → check the initial loss → overfit one batch → scale up → change one thing at a time. BatchNorm normalizes over the batch (CNNs) · LayerNorm/RMSNorm over the features (transformers). CNNs (→ 06) Output size lfloor(n+2p-k)/srfloor+1 · Params C_inC_outk^2+C_out ResNet: mathbf y=mathbf x+F(mathbf x) · Transfer: small data → freeze; more data → fine-tune all at a low LR. Sequences (→ 07) LM: p(x_1:T)=prod_t p(x_tmid x_<t) · Perplexity =e^CE loss RNN gradients scale like lambda^T → vanish/explode. LSTM's additive cell state fixes it. Transformers (→ 08) Attn(Q,K,V)=softmax(QK^top/sqrtd_k+M)V, causal mask M_ij=-infty for j>i Block: x += attn(norm(x)); x += mlp(norm(x)) , MLP dto4dto d Params ≈ 12Ld^2 + Vd · Attention cost O(T^2d) · RoPE = relative position via rotation Shapes: (B,T,C) → split heads (B,h,T,C/h) → attention → merge back LLMs (→ 09) Training compute Capprox 6ND FLOPs · Chinchilla-optimal Dapprox20N Pipeline: pretrain → SFT → preference (RLHF-PPO / DPO) → RL with verifiable rewards (GRPO) LoRA: W + BA, rank rll d · KV cache: store past K,V → decoding is memory-bandwidth-bound RAG for knowledge , fine-tuning for behavior Generative (→ 10) ELBO: log p(x)gemathbb E_q[log p(x|z)]-D_KL(q(z|x)\\|p(z)) · Reparam: z=mu+sigmaepsilon GAN: min_Gmax_D mathbb E[log D(x)]+mathbb E[log(1-D(G(z)))] DDPM: x_t=sqrtbaralpha_tx_0+sqrt1-baralpha_tepsilon, loss \\|epsilon-epsilon_theta(x_t,t)\\|^2 CFG: hatepsilon=epsilon_u+w(epsilon_c-epsilon_u) · Flow matching: regress v_theta(x_t,t) onto x_1-x_0 RL (→ 11) Return G_t=sum_kgamma^kr_t+k · Bellman: Q^*(s,a)=mathbb E[r+gammamax_a'Q^*(s',a')] Q-learning: Qleftarrow Q+alpha[r+gammamax Q(s',cdot)-Q] Policy gradient: nabla J=mathbb E[nablalogpi_theta(a|s)\\,A(s,a)] PPO: mathbb E[min(r_tA_t,\\ clip(r_t,1pmepsilon)A_t)], r_t=pi_theta/pi_old"},{"t":"Resources: the curated master list","u":"deep-learning/resources.html","s":"Deep Learning","h":[{"n":"The core stack (if you only use five things)","id":"the-core-stack-if-you-only-use-five-things"},{"n":"Books (free online unless 💲)","id":"books-free-online-unless"},{"n":"Courses (lectures free on YouTube or the course site)","id":"courses-lectures-free-on-youtube-or-the-course-site"},{"n":"Blogs & interactive explainers","id":"blogs-interactive-explainers"},{"n":"Code to read (well-written references)","id":"code-to-read-well-written-references"},{"n":"Tools","id":"tools"},{"n":"Paper reading order","id":"paper-reading-order"}],"x":"Resources: the curated master list Resources ⏱ 4 min read View Markdown source Rule: ⭐ = the main resource, and the only one you need . Everything else is optional depth or an alternative explanation if the ⭐ doesn't click. All ⭐ items are free unless marked 💲. The core stack (if you only use five things) ⭐ Karpathy: Neural Networks Zero to Hero : build everything from scratch (modules 04, 07, 08, 09) ⭐ Understanding Deep Learning (Prince, 2023) : the theory textbook (all modules) ⭐ Dive into Deep Learning (d2l.ai) : the textbook with runnable PyTorch code (05–07) ⭐ Stanford CS231n → CS336 : university-grade assignments (06, 09) ⭐ Mathematics for Machine Learning : math, only when you need it (01) Books (free online unless 💲) Book Best for Understanding Deep Learning, Prince modern, clear, illustrated. Best overall DL textbook Dive into Deep Learning, Zhang et al. code + math side by side Mathematics for Machine Learning, Deisenroth et al. linear algebra, calculus, and probability for ML Deep Learning: Foundations and Concepts, Bishop & Bishop rigorous, probabilistic view (free online viewing) Deep Learning, Goodfellow, Bengio, Courville classic (2016). Part I math and Part II are still great Probabilistic Machine Learning, Murphy encyclopedic reference (Book 1 + 2) Neural Networks and Deep Learning, Nielsen gentlest backprop introduction An Introduction to Statistical Learning (ISLP) classical ML with Python Reinforcement Learning, Sutton & Barto RL foundations The Principles of Deep Learning Theory physics-style DL theory (advanced) Geometric Deep Learning, Bronstein et al. symmetry, GNNs, equivariance How to Scale Your Model (DeepMind) LLM systems and parallelism 💲 Build a Large Language Model (From Scratch), Raschka LLMs step by step ( free code ) 💲 Programming Massively Parallel Processors, Hwu et al. CUDA / GPU programming Courses (lectures free on YouTube or the course site) Course Module Notes 3Blue1Brown: linear algebra & neural nets 01, 04, 08 intuition first ML Specialization (Andrew Ng) 03 free audit / financial aid options vary, check the page Stanford CS229: Machine Learning 03 math-heavy fast.ai: Practical Deep Learning 05–06 top-down alternative Stanford CS231n: CNNs for Visual Recognition 06 assignments are gold Stanford CS224n: NLP with Deep Learning 07–08 Stanford CS336: Language Modeling from Scratch 09 hardest and best LLM course Hugging Face Learn (LLM, Diffusion, Deep RL courses) 09–11 practical ecosystem MIT 6.S184: Flow Matching & Diffusion 10 SDE/ODE view OpenAI Spinning Up 11 deep RL bridge UC Berkeley CS285: Deep RL 11 advanced Stanford CS224W: ML with Graphs 12 GNNs ARENA 12 interpretability, hands-on GPU MODE lectures 12 CUDA/Triton MIT Missing Semester 02 shell, git, tooling Blogs & interactive explainers Lilian Weng : deep, well-cited surveys (diffusion, RL, LLMs, agents) Andrej Karpathy's blog : Recipe for Training NNs, RNN effectiveness colah's blog : LSTMs, visual explanations Jay Alammar : the Illustrated Transformer / GPT-2 / BERT Distill.pub : interactive research articles (archived, still excellent) Transformer Circuits Thread : interpretability research Sebastian Raschka's Ahead of AI : LLM research digests Yang Song's blog : score-based generative models Interactive: TensorFlow Playground · CNN Explainer · Transformer Explainer Code to read (well-written references) micrograd · nanoGPT · build-nanogpt · nanochat · llm.c CleanRL : single-file RL algorithms timm : vision models · diffusers · transformers · trl The Annotated Transformer · The Annotated S4 Tools Compute: Google Colab , Kaggle Notebooks (free GPUs) · rent: Lambda, RunPod, Vast.ai Tracking: Weights & Biases (free for personal use), TensorBoard Data/models: Hugging Face Hub Papers: arXiv , Hugging Face Papers , alphaXiv , Semantic Scholar Paper reading order Read each one after the module that covers it, when it'll feel easy. ✅ = reimplement a small version. Foundations 1. Learning representations by back-propagating errors (Rumelhart et al., 1986) : backprop (just skim) 2. ImageNet Classification with Deep CNNs, AlexNet (2012) 3. Adam (2014) · Decoupled Weight Decay, AdamW (2017) 4. Dropout (2014) · Batch Normalization (2015) · Layer Normalization (2016) 5. ✅ Deep Residual Learning, ResNet (2015) Sequences → Transformers 6. word2vec (2013) · Seq2Seq (2014) · Bahdanau Attention (2014) 7. ✅ Attention Is All You Need (2017) 8. BERT (2018) · ✅ GPT-2 (2019) · GPT-3 (2020) 9. ViT (2020) · RoFormer / RoPE (2021) · FlashAttention (2022) LLMs 10. Scaling Laws (Kaplan, 2020) · Chinchilla (2022) 11. InstructGPT / RLHF (2022) · ✅ LoRA (2021) · DPO (2023) 12. Llama (2023) · Chain-of-Thought (2022) · RAG (2020) 13. Mixtral / MoE (2024) · DeepSeek-R1 (2025) (RL for reasoning, GRPO) Generative 14. ✅ VAE (2013) · GAN (2014) 15. ✅ DDPM (2020) · Score-based SDEs (2020) · Classifier-Free Guidance (2022) 16. Latent Diffusion (2021) · DiT (2022) · ✅ Flow Matching (2022) 17. CLIP (2021) RL 18. ✅ DQN, Atari (2013) · AlphaGo Zero (2017) · ✅ PPO (2017) Frontier/theory (pick by track) 19. Double Descent (2019) · NTK (2018) · Grokking (2022) 20. A Mathematical Framework for Transformer Circuits (2021) · Toy Models of Superposition (2022) 21. Mamba (2023) · PINNs (2017) · Fourier Neural Operator (2020)"},{"t":"Progress & Learning Log","u":"deep-learning/progress.html","s":"Deep Learning","h":[{"n":"Status board","id":"status-board"},{"n":"Projects portfolio","id":"projects-portfolio"},{"n":"Fuzzy list","id":"fuzzy-list"},{"n":"Paper log","id":"paper-log"},{"n":"Session log (newest first)","id":"session-log-newest-first"}],"x":"Progress & Learning Log Progress log ⏱ 1 min read View Markdown source Update this every session (2 minutes). It's your memory, so you never relearn what you've already covered. Status board Legend: ⬜ not started · 🟨 in progress · ✅ done · ⏭️ skipped (passed skip test) # Module Status Skip test Build Started Finished 1-wk review 1-mo review 01 Math for DL ⬜ ⬜ ⬜ 02 Python, NumPy & tools ⬜ ⬜ ⬜ 03 ML fundamentals ⬜ ⬜ ⬜ 04 Neural nets from scratch ⬜ ⬜ ⬜ 05 PyTorch & training ⬜ ⬜ ⬜ 06 CNNs & vision ⬜ ⬜ ⬜ 07 Sequences & RNNs ⬜ ⬜ ⬜ 08 Transformers ⬜ ⬜ ⬜ 09 LLMs ⬜ ⬜ ⬜ 10 Generative models ⬜ ⬜ ⬜ 11 Reinforcement learning ⬜ ⬜ ⬜ 12 Frontiers (track: ____ ) ⬜ — ⬜ Projects portfolio Project Module Link to code Result micrograd (rewritten from memory) 04 NumPy MLP on MNIST 04 test acc: ResNet-18 on CIFAR-10 06 test acc: GPT from scratch 08 val loss: LoRA fine-tune 09 eval gain: DDPM / flow matching 10 samples: PPO on LunarLander 11 avg return: Fuzzy list Things that didn't fully click. Review them at the start of the next session and cross them off when they make sense. - [ ] … Paper log Date Paper Problem → Idea → Result My doubt / question Session log (newest first)"},{"t":"Template: a new topic","u":"template/TOPIC_TEMPLATE.html","s":"Extras","h":[{"n":"Roadmap (topic README.md)","id":"roadmap-topic-readmemd"},{"n":"Module (NN-.md)","id":"module-nn-md"},{"n":"Progress log entry","id":"progress-log-entry"}],"x":"Template: a new topic Template ⏱ 1 min read View Markdown source Copy this folder layout to start a topic: <topic-name>/ ├── README.md ← roadmap (use the \"Roadmap\" section below) ├── 01-<module>.md ← one file per module (use the \"Module\" section below) ├── cheatsheet.md ← formulas / commands / facts, one page ├── resources.md ← ⭐ main resource per area + optional extras ├── progress.md ← checklist + learning log └── interactive/ ← index.html + visual tools; link ../../_shared/style.css and reuse ../../_shared/flashcards.js for a flashcard deck Roadmap (topic README.md) # <Topic> **Why I'm learning it:** … **Target level:** … **Time budget:** … h/week → ~… months | Phase | Modules | Time | Outcome | |---|---|---|---| | 1. Foundations | 01, 02 | 3 wk | can … | | 2. Core | 03–05 | 6 wk | can … | | 3. Advanced | 06+ | open | can … | Module (NN- .md) # NN · <Module name> > **Goal:** one sentence. · **Time:** ~X h · **Needs:** modules … ## Skip test If you can do ALL of these without notes, skip the module. - [ ] … ## Core ideas (skim this to recap) - **Idea** — one-line explanation. Key formula. ## Learn from - ⭐ **Main resource** — what to use from it (chapters/lectures) - Optional: … ## Build - Project that proves you understand it. ## Traps - Common misunderstanding → correction. ## Done when - [ ] Build works - [ ] can explain X to someone else Progress log entry ### YYYY-MM-DD · module NN · 1.5 h - Did: … - Clicked: … - Still fuzzy: … ← review this next session"}];